ralph/progress.txt:224:- Bare-id job read endpoints (BE-0274 pattern, GET `/v1/jobs/{job_id}`) should authorize against the persisted job row's owning scope, not an org-root placeholder: the resolver reloads the tenant-scoped job through `store.JobReader.GetJob`, mirrors `ProjectID` / `EnvironmentID` / `ServiceID` into `policy.Scope`, then the handler performs the same tenant-scoped read for the response. This preserves project/environment/service-scoped grant containment while unknown or cross-tenant ids still collapse to the repository's `E_NOT_FOUND` path.
ralph/progress.txt:226:- Environment state-machine stories use a pure `EnvironmentStatus.CanTransitionTo` table plus an atomic repository `Transition` method: lock the tenant-scoped environment row, reject impossible edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, update `environments.status`, and append the matching `environment_events` row in the same transaction with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata. Redact free-text reason text before persisting event message/metadata.
ralph/progress.txt:227:- Service state-machine stories use a pure `ServiceStatus.CanTransitionTo` table plus an atomic repository `Transition` method: lock the tenant-scoped service row, reject impossible edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, update `services.status`, and append the matching `service_events` row in the same transaction with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata. Redact free-text reason text before persisting event message/metadata.
ralph/progress.txt:229:- Provisioning job state-machine stories use a pure `JobStatus.CanTransitionTo` table plus an atomic repository `Transition` method: lock the tenant-scoped job row, reject impossible edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, derive lease/attempt/timestamp bookkeeping from the target status, and append the matching `provisioning_job_events` row in the same transaction with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata. Redact free-text reason/error summaries before persisting event message/metadata.
ralph/progress.txt:230:- Deployment state-machine stories use a pure `DeploymentStatus.CanTransitionTo` table plus an atomic repository `Transition` method: lock the tenant-scoped deployment row, reject impossible edges with `E_INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION` before any write, update lifecycle timestamps/error fields, and append the matching `deployment_events` row in the same transaction with actor/request/previous/next/reason metadata. Redact free-text reason/error summaries before persisting event message/metadata.
ralph/progress.txt:231:- Worker Dokploy import jobs (BE-0502 pattern for `import_dokploy_resource`) are organization-scoped only and use a typed payload `(organization_id, yalla_organization_id, dokploy_organization_id[, assignment_dokploy_org_id])`. The worker reloads the Yalla organization from Postgres, rejects stale desired versions before scanning Dokploy, then delegates classification/apply to `migrateimport.Importer` through a store-backed repository adapter. Each imported project/environment/service row and its `dokploy_refs` mapping are inserted in the same transaction, so replay after partial success classifies existing refs as `skip_already_imported` and creates only missing descendants. Scanner/Dokploy failures stay retryable unless cancellation interrupted the context; malformed/cross-tenant/stale desired state is `worker.Terminal`.
ralph/progress.txt:237:- Worker variable sync jobs (BE-0496 pattern for `sync_variables`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id[, engine])`, load the service through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope and `DesiredVersion`, resolve the service-kind-specific Dokploy service ref, fetch all four variable scopes in one read transaction, merge them with `internal/controlplane/variables.Resolver` and the configured secrets provider, render deterministic dotenv content, and call the typed Dokploy `SyncVariables` intent. The typed client maps application to `/application.saveEnvironment`, compose to `/compose.update`, and database engines to `/<engine>.saveEnvironment`; fake Dokploy request recording must redact the entire rendered `env` field because it contains plaintext effective values.
ralph/progress.txt:238:- Worker domain sync jobs (BE-0495 pattern for `sync_domains`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id)`, load the service through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope and `DesiredVersion`, resolve the service-kind-specific Dokploy service ref, list desired `service_domains`, and call the typed Dokploy `EnsureDomain` intent once per desired row. Persist mappings as `dokploy_refs` rows with `yalla_kind='service_domain'`, `yalla_id=service_domains.id`, and `dokploy_resource='domain'`; replays pass `ExistingID` to `EnsureDomain` and must not insert duplicate domain mappings. Retryable upstream errors stay plain for the durable queue to persist redacted `error_summary`; malformed/stale/cross-tenant desired state is `worker.Terminal`.
ralph/progress.txt:239:- Worker project teardown jobs (BE-0494 pattern for `project.delete` / PRD alias `delete_project`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id)`, load the project through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope and `DesiredVersion` against the persisted row, resolve the `project` `dokploy_refs` row, and call the typed Dokploy `RemoveProject` intent. A job already marked `succeeded` is a replay no-op; a prior upstream deletion is idempotent because the typed Dokploy client treats remove 404 as success. Like environment/service delete jobs, project delete jobs do not reject `deletion_scheduled_at` because scheduled deletion is the expected lifecycle path.
ralph/progress.txt:240:- Worker environment teardown jobs (BE-0493 pattern for `environment.delete` / PRD alias `delete_environment`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id)`, load the environment through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope and `DesiredVersion` against the persisted row, resolve the `environment` `dokploy_refs` row, and call the typed Dokploy `RemoveEnvironment` intent. A job already marked `succeeded` is a replay no-op; a prior upstream deletion is idempotent because the typed Dokploy client treats remove 404 as success. Like service delete jobs, environment delete jobs do not reject `deletion_scheduled_at` because scheduled deletion is the expected lifecycle path.
ralph/progress.txt:241:- Worker teardown jobs (BE-0492 pattern for `service.delete` / PRD alias `delete_service`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed scoped payload `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id)`, load the service through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope and `DesiredVersion` against the persisted row, resolve the service-kind-specific `dokploy_refs` row, and call the typed Dokploy `RemoveService` intent. A job already marked `succeeded` is a replay no-op; a prior upstream deletion is also idempotent because the typed Dokploy client treats remove 404 as success. Unlike runtime-control jobs, delete jobs do not reject `deletion_scheduled_at`; scheduled deletion is the normal lifecycle path.
ralph/progress.txt:242:- Worker runtime-control jobs (BE-0488/BE-0489/BE-0490/BE-0491 pattern for `service.restart` / PRD alias `restart_service`, `service.stop` / PRD alias `stop_service`, `service.start` / PRD alias `start_service`, and `service.rollback` / PRD alias `rollback_service`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed payload parser carrying `(organization_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id)`, load the service through the tenant-scoped repository, verify payload scope against the persisted row, refuse stale `DesiredVersion` and `deletion_scheduled_at` before any Dokploy call, resolve the concrete service Dokploy ID from the service-kind-specific `dokploy_refs` row, and call a typed Dokploy runtime intent such as `RestartService`, `StopService`, `StartService`, or `RollbackService`. Keep accepting PRD underscore aliases (`restart_service`, `stop_service`, `start_service`, `rollback_service`, etc.) for compatibility with manually seeded jobs. Jobs with `Status == succeeded` should be no-op replays and must not issue another Dokploy POST; retryable upstream errors stay plain so the durable queue can persist a redacted `error_summary`.
ralph/progress.txt:244:- Worker child ensure jobs (BE-0482/BE-0483/BE-0484 pattern for `ensure_project`, `ensure_environment`, and `ensure_application_service`) extend `worker.Provisioner` with a typed payload parser, validate the job scope fields against payload IDs, load the source-of-truth row through the tenant-scoped repository, check `DesiredVersion` before Dokploy calls and again before persisting refs, resolve the parent Dokploy ID from tenant-scoped `dokploy_refs`, call the typed Dokploy client through mapper-produced intents, and persist exactly one child `dokploy_refs` row. Replay should read the existing child ref, call the typed client with `ExistingID`, and leave the mapping table unchanged; malformed/stale/cross-tenant/missing desired state are `worker.Terminal`, retryable Dokploy errors stay plain, and queue-level tests should verify persisted `error_summary` stays redacted. Service-kind-specific jobs must also verify the source-of-truth `services.kind` before calling Dokploy and persist the matching Dokploy resource kind (`application`, `compose`, or `database`). `ensure_database_service` additionally validates the concrete engine in the durable job payload because the current `services` row stores only the broad `database` kind.
ralph/progress.txt:246:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **project-scoped preview lifecycle wrapper with Insert + GetByID + ListByProject + Update + ScheduleDeletion + DeleteByID, database-owned version/timestamps, and soft-delete state visible on read paths** (BE-0480 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/preview_environment_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for preview_environments). Seed two complete Organization -> Project -> source Environment + preview-kind wrapped Environment chains, use overlapping-looking DisplayName/ChangeRef fixtures, assert ListByProject renders only the caller's stable page and returns a non-nil empty slice for `(orgA, projB)`, assert Get/Update/ScheduleDeletion/Delete using orgA with orgB's `(project_id, preview_id)` return typed `E_NOT_FOUND`, byte-compare both orgA and orgB bystanders after failed cross-tenant writes, and explicitly schedule deletion on orgB to prove soft-deleted preview rows remain tenant-scoped on both list and direct-id read paths. Add a global-PK collision probe across tenants because `preview_environments.id` is globally unique even though all normal reads/writes are tenant-scoped; the conflict must leave the prior owner byte-identical and create no row for the requester.
ralph/progress.txt:247:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **project-scoped preview lifecycle table whose row points at both a preview-kind environment clone and a source environment, carries soft-delete lifecycle state, and exposes Insert + GetByID + ListByProject + Update + ScheduleDeletion + DeleteByID** (BE-0479 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/preview_environment_repository_invariants_test.go for preview_environments). The table is tenant-scoped by direct `(organization_id, project_id)` predicates and composite FKs to `environments (organization_id, project_id, id)`, uses `penv_` IDs via `domain.KindPreviewEnvironment`, and adds a trigger to enforce that the wrapped environment row has `kind='preview'` because a FK cannot express referenced-row-body predicates. It keeps status/deletion state out of customer metadata Update, and uses ScheduleDeletion (`status='deleting'`, `deletion_scheduled_at=COALESCE(..., now())`) for API teardown while preserving hard DeleteByID for worker cleanup after Dokploy teardown. Forward-applicable to future project-scoped lifecycle rows that wrap a concrete hierarchy resource with worker-owned status: test row shape/default status, deterministic `(created_at,id)` list ordering, optimistic conflicts, soft-delete lifecycle, hard-delete cleanup, constraint conflicts, referenced-row-body trigger conflicts, rollback, nil-Tx guards, and database-owned version/timestamps.
ralph/progress.txt:249:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for `service_backups` / PRD `backup_schedules` (BE-0477 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_backup_repository_invariants_test.go): this is a service-scoped desired-state leaf table with worker-projected run state. The repository surface is Insert + GetByID + ListByService + Update + MarkPending + DeleteByID; every query predicates on `(organization_id, service_id[, id])`. Insert treats `RetentionCount == 0` as omitted and normalizes it to the stable default 7, while negative/out-of-range retention still relies on the DB CHECK and maps to typed `E_CONFLICT`. Update must only write customer policy fields `(display_name, schedule, retention_count, enabled)` and preserve worker state `(status, last_run_at, last_succeeded_at)`; MarkPending is the narrow worker-signal transition and should bump version via trigger. DeleteByID is hard delete with optional If-Match, returning the deleted row snapshot. Forward-applicable to future service-scoped policy rows with worker-owned status columns: test default normalization, deterministic list ordering, optimistic conflicts, timestamp/version triggers, CHECK/FK conflicts, rollback, nil-Tx guards, and preservation of worker-owned columns during customer updates.
ralph/progress.txt:250:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **service-scoped hard-delete leaf table with Insert + GetByID + ListByService + Update + DeleteByID and optimistic version triggers** (BE-0476 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_domain_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for service_domains). Seed two full Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service chains, use overlapping-looking but globally unique (hostname, path) fixtures, assert ListByService returns only the caller tenant's stable page and a non-nil empty slice for a cross-tenant service_id, assert Get/Update/Delete using orgA with orgB's service/domain ids return typed E_NOT_FOUND, and byte-compare both orgA and orgB bystanders after failed cross-tenant mutations so missing organization predicates show up as version/updated_at drift. For service_domains specifically, an empty create path is intentionally normalized by the service layer to "/" and the database allows it; do not treat blank path as a DB CHECK conflict in repository constraint tests.
ralph/progress.txt:251:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **tenant-scoped service leaf table whose public surface is Insert + GetByID + ListByService + Update + DeleteByID, whose row has database-owned version/created_at/updated_at, and whose parent link is the composite `(organization_id, service_id)` FK** (BE-0475 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_domain_repository_invariants_test.go for service_domains). The reusable shape is: seed the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service chain with existing helpers, insert through the typed repository rather than raw SQL, assert version=1 and created_at==updated_at on INSERT, assert Update bumps version and advances updated_at while preserving created_at and identity fields, assert matching If-Match succeeds while stale If-Match on Update/Delete returns typed `E_CONFLICT`, assert DeleteByID returns the final row and removes it, and cover CHECK/FK/UNIQUE violations, transaction rollback, parent cascade, deterministic ListByService ordering, and nil-Tx guards. Forward-applicable to future service-scoped leaf tables with hard-delete lifecycle and optimistic concurrency.
ralph/progress.txt:252:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go + INTERNAL *_internal_test.go + CANONICAL *_canonical_test.go ALL ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY + VALIDATOR-EXHAUSTIVENESS STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE IS APPEND + READ + ONE STATE-TRANSITION (MarkRevoked, *Tx-bound) WITH A REPOSITORY-LEVEL LIMIT CAP, AND WHOSE UPDATE SURFACE IS GUARDED BY THREE STACKED BEFORE-UPDATE TRIGGERS (set_updated_at + bump_version + a per-column rewrite-rejecting trigger), AND WHOSE Append FILLS A ZERO StartedAt SERVER-SIDE IN THE REPOSITORY (not via DB DEFAULT)** (BE-0473 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/break_glass_repository_invariants_test.go for break_glass_sessions). Subspecies of the BE-0469 (idempotency_keys) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the THREE-trigger UPDATE surface is unique in store — the rewrite-rejecting trigger (break_glass_sessions_reject_rewrite) raises SQLSTATE 23001 (restrict_violation) for any UPDATE that mutates a non-revocation column AND for any UPDATE that blanks the reason column. The test file walks the FULL trigger predicate matrix (id, organization_id, actor_id, actor_kind, reason, started_at, expires_at, request_id, correlation_id) via raw-SQL UPDATEs and asserts BOTH the typed error AND byte-identical row re-load AFTER the rejection. A regression that demoted any single column from the trigger predicate would surface as a per-case failure on its first iteration. The existing primary _test.go's TestBreakGlassSchemaRejectsUpdateToImmutableColumns covers ONLY reason rewrite, so the per-column-matrix invariants probe is genuinely new coverage. (b) The Append fills a zero StartedAt server-side via `if s.StartedAt.IsZero() { s.StartedAt = time.Now().UTC() }` BEFORE the INSERT — the column has NO DEFAULT in the migration (vs created_at/updated_at which DO have DEFAULT now()). A regression that removed the IsZero branch would NOT surface as a CHECK violation (expires_at > started_at is satisfied with zero started_at + any future expires_at) — it would silently store year-0001 rows. TestBreakGlassRepositoryAppendStartedAtDefaultsToNow is the regression backstop: it asserts the filled StartedAt falls within a ±2s wallclock bracket. (c) MarkRevoked's UPDATE filters on `revoked_at IS NULL` (column predicate, NOT row-existence predicate) — same shape as DriftFindingRepository.MarkResolved (BE-0471). The zero-rows branch falls through to a tenant-scoped follow-up Get inside the SAME *Tx to distinguish "missing" (typed apierr.NotFound, caller-supplied id) from "already revoked" (typed apierr.Conflict). This means TestBreakGlassRepositoryMarkRevokedCrossTenantIsNotFound is NOT redundant with TestBreakGlassRepositoryGetCrossTenantIsNotFound: a regression that dropped tenant scoping from BOTH the UPDATE and the follow-up Get would silently succeed in revoking another tenant's session. The cross-tenant Get + cross-tenant MarkRevoked pair pins both halves of the contract; either alone is insufficient. (d) The CHECK / FK violation matrix for break_glass_sessions has FOUR distinct probes that surface as typed apierr.Conflict via mapWriteError: unknown organization (FK), blank actor_kind + unknown actor_kind (both fail the CHECK actor_kind IN ('usr','sa')), blank reason (CHECK length(reason) > 0), and expires_at <= started_at (CHECK expires_at > started_at — exercised with both equal-to-started_at AND predates-started_at sub-cases). The repository does NOT pre-validate these fields (the service buildSessionToCreate is the chokepoint for validation), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces via mapWriteError. (e) The `twoOrgs(t, db, f, label)` helper is genuinely new at package scope (verified via grep on `^func twoOrgs` across all store_test files — no prior declaration exists) — it names the two-organization fixture pattern (a TARGET tenant + a SUPPORT actor's home tenant) that every break_glass test needs. The 28-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + bgs_ prefix + server-side timestamps within ±2s + version=1 + RevokedAt nil), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-StartedAt-defaults-to-now (the load-bearing server-side-fill probe), four CHECK/FK-violation Conflicts (unknown-org, blank-actor_kind, unknown-actor_kind, blank-reason), expires_at-not-after-started_at-Conflict (equal + predate sub-cases), Append-nil-Tx-Internal, Append-transaction-rollback, Append-peer-row-byte-identical, Get-reads-back, Get-unknown-NotFound, Get-cross-tenant-NotFound, Get-expired-returns-row-as-is (Active(t) observation, NOT Get filtering), List-newest-first-ordering (started_at DESC, id DESC), List-limit-clamping matrix (non-positive / negative / above-cap / in-range), List-unknown-org-empty-slice, MarkRevoked-happy-path (RETURNING byte-equal raw-SQL + version+1 via bump_version + updated_at advance via set_updated_at), MarkRevoked-already-revoked-Conflict (does not move revoked_at/revoked_by_*), MarkRevoked-unknown-NotFound, MarkRevoked-cross-tenant-NotFound (does not touch row), MarkRevoked-nil-Tx-Internal, MarkRevoked-peer-row-byte-identical, append-mostly-trigger-rejects-column-rewrites (per-column matrix), append-mostly-trigger-rejects-blank-reason (trigger's leading guard), organization-DELETE-cascade. New helpers introduced (per-file BreakGlass-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawBreakGlassRow, loadBreakGlassRowByID, countBreakGlassRowsForOrg, mintBreakGlassSessionID, breakGlassSessionFixture, seedBreakGlassSession, assertBreakGlassRowByteIdentical, breakGlassTxRollbackSentinel, sanitizeTestNameForBreakGlassID, AND the package-new twoOrgs helper for the two-org fixture shape. REUSED verbatim from sibling files: assertTimePtrEqual (drift_finding_repository_invariants_test.go), seedOrg (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go), wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go). NO migration added (0019_break_glass_sessions landed in an early migration). Forward-applicable to ANY future repository CRUD-and-invariants story whose table carries a three-trigger UPDATE surface (set_updated_at + bump_version + rewrite-rejecting) AND whose Append fills a zero timestamp server-side in the repository rather than via DB DEFAULT — the per-column trigger-predicate matrix and the ±2s wallclock-bracket pattern are the chokepoints that catch the two most common regression shapes (a demoted trigger column or a removed IsZero default-fill branch).
ralph/progress.txt:253:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + *_repository_invariants_test.go ALREADY EXIST IN THE IMMEDIATELY PRIOR PRD STORY, WHOSE PUBLIC SURFACE IS THE FULL Append + GetByID + ListByOrganization + MarkResolved (state-transition) MATRIX, WHOSE OPTIONAL RESOURCE LEGS ARE NULLABLE COMPOSITE FKs MATCH SIMPLE, AND WHOSE ROW BODY HAS A TENANT-SHARED TEXT COLUMN (e.g. dokploy_resource_id with NO UNIQUE constraint cross-tenant)** (BE-0472 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/drift_finding_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go). Subspecies of the BE-0468 (audit_events) full-surface template with three load-bearing deltas: (a) the public mutation surface is TWO writes (Append + MarkResolved) rather than one (Append), so the tenant-isolation file must add a deepened cross-tenant MarkResolved probe with a MULTI-ROW orgB pool (target + (kind, reason, level)-sharing sibling + already-resolved row) — the existing invariants-file cross-tenant MarkResolved probe targets only one row inline and does not exercise the bystander byte-identity loop. (b) The tenant-shared text shape probe uses dokploy_resource_id (NOT NULL DEFAULT '' with NO UNIQUE cross-tenant) — orgA and orgB seed rows with the SAME dokploy_resource_id value across both Append-bystander-byte-identity AND ListByOrganization-cross-tenant probes. A regression that confused tenants by dokploy_resource_id rather than organization_id would surface as a foreign-tenant row in the list result or a bystander row drift. (c) The optional resource legs (project_id / environment_id / service_id / service_domain_id) are NULLABLE composite FKs MATCH SIMPLE, so the two-tenant fixture seeds per-tenant parent chains (orgA+projA+envA+svcA, orgB+projB+envB+svcB) — a single shared chain would not satisfy the composite FK on the cross-tenant probes, and a missing chain would force every test into the organization-level (every-leg-NULL) shape. The fixture helper seedTwoTenantDriftFindingFixture lands the full per-tenant chain by default; tests opt into organization-level findings inline by leaving the legs unset. The 9-test shape this file lands: Append-on-orgA-does-not-touch-orgB-rows (multi-row orgB bystander pool covering kind/reason/level/lifecycle spread + tenant-shared dokploy_resource_id), Append-on-orgA-does-not-change-orgB-row-count, Append-cross-tenant-id-collision (global PK collision + foreign-column-leak audit on the Conflict payload), ListByOrganization-cross-tenant-returns-only-own-rows (the load-bearing list probe with tenant-shared dokploy_resource_id AND identical (kind, reason, level) tuples), ListByOrganization-limit-clamping-is-tenant-scoped (raw-SQL seed 205 orgA rows above the 200-cap + orgB-only above-cap list returns exactly orgB's count), ListByOrganization-unknown-tenant-empty-and-side-effect-free, GetByID-cross-tenant-bystander-byte-identical (deepens existing invariants-file probe with bystander row + per-tenant count + error-payload foreign-column-leak audit), MarkResolved-cross-tenant-deep-bystander-byte-identical (deepens existing invariants-file probe with multi-row orgB pool including (kind, reason, level)-sharing sibling), and organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped (deepens existing invariants-file probe with three-row orgB pool covering pending + resolved + shared-dokploy_resource_id + post-cascade ListByOrganization probe). New helpers introduced: twoTenantDriftFindingFixture (full per-tenant parent chain), seedTwoTenantDriftFindingFixture, totalDriftFindingRows (global counterpart to countDriftFindingRowsForOrg for the cross-tenant id-collision global-count probe). Reused verbatim from sibling files: rawDriftFindingRow, loadDriftFindingRowByID, countDriftFindingRowsForOrg, mintDriftFindingID, driftFindingFixture, seedDriftFinding, assertDriftFindingByteIdentical (drift_finding_repository_invariants_test.go); seedOrg, seedProject, seedEnvironment, seedService (schema_test.go); newStore (store_test.go); wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go); containsValue (organization_variable_tenant_isolation_test.go); itoa (audit_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go). The itoa helper from audit_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go is reused for the raw-SQL seed loop in TestDriftFindingRepositoryListByOrganizationLimitClampingIsTenantScoped to avoid importing strconv for one call site; a duplicate top-level itoa in this file would fail compilation because all *_test.go files share package store_test. Forward-applicable to ANY future repository tenant-isolation story whose surface is Append + GetByID + ListByOrganization + a single state-transition (MarkResolved-like) mutation AND whose row body carries a tenant-shared text column without a UNIQUE constraint cross-tenant — the dokploy_resource_id-shared-text-shape probe shape is the chokepoint that catches a regression switching tenant-discrimination from organization_id to any other column.
ralph/progress.txt:254:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go + INTERNAL *_internal_test.go + READER ADAPTER + READER ADAPTER TEST ALL ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE IS APPEND-ONLY WITH ONLY ONE WRITE SURFACE (Append) AND ONE READ SURFACE (ListByOrganization, no GetByID) AND A REPOSITORY-LEVEL LIMIT CAP, AND WHOSE INSERT COLUMN LIST DELIBERATELY OMITS occurred_at + created_at (so DEFAULT now() applies and caller-supplied timestamps are SILENTLY DISCARDED)** (BE-0467 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/audit_repository_invariants_test.go for audit_events). Subspecies of the BE-0461 (provisioning_jobs) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the typed repository is the NARROWEST in the store package — Append + ListByOrganization only, NO GetByID, NO Update, NO Delete (row removal reachable only through organizations ON DELETE CASCADE on tenant teardown). The acceptance-criteria phrase "create typed repository methods for creating, reading, updating, and deleting ... where the lifecycle requires it" narrows to "Append + List only; the audit log lifecycle structurally rejects per-row Update / Delete" and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap; (b) the audit_events INSERT column list (auditEventColumns minus occurred_at/created_at) deliberately omits both timestamp columns, so DEFAULT now() applies regardless of caller. A test `TestAuditRepositoryAppendIgnoresCallerSuppliedTimestamps` is the regression backstop — a future refactor that added occurred_at to the INSERT (so a caller could back-date an audit record) would silently break the "audit timestamps are server-side" guarantee unless this test was also updated. The probe seeds OccurredAt=year-2000 and asserts the persisted row's OccurredAt is within [now-1s, now+1s], NOT the back-dated value; (c) CHECK constraints that the application does NOT pre-validate (blank action / blank resource_kind / blank reason / actor_kind outside the closed-set ('', 'usr', 'sa', 'system')) MUST be exercised: Append does NOT pre-check these fields (the audit service is the chokepoint for the principal-mapping inputs), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces as typed `apierr.Conflict` via mapWriteError. Each test asserts BOTH the error code AND `countAuditRowsForOrg(org.ID) == 0` to pin "the rejection left no row behind"; (d) the repository-level limit cap (`auditEventListMaxLimit = 200`) is a public invariant of the ListByOrganization contract and MUST be exercised in three branches — non-positive (limit <= 0 -> 200), above-cap (limit > 200 -> 200), in-range (limit in (0, 200] honoured verbatim). A regression that returned the empty slice when limit <= 0 (or every row when limit was unbounded) would surface here. The seeding helper `seedAuditRowsRawSQL` bypasses the repository and uses direct INSERT with caller-controlled occurred_at so 201 rows can be planted in O(n) without sleeping; (e) the secondary-sort ORDER BY id DESC tiebreaker on tied occurred_at is the load-bearing list-ordering invariant: a UI rendering a pair of audit events written in the same Postgres microsecond would flicker without it. The probe seeds TWO rows with the SAME occurred_at (via raw INSERT — Append's DEFAULT now() would advance between calls) and asserts listed[0].ID > listed[1].ID lexicographically. The 16-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + aud_ prefix + server-side timestamps within [before, after]), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-ignores-caller-supplied-timestamps (the load-bearing server-side-timestamp probe), four CHECK-violation Conflicts (blank-action, blank-resource_kind, blank-reason, unknown-actor_kind), duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-transaction-rollback (sentinel error + countAuditRowsForOrg == 0), Append-cascades-from-organization-delete (orgA wipe + orgB bystander byte-identity across id/organization_id/action/reason/occurred_at/created_at), Append-peer-row-byte-identical (the BE-0468 byte-identity bystander predecessor — peer Append must NOT touch a prior row), List-orders-id-DESC-on-timestamp-tie, three List-limit-clamping branches (non-positive, above-cap, in-range), List-unknown-org-returns-empty. New helpers (per-file Audit-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawAuditRow, loadAuditRowByID, countAuditRowsForOrg (the per-org counter — organization is the only parent FK for audit_events, so this is also the natural per-parent counter), mintAuditID, auditTxRollbackSentinel, seedAuditRowsRawSQL (the raw-INSERT batch seeder for cap-boundary tests). Reused verbatim from sibling audit_test.go: auditEventFixture, appendAudit, assertAuditEventByteIdentical; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table is append-only with a single (Append, List) surface and a repository-level limit cap (a future security_events / system_audit table would inherit the same template, including the server-side-timestamp invariant if the INSERT column list omits the timestamp legs).
ralph/progress.txt:255:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE HAS A CLAIM PROTOCOL (Claim + Complete + Release + Find — three writes, one read) DRIVEN BY AN UPSERT WITH AN ON-CONFLICT TARGET ON A NON-PK UNIQUE TUPLE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key), AND WHOSE ROW BODY CARRIES A MULTI-COLUMN COMPLETION-CONSISTENT CHECK** (BE-0469 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/idempotency_repository_invariants_test.go for idempotency_keys). Subspecies of the BE-0467 (audit_events) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the Claim INSERT uses `ON CONFLICT (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) DO UPDATE` rather than the canonical `ON CONFLICT (id)` -- the PK is the secondary disambiguator, not the conflict target. So a duplicate id on a DIFFERENT (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) tuple is NOT caught by ON CONFLICT; the PK violation flows through the database and surfaces through mapWriteError as apierr.Conflict. The TestIdempotencyRepositoryClaimDuplicateIDIsConflict probe MUST force the collision through caller-supplied ids on different (principal, key) tuples; a regression that switched the ON CONFLICT target to (id) would swallow the collision and silently rewrite a peer row. (b) The `idempotency_keys_completion_consistent` CHECK requires (response_status, response_body, completed_at) to be either ALL NULL (pending) or ALL NOT NULL (completed). The Complete method coerces a nil `body []byte` argument to `[]byte{}` (zero-length but non-nil) BEFORE the UPDATE so the CHECK's `response_body IS NOT NULL` clause holds for empty envelopes. TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteCoercesNilBodyToEmpty pins both the application-layer coercion AND the database-side non-NULL empty-bytea storage. The pending-side of the CHECK is pinned by TestIdempotencyRepositoryClaimMintsRowShape (a fresh Claim must leave all three completion columns NULL); the completed-side by TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteRowShape (after Complete, all three must be non-NULL AND a raw-SQL re-load must byte-equal the returned struct, AND created_at + expires_at must NOT have drifted across the Complete UPDATE -- only the four completion columns transition). (c) The Complete method's `now time.Time` argument is the only application-controlled timestamp on the table: created_at + expires_at are caller-supplied via Claim and completed_at can be caller-supplied via Complete's now arg (zero defaults to time.Now().UTC()). TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteHonoursExplicitNow uses a specific 2024 timestamp far from time.Now() so a regression that ignored the arg and wrote time.Now() would fail with an obvious delta. This is a structurally distinct invariant from BE-0467 (audit_events) where ALL timestamps are server-side via DEFAULT now() and a caller-supplied OccurredAt must be discarded. (d) The id minter newIdempotencyID emits `idk_<32hex>` (16 bytes of crypto/rand entropy hex-encoded — 4 + 32 = 36 chars total). The row-shape probe MUST pin BOTH the prefix AND the total length so a regression that shortened the entropy or reformatted the prefix surfaces here. Test-local mintIdempotencyID(t, suffix) follows the established per-file suffixed-helper pattern (`idk_`+t.Name()+`_`+suffix) so parallel tests that need a deterministic caller-supplied id (to force a PK collision) cannot collide. (e) The Release method has NO pre-validation coverage in the primary idempotency_test.go's TestIdempotencyClaimValidation (which only covers Claim nil-tx + Claim missing-fields + Complete nil-tx + Complete out-of-range-status). The invariants file fills the Release gap with TestIdempotencyRepositoryReleaseRejectsNilTx so all three mutating methods (Claim / Complete / Release) have a pinned nil-tx guard. The 15-test shape this file lands: Claim-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + idk_ prefix + 36-char length + server-side created_at + ExpiresAt round-trip + pending-side completion-consistent), Claim-respects-caller-supplied-id, Claim-duplicate-id-Conflict (PK collision on different (org, principal, key)), Claim-unknown-organization-Conflict (FK violation), Complete-row-shape (completed-side completion-consistent + created_at/expires_at non-drift), Complete-honours-explicit-now (specific 2024 timestamp), Complete-coerces-nil-body-to-empty (non-NULL empty bytea), Release-rejects-nil-tx, Release-on-completed-is-noop (row stays in place), Release-on-missing-key-is-noop, Claim-transaction-rollback (sentinel + countIdempotencyRowsForOrg == 0), Cascade-from-organization-delete (orgA wipe + orgB bystander byte-identity across id/route/request_hash/request_id/status/created_at/expires_at), Claim-peer-row-byte-identical, Find-returns-expired-row-as-is (Find does NOT filter on expires_at > now()), ExpiresAt-round-trip (microsecond-precise far-future timestamp). Note: idempotency_keys has NO updated_at column and NO BEFORE UPDATE trigger -- the peer-row byte-identity probe relies on the per-column projection across all 13 observable columns without a trigger-managed drift anchor (vs BE-0466 dokploy_refs which anchors on the set_updated_at trigger). Note: the response_status BETWEEN 100 AND 599 CHECK is unreachable from the typed Complete API (it pre-rejects out-of-range as apierr.Internal); same for the status IN ('pending', 'completed') CHECK (Claim hard-codes 'pending', Complete hard-codes 'completed'); same for the length() > 0 CHECKs on principal_id/idempotency_key/route/request_hash (Claim pre-validates these as apierr.InvalidInput). These CHECKs are pinned at the DB level only and NOT probed from Go in the invariants file -- the file header MUST document this delegation so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. New helpers introduced (per-file Idempotency-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawIdempotencyRow (with NULLABLE *int / *time.Time fields so pending NULLs are distinguishable from zero-value scalars), loadIdempotencyRowByID, countIdempotencyRowsForOrg, mintIdempotencyID, idempotencyTxRollbackSentinel. REUSED verbatim from sibling idempotency_test.go: idemFixture, claimIdem, completeIdem, findIdem, wantErrCode, newIdempotencyStore; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore. NO migration added. The byte-identical-bystander tenant-isolation gap-fill is the paired BE-0470 story. Forward-applicable to any future PRD CRUD-and-invariants story whose underlying table has a claim-protocol-style upsert on a non-PK UNIQUE tuple with a multi-column completion-consistent CHECK (e.g. a future webhook-delivery-claims or rate-limit-burst-tokens table).
ralph/progress.txt:256:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TABLE WHOSE SCHEMA LANDED IN AN EARLY MIGRATION, WHOSE TYPED REPOSITORY + INVARIANTS TESTS LANDED IN THE IMMEDIATELY PRIOR PRD STORY, AND WHOSE ROW BODY HAS NO CUSTOMER-MUTABLE COLUMN (Insert + Get + List + Delete only, NO Update / NO state-transition surface) AND CARRIES TWO UNIQUE CONSTRAINTS — ONE GLOBAL across tenants AND ONE PER-TENANT** (BE-0466 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref_tenant_isolation_test.go for dokploy_refs). Subspecies of the BE-0464 pattern (append-only no-Update tenant-isolation) with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the table HAS an updated_at column maintained by a set_updated_at BEFORE UPDATE trigger (vs BE-0464's no-updated_at + BEFORE-UPDATE-rejection trigger), so assertXxxByteIdentical anchors on BOTH created_at AND updated_at as bystander drift probes; a foreign-tenant UPDATE that snuck past the organization_id predicate would fire the trigger and surface as an updated_at drift, even if every other column happened to match. (b) The GLOBAL UNIQUE leg (e.g. dokploy_refs UNIQUE (dokploy_resource, dokploy_id)) demands a dokploy_refs-specific cross-tenant probe not present in any prior tenant-isolation file: orgA Inserting at a (resource, dokploy_id) ALREADY OWNED by orgB must surface as typed apierr.Conflict AND leave orgB's row byte-identical — this is the cross-tenant mirror of the same-tenant duplicate-target Conflict pinned in the BE-0465 invariants file, with the added byte-identity invariant. A regression that widened the GLOBAL UNIQUE to (organization_id, dokploy_resource, dokploy_id) would let orgA's row through (orgA.count would go to 1); a regression that silently UPDATEd orgB's row before the UNIQUE fired would surface as yalla_kind/yalla_id drift on the bystander. (c) GetByXxxTarget (e.g. dokploy_refs.GetByDokployTarget(orgID, resource, dokploy_id)) is a SECOND tenant-scoped read surface beyond the per-id Get — and because the (resource, dokploy_id) tuple is GLOBAL-UNIQUE, the row DOES exist for orgB's foreign-target call, so the tenant predicate is the ONLY thing hiding it. The cross-tenant test is structurally load-bearing: a regression that dropped the organization_id predicate from GetByXxxTarget would surface a successful read of orgA's row from orgB's caller (worst-case cross-tenant data leak). This file must also include a SANITY clause that calls GetByXxxTarget(orgA, resource, alphaDokployID) and expects success; without it the entire test could silently pass if the repository broke for both tenants. (d) Delete is *Tx-bound and returns NotFound via the tag.RowsAffected() == 0 path (not via pgx.ErrNoRows). The cross-tenant probe must run inside an s.Write closure and assert BOTH typed NotFound AND orgA's row byte-identity after — a regression that dropped the organization_id leg from the DELETE predicate would surface as either tag.RowsAffected() == 1 (orgB's call returns nil and orgA's row vanishes — the worst-case data-loss bug) OR as an orgA bystander column drift. The closest sibling (BE-0464 job_attempts) has no Delete surface, so this is a BE-0466-unique probe. (e) The positive-direction "same X in two tenants both persist" test must NOT use the dokploy_id column (the GLOBAL UNIQUE rejects same dokploy_id across tenants) — it instead probes the (yalla_kind, dokploy_resource) pair under DIFFERENT yalla_id/dokploy_id per tenant. The probe pins that there is NO phantom UNIQUE on (yalla_kind, dokploy_resource) alone — without it, a regression resolving such a phantom UNIQUE would prevent orgA's Insert and every byte-identical bystander assertion would silently pass against an unmutated row that was never written. The 10-test shape this file lands: Insert-bystander-byte-identity / Insert-row-count-invariance / Insert-cross-tenant-GLOBAL-UNIQUE-Conflict-AND-orgB-byte-identical / same-(yalla_kind,dokploy_resource)-in-two-tenants-positive-direction with per-tenant ListByYallaResource read-side proof / Get-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak (substring checks on yalla_id / dokploy_id / dokploy_resource — the 3 caller-controllable identifiers that a regression could echo) / GetByDokployTarget-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak + side-effect-free read counts + orgA-sanity-success / ListByYallaResource-cross-tenant-empty + per-tenant COUNT(*) invariance for BOTH tenants / ListByOrganization-cross-tenant-returns-only-own-rows + orgA sanity / Delete-cross-tenant-NotFound + orgA bystander byte-identity + orgA count invariance / organizations DELETE cascade is tenant-scoped across multiple bystander kinds. NEW helpers all carry the per-file DokployRef suffix: assertDokployRefByteIdentical, twoTenantDokployRefFixture + seedTwoTenantDokployRefFixture (4-deep hierarchy seeder: org/proj/env/svc per tenant — needed because dokploy_refs maps to services as the most common yalla_kind), firstDokployRefDokployID; REUSED verbatim from invariants file: mintDokployID, runInsertDokployRefOrFail, rawDokployRefRow, loadDokployRefRowByID, countDokployRefRowsForOrg; from sibling tenant-isolation: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService/containsValue. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future tenant-isolation story whose table has a GLOBAL UNIQUE on (kind, external_id) — a future external-reference/billing-target table would inherit the same template, including the structurally-load-bearing GetByXxxTarget cross-tenant probe and the GLOBAL-UNIQUE cross-tenant-Conflict + bystander byte-identity pair.
ralph/progress.txt:257:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TABLE WHOSE TYPED REPOSITORY IS THE NARROWEST IN THE STORE PACKAGE — APPEND (write) + ListByOrganization (read) ONLY, with NO GetByID, NO Update, NO Delete (row removal reachable only through organizations ON DELETE CASCADE on tenant teardown), AND WHOSE PRIMARY KEY IS GLOBAL (no UNIQUE on (organization_id, id) because the id minter emits 128-bit-entropy opaque tokens that are globally unique by construction)** (BE-0468 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/audit_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for audit_events). Subspecies of the BE-0466 (dokploy_refs) tenant-isolation pattern with five load-bearing deltas that collapse the surface back to the narrowest viable cross-tenant shape: (a) the GetByID / GetByXxxTarget / Delete cross-tenant probes from BE-0466 are STRUCTURALLY ABSENT (no such methods exist on the repository), so the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. The BE-0466 9-probe shape collapses to a 7-probe shape: Append-bystander-byte-identity / Append-bystander-row-count-invariance / Append-cross-tenant-id-collision-Conflict-with-orgA-byte-identity-and-no-foreign-column-echo / ListByOrganization-cross-tenant-returns-only-own-rows-byte-identical + orgA-COUNT-invariance / ListByOrganization-above-cap-limit-clamping-is-tenant-scoped / ListByOrganization-unknown-tenant-empty-and-preserves-real-tenants-COUNT / organizations DELETE cascade is tenant-scoped (3-row orgB pool spanning the decision/metadata spread, plus post-cascade ListByOrganization probe). (b) The cross-tenant id-collision Conflict probe is the load-bearing audit-events-specific contribution: orgA writes a row carrying distinctive business columns (alpha_distinctive_* strings, a unique IP, a unique user-agent, a unique metadata key/value pair) and orgB attempts an Append with the SAME id under its own tenant; the PRIMARY KEY (id) global UNIQUE makes this a Conflict regardless of organization_id, AND the Conflict payload must echo ONLY the supplied id (which orgB has) and never any foreign column from orgA's row (action, resource_kind, resource_id, reason, request_id, correlation_id, ip_address, user_agent, or metadata values). A regression that widened the conflict resolution by organization_id (e.g. an ON CONFLICT (organization_id, id) DO UPDATE clause) would silently allow orgB to mint a row carrying orgA's id under its own tenant — the worst-case audit-log correlation-corruption shape. The not-foreign-substring assertions (containsValue against each distinctive value) are the second-line defence behind the apierr.Conflict code probe. (c) The above-cap-limit-clamping-is-tenant-scoped probe is the load-bearing read-side contribution: a regression that dropped the organization_id predicate from ListByOrganization but kept the LIMIT clause would surface here as a returned-row count well above orgB's seeded count (min(alphaSeed + bravoSeed, auditEventListMaxLimit) instead of just bravoSeed). The test seeds orgA's pool ABOVE the cap (auditEventListMaxLimit + 5 = 205 rows via raw-SQL INSERT — a 205-row Append-loop through the store would multiply the transaction setup cost too much for a single probe) and orgB's pool at a small fixed count (4); orgB's ListByOrganization with an above-cap limit request (1000) must resolve to exactly 4 rows, NOT the 200 cap. (d) assertAuditRowByteIdentical is structurally distinct from the application-level assertAuditEventByteIdentical in audit_test.go: the AuditEvent struct comparator would silently round-trip a metadata jsonb byte drift (the marshalled bytes differ but the unmarshalled map equals) or a sub-second occurred_at rewrite (the application struct's time.Time has Go-native nanosecond precision but the database column's microsecond precision can mask a rewrite within the same microsecond). The raw-row helper compares the persisted bytes exactly as the database stores them — the second-line defence the BE-0468 acceptance criteria require. (e) The unknown-tenant-empty-and-preserves-real-tenants-COUNT probe is the cross-tenant variant of the BE-0467 List-unknown-org-returns-empty: the invariants file seeds a single tenant and asserts the empty slice; the BE-0468 file seeds TWO real tenants and asserts BOTH (i) the empty slice for the phantom org-id AND (ii) BOTH real tenants' per-org COUNT(*) is unchanged after the read. A regression that silently auto-vacuumed orphan rows or counted rows owned by ANY tenant into a global cache would surface here. New helpers introduced (per-file Audit-suffix already established by BE-0467 to avoid store_test collisions): assertAuditRowByteIdentical (raw-row byte-level snapshot comparator), twoTenantAuditFixture + seedTwoTenantAuditFixture (two-organization fixture, NARROWER than the BE-0466 dokploy_refs equivalent because audit_events has no project/environment/service parent chain), itoa (tiny local int-to-string converter for the raw-INSERT seed loop in the above-cap-limit-clamping test — keeps the import graph free of strconv at this site). REUSED verbatim from sibling audit_test.go: auditEventFixture, appendAudit; from audit_repository_invariants_test.go: rawAuditRow, loadAuditRowByID, countAuditRowsForOrg, mintAuditID; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore; from organization_variable_tenant_isolation_test.go: containsValue. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future tenant-isolation story whose table has the (Append, ListByOrganization) narrow surface and a global PRIMARY KEY: a future security_events / system_audit / webhook_events table would inherit the same template, including the cross-tenant id-collision Conflict + foreign-column-no-echo invariant and the above-cap-limit-clamping-is-tenant-scoped invariant.
ralph/progress.txt:258:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE SCHEMA ALREADY EXISTS FROM AN EARLY MIGRATION BUT WHOSE TYPED REPOSITORY IS THE FIRST STORY-LEVEL CALLER** -- i.e. the table landed in an ancient migration (dokploy_refs in 0002_tenant_hierarchy) and has been exercised only by raw-SQL schema_test.go tests; the CRUD-and-invariants PRD story creates BOTH the *_repository.go file AND the *_repository_invariants_test.go file from scratch (BE-0465 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref.go + internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref_repository_invariants_test.go). Five load-bearing differences from a first-story-for-table CRUD vertical (BE-0457 deployments, BE-0463 job_attempts, etc.): (a) NO migration is added because the table already exists -- grep the schema_test.go assertions BEFORE writing to learn the column shape and the CHECK / UNIQUE / FK invariants the raw-SQL tests already pin; (b) the typed repository MUST NOT add columns the table lacks even if a comment in another migration suggests otherwise -- migration 0011_optimistic_versions's preamble claims to cover dokploy_refs 'for parity' but its actual ALTER statements only touch organizations / projects / environments / services, so the DokployRef struct MUST NOT carry a Version field and the repository MUST NOT include version in dokployRefColumns or scan it (the single fastest way to discover this kind of drift is the error-chain unwrap debug pattern: write a one-shot test that prints %T:%s for each `cur.(unwrapper).Unwrap()` layer, which surfaces the underlying *pgconn.PgError with the SQLSTATE 42703 'column X does not exist' message in one round trip); (c) if the table has NO customer-mutable column the typed repository surface is INSERT + GET + LIST + DELETE only -- no Update method, no optimistic-concurrency If-Match path -- and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap (the 'optimistic versioning where applicable' acceptance-criteria phrase narrows to 'not applicable here'); (d) when the table has a bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY (the FIRST and ONLY int64 PK in the store package), the typed struct's ID field is int64 (not string), the INSERT statement OMITS id from the column list entirely (any value supplied -- even NULL or DEFAULT -- trips SQLSTATE 428C9 'cannot insert a non-DEFAULT value into column "id"'), and the typed error payload renders the int64 id via a private package-local helper rather than importing strconv -- keeping the store package's 'no strconv' invariant intact. A test InsertIgnoresCallerSuppliedID is the regression backstop (a caller that smuggles ID: 999_999_999 must still see the database-minted id in the returned struct); (e) when the table has TWO UNIQUE constraints, one GLOBAL across tenants (e.g. (dokploy_resource, dokploy_id) on dokploy_refs -- a Dokploy object cannot be claimed twice across tenants) and one PER-TENANT (e.g. (organization_id, yalla_id, dokploy_resource, dokploy_id)), BOTH must be exercised explicitly by the invariants tests -- the global-UNIQUE test must seed orgA's row then try to insert orgB's row with the SAME (dokploy_resource, dokploy_id) and assert countRowsForOrg(orgB) == 0 after the rejection. The 19-test shape this file lands: Insert-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal), Insert-ignores-caller-supplied-id, four typed-Internal rejections (nil-tx + blank-closed-set-args), four CHECK/FK-violation Conflicts (unknown-yalla_kind, unknown-dokploy_resource, blank-yalla_id, blank-dokploy_id, unknown-organization_id), both UNIQUE violations (global + per-tenant), positive-direction 'multiple domains for same service all persist' (pins the per-tenant UNIQUE includes dokploy_id), Insert-rollback, Get-happy + Get-unknown, GetByDokployTarget-happy + unknown + blank-resource-Internal, ListByYallaResource-ordered + empty + blank-kind-Internal, ListByOrganization-ordered + empty, Delete-happy + unknown + nil-tx, organization-delete-cascade, and a raw-SQL set_updated_at trigger pin (no bump_version trigger exists despite migration 0011's preamble). New helpers introduced (per-file DokployRef-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawDokployRefRow, loadDokployRefRowByID, countDokployRefRowsForOrg, mintDokployID, runInsertDokployRefOrFail, dokployRefTxRollbackSentinel. Reused verbatim: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). The byte-identical-bystander tenant-isolation gap-fill is the paired BE-0466 story. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table landed in an early migration and has been carried by raw-SQL schema tests since (a future idempotency_keys repository would inherit the same template, though without the bigint identity PK twist).
ralph/progress.txt:259:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for an APPEND-ONLY TABLE whose *_repository.go + invariants tests already exist (BE-0464 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/job_attempt_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for job_attempts). Subspecies of the BE-0462 pattern with three load-bearing deltas: (a) NO updated_at column AND a BEFORE UPDATE trigger that rejects every update, so the trigger-managed-anchor invariant used in BE-0458/BE-0462's byte-identity bystander helper does NOT apply -- assertXxxByteIdentical compares created_at as the sole timestamp anchor and every business column individually (the bystander proof now probes the trigger AND the predicate, not the trigger-managed updated_at refresh). (b) Repository surface is narrower: ONE write surface (Append, *Tx-bound), TWO read surfaces (GetByID per-id, ListByParent per-parent-and-org), NO Update/Delete/Transition. So the test set drops from BE-0462's 8 to 7: no cross-tenant-Transition test exists -- the "foreign-tenant write through a state-machine surface silently UPDATEd a peer row" probe has no analogue. (c) The invariants file already exports a per-(org, parent) counter (countXxxRowsForParent) but NOT a per-org counter -- the tenant-isolation file MUST add countXxxRowsForOrg because the org-cascade test needs to probe "did orgB's TOTAL count change across all parents?" across multiple parent rows, not just per-parent. Forward-applicable to any future append-only-attempt/event table whose schema follows the (composite-FK-to-parent + BEFORE-UPDATE-rejection-trigger + no-updated_at + per-tenant-UNIQUE) shape (e.g. future audit-log tables, future immutable timeline tables). Tests pinned: Append(orgA) bystander byte-identity across orgB peers spanning every status the closed-set carries / per-tenant row-count invariance / same-(parent_id, sequence_number)-in-two-tenants positive-direction pair with per-tenant ListByParent read-side proof / GetByID cross-tenant NotFound + no-leak substring check on every non-id business column carrying caller-controllable content (worker_id, error_summary, error_code, request_id, correlation_id, parent_id) / ListByParent cross-tenant empty + per-tenant COUNT(*) invariance for BOTH tenants / parent-row delete cascade tenant-scoped / parent-organization delete cascade tenant-scoped. NEW helpers all carry the per-file Xxx suffix (BE-0464: assertJobAttemptByteIdentical, twoTenantJobAttemptFixture, seedTwoTenantJobAttemptFixture, countJobAttemptRowsForOrg, firstAttemptID); REUSED verbatim from invariants file: rawXxxRow, loadXxxRowByID, countXxxRowsForParent, mintXxxID, runAppendXxxOrFail, seedXxx; from sibling tenant-isolation: seedOrg/seedProject/seedQueuedJob/containsValue. NO migration added.
ralph/progress.txt:260:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go, PRIMARY *_test.go AND *_repository_invariants_test.go ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY + BE-046x INVARIANTS STORY** -- i.e. the tenant-isolation PRD story follows the same gap-fill pattern as the invariants story did (BE-0462 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/job_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for provisioning_jobs). Five load-bearing differences from a first-story-for-table tenant-isolation vertical (BE-0458 deployments, BE-0456 quota_reservations, etc.): (a) NO migration is added -- the table was already exercised by both the subsystem story AND the BE-046x invariants story; (b) the rawXxxRow/loadXxxRowByID/countXxxRowsForOrg/mintXxxID/xxxTxRollbackSentinel helpers ALREADY EXIST in the invariants_test file in the SAME package store_test -- grep BEFORE writing and REUSE verbatim; (c) the lightweight cross-tenant smoke tests in the subsystem _test.go are kept (do NOT delete them) and this file is a GAP-FILL against the canonical *_tenant_isolation_test.go template (deployment_tenant_isolation_test.go is the closest template), adding the byte-identity bystander proofs / no-leak substring checks / per-tenant COUNT(*) invariance / cascade-tenant-scope proofs the smoke tests deliberately did not pin; (d) helpers introduced here MUST carry the per-file suffix (`Job`, `Dep`, `QR`, ...) and ARE distinct from the rawXxxRow/loadXxxRowByID family already exported by the invariants file (those are in the same package); the new ones to add are timePtrEqualXxx, fmtTimePtrXxx, strPtrEqualXxx, fmtStrPtrXxx (only if the table has nullable *string columns), assertXxxByteIdentical, twoTenantXxxFixture+seedTwoTenantXxxFixture, plus one or two opinionated `seedYyyXxx` wrappers (e.g. seedQueuedJob/seedRunningJob); (e) when the table has NO `version` column (provisioning_jobs is the canonical example, vs deployments/projects/services which DO have one), the `assertXxxByteIdentical` MUST NOT compare a version column and the file header MUST call out `updated_at` as the SOLE trigger-managed drift anchor (the BEFORE UPDATE set_updated_at trigger is the load-bearing anchor; a missing organization_id predicate would surface as updated_at drift even when every other column happened to look right). Mandatory test shape (the 8 tests this file lands, adapted from BE-0458): Insert(orgA)DoesNotTouchOrgB (byte-identity over queued + running-with-live-lease + idempotency-collide bystanders), Insert(orgA)DoesNotChangeOrgBRowCount, InsertSameIdempotencyKeyInTwoTenantsBothPersist (positive direction + per-tenant FindByIdempotencyKey round-trip), GetCrossTenantReturnsNotFoundAndDoesNotEcho (typed apierr.NotFound + substring no-leak on idempotency_key/request_id/correlation_id/project_id), FindByIdempotencyKeyCrossTenantDoesNotLeak (returns (zero, false, nil) + zero-value field-by-field no-leak), <StateMutation>CrossTenantLeavesOrgABystandersByteIdentical (the table's single mutating surface attempted by orgB on orgA's id -- Cancel for deployments, Transition(..., cancelled, mut) for jobs -- MUST surface as apierr.NotFound NOT Conflict, because the FOR UPDATE SELECT under (organization_id, id) returns ErrNoRows BEFORE the state-machine validator runs; bystanders include the named target + queued sibling + running sibling carrying a live lease), ListByOrganizationCrossTenantReturnsEmptyAndDoesNotLeakTotal (empty slice + side-effect-free read + per-tenant COUNT(*) invariance), OrganizationDeleteCascadeIsTenantScoped (orgA deletion cascades only to orgA's rows; orgB rows survive byte-identical -- include a row carrying a live lease to anchor lease_owner/lease_deadline/started_at). Always document in the file header what the file DELIBERATELY DELEGATES (composite-FK cross-tenant-parent path pinned by invariants story, closed-set / lifecycle CHECKs / state-machine matrix / lease bookkeeping / claimer pinned by subsystem story, redactor + payload marshalling pinned by internal_test) so a future reader does not mistake the omission for a coverage gap. When the table's worker-side claimer is INTENTIONALLY NOT tenant-scoped (e.g. ClaimNext SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED across all tenants), call it out explicitly in the header rationale. Forward-applicable to any future tenant-isolation story whose table follows the (subsystem-tests + invariants-tests + table-has-no-version-column) shape (e.g. future job_attempts / dokploy_refs if their repos land before their PRD stories).
ralph/progress.txt:261:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go AND PRIMARY *_test.go ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY** — i.e. the CRUD-and-invariants PRD story is NOT the first story for the table (BE-0461 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/job_repository_invariants_test.go for provisioning_jobs). Structurally distinct from a "first-story-for-table" CRUD-and-invariants vertical (BE-0457 deployments, BE-0455 quota_reservations, etc.) in three load-bearing ways: (a) NO migration is added — the table landed in an earlier migration (0008_provisioning_jobs.up.sql for provisioning_jobs) and is already exercised by worker-subsystem tests; (b) NO existing helpers may be re-introduced — grep for `jobFixture / insertJob / transitionJob` (or the analogous resource fixtures) BEFORE writing and reuse them verbatim; collisions inside `package store_test` are compile errors; (c) the new file is a GAP-FILL pass against the canonical *_repository_invariants_test.go pattern (deployment_repository_invariants_test.go is the closest template), NOT a reimplementation. Mandatory gap-fill checklist (the 7-test shape this file lands): InsertMintsRowShape (byte-equal raw-SQL re-load vs Insert's RETURNING projection across every column, INCLUDING nullable resource-target legs which must round-trip as SQL NULL not empty string), InsertDuplicateIDReturnsConflict (PRIMARY KEY collision with a DIFFERENT idempotency key — distinct from the UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key) violation already pinned), InsertRollbackPersistsNoRow (sentinel-error rollback + countXxxRowsForOrg == 0 after), GetUnknownReturnsNotFound (same-tenant unknown id, NOT the cross-tenant variant — pgx.ErrNoRows must map to typed apierr.NotFound), ListByOrganizationReturnsReverseChronological (explicit sleeps + 3 rows + assert ordered created_at DESC, id DESC), ListByOrganizationEmptyReturnsEmptySlice (pin len == 0 rather than nil-vs-empty because some repos legitimately return either), ListByOrganizationClampsNonPositiveLimit (probe BOTH limit=0 AND limit=-1 — if the implementation used the caller's value as a literal LIMIT, both would return 0 rows). New helpers introduced (unique to this file, with a per-file `Job` suffix to avoid the package-shared `store_test` collisions): rawJobRow, loadJobRowByID, countJobRowsForOrg, mintJobID, jobTxRollbackSentinel. Reused verbatim: jobFixture, insertJob, transitionJob (from job_test.go), seedOrg/seedProject (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). The file header MUST document what it deliberately does NOT re-cover (validation guards / nil-Tx / state-machine paths / lease-claim / cross-tenant reads / closed-set status / redactor / payload marshalling are pinned by job_test.go and job_internal_test.go) so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. Special-case: the provisioning_jobs row body has THREE concurrent CHECK constraints (finished_consistent, lease_consistent, attempts_bounded) but Insert's coercion of Status='queued' / Attempts=0 / no lease columns makes them UNREACHABLE from public Insert input — they are Transition-time invariants, already pinned by job_test.go's Transition* suite; the invariants file does NOT try to drive them via Insert. Forward-applicable to any future "Repository: <existing-table> CRUD and invariants" story whose table already has worker-subsystem tests (e.g. future audit_events / job_attempts / dokploy_refs / idempotency_keys if their repos land before their PRD CRUD stories).
ralph/progress.txt:262:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for an **APPEND-ONLY CHILD table whose ONLY public mutation surface is Append() inside a *Tx, anchored by a composite FK (organization_id, deployment_id) to a tenant-scoped parent table with ON DELETE CASCADE, whose row body has NO version column AND NO updated_at column AND NO Update/Delete surface (BEFORE UPDATE trigger rejects every UPDATE at the SQL level), AND whose tenant teardown is reachable only TRANSITIVELY via parent-deployment-cascade OR parent-organization-cascade** (BE-0460 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_event_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0458 (deployments — lifecycle-bearing table with version + updated_at trigger pair and a Cancel state-transition surface) in five load-bearing ways: (a) there is NO bump_version trigger AND NO set_updated_at trigger to anchor a cross-row UPDATE detection, because the BEFORE UPDATE trigger rejects every UPDATE outright; the bystander byte-identity assertion is therefore really proving Append's INSERT statement never even ATTEMPTS a write outside the supplied (organization_id, deployment_id) tuple. (b) The cross-tenant guard test must seed a SYMMETRIC PAIR of forged Append attempts — (orgA, bravoDep) AND (orgB, alphaDep) — and probe deployment_events by the forged ids to assert count = 0 for BOTH; a single direction would miss a regression that derived organization_id from the deployment row at write time, AND a missing forged-id-was-never-persisted probe would miss a buggy retry loop that swallowed the FK error after a phantom INSERT. (c) The cascade variants are TRANSITIVE and must be tested at BOTH depths separately — parent-deployment delete cascade (composite FK ON DELETE CASCADE on deployments(organization_id, id)) AND parent-organization delete cascade (the transitive chain organizations -> deployments -> deployment_events) — because each FK leg's organization_id scoping must be proven independently; a regression that dropped the organization_id leg of either FK would surface as either a bravo row vanishing or its byte content drifting. (d) The "soft-deleted rows where applicable" acceptance-criteria phrase narrows to "where applicable": deployment_events has no soft-delete column and no Delete repository surface, so cascade is the analogous proof and is the substitute test surface — the file header MUST document this delegation explicitly so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. (e) The metadata column is jsonb stored as []byte; bystander byte-identity uses bytes.Equal not json equivalence (a regression that stripped or reordered keys would defeat json equivalence but survive bytes.Equal — and the test wants to catch ANY rewrite, semantic or representational). The 8-test shape (Append-bystander-byte-identity across queued+running+progress peers / Append-row-count-invariant / Append-foreign-parent-deployment-rejected-BOTH-directions-with-forged-id-not-persisted+bystander-byte-identity / GetByID-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak (message/metadata key/value/request_id/correlation_id/event_type/deployment_id) / ListByDeployment-cross-tenant-empty-and-counts-unchanged across per-org AND per-deployment probes / ListByDeployment-foreign-deployment-id-of-SAME-tenant-returns-empty (composite (organization_id, deployment_id) probe distinct from "foreign org+foreign deployment") / parent-deployment-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped across queued+running+progress orgB peers / parent-organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped) leaves BE-0459's invariants surface (CRUD/append-only-trigger/closed-set-event_type/composite-FK/duplicate-id/nil-tx/rollback/metadata-redaction/caller-supplied-occurred_at/cascade-on-deployment) untouched. Helpers reused from BE-0459: rawDeploymentEventRow, loadDeploymentEventRowByID, countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment, mintDeploymentEventID, runAppendDeploymentEventOrFail, seedDeploymentEvent; from BE-0458: seedTwoTenantDeploymentFixture, twoTenantDeploymentFixture type; from BE-0457: seedQueuedDeployment; from store_test: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService, newStore, containsValue. New helpers introduced (unique to this file): twoTenantDeploymentEventFixture + seedTwoTenantDeploymentEventFixture (the two-tenant boilerplate that ALSO seeds one parent deployment per tenant, because events need a parent deployment as the FK target — a pure two-tenant org/proj/env/svc fixture is insufficient for events), assertDeploymentEventByteIdentical (anchored on every column INCLUDING metadata []byte via bytes.Equal; NO version/updated_at columns to anchor cross-row UPDATE detection), countDeploymentEventRowsForOrg (per-tenant aggregate variant of countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment — the cascade tests need both, per-tenant is the cascade-scoped probe, per-deployment is the Append-scoped probe). Forward-applicable to any tenant-isolation story whose table is an append-only child anchored by a composite FK to a tenant-scoped parent with cascade (e.g. future audit_events / job_events / log lines stories).
ralph/progress.txt:264:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED LIFECYCLE-BEARING table whose Insert is caller-id (no per-row id minting in the repository), whose only public state-transition surface is a single Cancel() with optimistic-concurrency disambiguation, and whose row body carries a lifecycle CHECK (terminal-status <-> finished_at) plus a bump_version trigger** (BE-0457 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0455 (quota_reservations — the repository mints qres_<token> ids and has no Update/Cancel surface) in three load-bearing ways: (a) the test must mint deployment ids itself (DeploymentService.Create owns domain.NewID(domain.KindDeployment); the repo does NOT mint). A test-local mintDeploymentID(t, suffix) helper using `"dep_"+t.Name()+"_"+suffix` avoids global PK collisions across parallel cases without a shared atomic counter — every *_test.go under internal/controlplane/store/ shares one package, so t.Name() is the natural disambiguator. (b) The terminal-status-on-Insert probe leverages the deployments_finished_consistent CHECK: Insert's INSERT column list omits finished_at, so any terminal Status passed in violates the CHECK by construction (status terminal AND finished_at NULL). This is the one-line proof that this surface can only mint non-terminal rows; no separate trigger test is needed. (c) Cancel exercises THREE disambiguation legs against the SAME *Tx — nil expectedVersion + terminal row -> apierr.Conflict (no current_version attached), non-nil expectedVersion + version mismatch -> apierr.ConflictStale (current_version attached, verified via apierr.CurrentVersionOf), unknown id -> apierr.NotFound. The cross-tenant Cancel probe MUST include a byte-identity assertion on the foreign-tenant row (status unchanged, finished_at still NULL, version not bumped) — without it, a regression that scoped the UPDATE by id alone and disambiguated by organization_id would silently mutate the foreign tenant's row before failing the lookup. The 31-test shape (Insert: row-shape / status-default / non-queued-status-persists / dup-id / dup-(org,idem) / blank-id / blank-requested_by / blank-idem / unknown-source / terminal-without-finished / unknown-org / cross-tenant-service / nil-tx / rollback; GetByID: persisted-row / unknown / cross-tenant; FindByIdempotencyKey: present / missing / cross-tenant; Cancel: queued / running / matching-version / stale-version-ConflictStale / terminal-row-Conflict / unknown-NotFound / cross-tenant-NotFound-with-byte-identity / nil-tx; ListByService: reverse-chronological / empty-non-nil-slice / cross-tenant-empty) leaves the BE-0458 tenant-isolation file to add the bystander byte-identical proof + organization-delete-cascade probes. Helpers reused: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). New helpers introduced (unique to this file): rawDeploymentRow, loadDeploymentRowByID, countDeploymentRowsForOrg, mintDeploymentID, runInsertDeploymentOrFail, seedQueuedDeployment, seedRunningDeployment, deploymentTxRollbackSentinel.
ralph/progress.txt:266:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an EXTERNAL code path (quota.Checker, not the repository), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO — ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) isolates rows by resource within a tenant); (c) the row-already-exists-with-non-zero-value leg has no policies analog — pre-seed via raw SQL at a sentinel value (e.g. used_value=17, id=`qu_seed_<resource>`) and prove BOTH the seeded id AND the seeded used_value survive a LockUsage call (this is the ONLY way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted prefix id does not replace the row's id — a regression to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would be a catastrophic data-loss path that typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to the typed Resource (e.g. `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")`) to bypass Go's compile-time check and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer — surfacing as yerr.CodeConflict through mapWriteError. The 8-new-test shape (row-shape, second-call-byte-identity, peer-row-byte-identity, persisted-used-value, unknown-org-Conflict, unknown-resource-Conflict, tx-rollback, nil-tx-Internal) leaves the pre-existing TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageCreatesAndReturnsCounter in quota_repository_test.go alone as the load-bearing two-call-idempotency probe. Helper symbols introduced: `lockUsageOrFail`, `loadQuotaUsageRow`, `countQuotaUsageRowsForOrg`, `rawQuotaUsageRow`, `quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel`. Reused symbols from sibling files: `seedOrg` (schema_test.go), `seedQuotaUsage` (usage_test.go), `newStore` (store_test.go) — note `seedQuotaUsage` is the ONLY way to set used_value to a non-zero value through a test fixture because the repository surface has no equivalent (LockUsage cannot increment).
ralph/progress.txt:267:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **DUAL-SCOPE ledger-style table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert and whose row body is observable EXCLUSIVELY through typed resolvers (no per-id Get/Update/Delete, no ListByParent)** (BE-0452 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go). quota_policies has TWO scope_kind values ('plan_default' with NULL organization_id; 'organization' with non-NULL) anchored by partial unique indexes (e.g. `quota_policies_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind='organization'`) and NO version column — so the byte-identical-bystander anchor reduces to the trigger-managed updated_at ALONE. The 9-test shape diverges from BE-0440 (project_grant, ListByParent-only) and BE-0436 (api_key_scope, per-id Get + per-id mutations) in four load-bearing ways: (a) cross-tenant projection is proved through EVERY resolver leg independently — one test per resolver method (EffectiveLimit per-resource tenant-scoped; ListEffectiveLimits count isolation; ListOrganizationUsage two-CTE-leg isolation) — because each resolver has its own WHERE clause and a regression dropping any one leg leaks a different column; (b) a NEW plan-isolation leg is added: orgA on 'starter' must not see 'pro' plan defaults even though both rows live in the SAME table with NULL organization_id — the plan column is the second tenant boundary on the plan_default scope and a missing plan predicate is a distinct regression surface from a missing organization_id predicate; (c) the byte-identical-bystander proof is observed via a RAW-SQL load of the underlying quota_policies row (reusing BE-0451's loadOrganizationPolicyRow + rawQuotaPolicyRow), because the typed reader projections deliberately omit id/created_at/updated_at/scope_kind/plan — a regression that stamped correct-looking limit_value and enforcement_mode onto the peer's row would slip past every typed-resolver projection if asserted only via EffectiveLimit; (d) ListOrganizationUsage has TWO independent tenant-predicate legs (one in the policies CTE on organization_id, one in the usage CTE on organization_id) — they need INDEPENDENT proofs because either leg dropping alone leaks a different column (limit_value vs used_value), so the file has BOTH a "policies-CTE leg isolated" test (orgA has an override, orgB has overrides on different resources, no counters) AND a "usage-CTE leg isolated" test (both tenants have a quota_usage counter on the SAME resource with DISTINCT used_value). NO per-id Get => NO (Code, Hint) existence-leak projection: the cross-tenant probe surfaces as an EMPTY projection (resolver returns ok=false, or len(rows)==0), NOT a typed apierr.NotFound — the empty-slice / ok=false IS the existence-indistinguishability proof. The shared-conflict-tuple fixture for the Upsert-UPDATE bystander test MUST be built via upsertOrganizationPolicy (not seedOrgPolicy raw SQL), because using the repository to build the fixture both proves the partial unique index lets the peer row land at all AND aligns baseline.updated_at timestamps so a wrong-row UPDATE shows up as sub-second drift. The corollary "same-resource-in-two-tenants-both-succeed" test is the load-bearing positive-direction pair — without it, a regression that resolved the conflict by (resource,) alone would have prevented the bystander INSERT, and every byte-identical assertion in the UPDATE-branch test would silently pass against a non-existent row. Helper naming caveat: nullable-column helpers MUST get a per-file suffix (stringPtrEqualQP / fmtStringPtrQP) — BE-0440's project-grant file already defines a stringPtrEqual variant in store_test and same-named helpers collide at compile time. Forward-applicable to any future dual-scope ledger-style table (a hypothetical KindFeatureFlagDefault + KindFeatureFlagOverride pairing, or KindPriceTier defaults + organization overrides, would inherit this template).
ralph/progress.txt:268:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **multi-table resource FAMILY whose physical realization is one table per scope in the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy AND whose row body carries SECRET-BEARING columns (value plaintext + sealed-secret tuple)** (BE-0450 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/{organization,project,environment,service}_variable_tenant_isolation_test.go). Mirrors the BE-0449 per-scope split of the invariants tests: when the family is hierarchical with diverging mutation surfaces (organization variant exposes per-key GetByKey + DeleteByKey + UpdateMutable on top of the bulk Upsert + DeleteByXxxExceptKeys pair; project/environment/service variants expose ONLY the bulk pair), ship one *_tenant_isolation_test.go per physical table. The project/environment/service files converge on a uniform 8-test shape: ListByX-correct-across-tenants, ListByX-cross-tenant-scope-returns-empty, ListByX-isolates-shared-key, Upsert-INSERT-bystander-byte-identical, Upsert-UPDATE-bystander-byte-identical, DeleteByXExceptKeys-clear-all-bystander, DeleteByXExceptKeys-keep-subset-bystander, Upsert-same-key-in-two-tenants-both-succeed. The organization file ADDS four per-key tests that have no analog in the other three: GetByKey-cross-tenant-returns-NotFound (with error-surface no-value-echo), DeleteByKey-on-orgA-bystander (plus second-DeleteByKey-on-now-gone-key-returns-NotFound-no-value-echo), UpdateMutable-on-orgA-bystander (Version bumps on orgA, orgB stays byte-identical), UpdateMutable-cross-tenant-key-returns-NotFound-no-value-echo-bystander-byte-identical. THE LOAD-BEARING DIVERGENCE from the BE-0448 grant tenant-isolation template is the byte-identical-bystander projection: variables MUST anchor BOTH Value AND the (SecretProvider, SecretKeyID, SecretCiphertext) tuple AND the IsSecret flag, because variables carry secret-bearing columns that grants do not — a regression that stamped orgA's payload onto orgB's row or that re-sealed orgB's row with orgA's secret bytes would otherwise slip past every identity-column check. bytes.Equal (stdlib) is the right SecretCiphertext compare helper; no custom one needed. Helper REUSE strategy across the family: organization/environment/service tenant-isolation files reuse newXVariableID / upsertXVariable / listXVariablesOrFail defined in their sibling BE-0449 invariants files (same store_test package); the project file DEFINES its own newProjectVariableID(f, org, label) / upsertProjectVariable / listProjectVariablesOrFail because project_variable_repository_invariants_test.go inlines its helpers (no shared symbols to reuse). The project helper signature takes f + org because f.Project requires an org to synthesise the id — the org/env/service helpers use a factory-counter-only or fake-parent scheme. Picking the wrong helper shape at call sites is the most common compile failure when porting tests across the family. Each file defines getXVariableFromListOrFail and assertXVariableByteIdentical with distinct names to avoid same-package collisions across store_test. The containsValue substring helper for the error-surface no-value-echo assertions is defined in the organization file (only organization-scope per-key methods can RETURN errors with message/hint text); env/service/project paths surface their failures via FK Conflict or PK Conflict, both of which are invariants-file legs (not tenant-isolation legs). The "Upsert-same-key-in-two-tenants-both-succeed" test is structurally necessary as the positive-direction pair for the bystander byte-identical proofs — without it, a regression that resolved the conflict on (scope_id, key) alone would still pass every bystander check (the bystander INSERTs first, the peer Upsert then hits PK uniqueness or no conflict at all). OrganizationVariableRepository.ListByOrganization returns a NIL slice on no-rows (unlike its project/environment/service siblings which use make([]T, 0)) — the tenant-isolation contract is "len == 0", not "non-nil"; the org-scope test documents this divergence in a comment and DOES NOT assert non-nil. Forward-applicable to any future multi-table secret-bearing family at this hierarchy.
ralph/progress.txt:269:- Repository CRUD invariants for a **multi-table resource FAMILY whose physical realization is one table per scope in the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy, sharing identical CHECK / FK / CASCADE / bump_version / set_updated_at invariants but with per-scope mutation surfaces** (BE-0449 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/{organization,project,environment,service}_variable_repository_invariants_test.go). A PRD note that reads "Database table: <name>" can refer to such a family (variables = 4 scope tables, quota_* = 3 ledger tables) — when the family is hierarchical, ship one *_repository_invariants_test.go per physical table rather than collapsing into a single mega-file, because per-scope mutation surfaces diverge (organization_variables uniquely exposes UpdateMutable + DeleteByKey on top of the bulk-replace surface; project / environment / service variables expose only the bulk Upsert + DeleteByXxxExceptKeys pair). Each file in the family extends the BE-0447 grant-table invariants template with three uniform substitutions: (a) the Upsert payload struct is the scope's XxxVariableUpsert (Value, IsSecret, SecretProvider, SecretKeyID, SecretCiphertext) instead of role + principal_kind; (b) a brand-new secret_columns_consistent CHECK-violation test is added (IsSecret=true with non-empty Value triggers the secret branch's `value=''` clause and surfaces as typed apierr.Conflict — exhaustively probing the OTHER drift directions is needless because the repository's nullableSecretColumns helper makes them structurally impossible from the caller side); (c) the leaf-scope variants (env / service) add a cross-tenant composite-FK test mirroring BE-0447's service-grant cross-tenant proof. Every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same store_test package, so the per-scope helpers MUST get distinct names — newOrganizationVariableID / newEnvironmentVariableID / newServiceVariableID, upsertOrganizationVariable / upsertEnvironmentVariable / upsertServiceVariable, listOrganizationVariablesOrFail / listEnvironmentVariablesOrFail / listServiceVariablesOrFail, and organizationVariableTxRollbackSentinel / projectVariableTxRollbackSentinel / environmentVariableTxRollbackSentinel / serviceVariableTxRollbackSentinel. The project-variables file is the smallest because project_variable_test.go already covers Upsert INSERT/UPDATE happy paths, cross-tenant rejection, the bulk delete surface, and the nil-Tx guards — its invariants file pins ONLY the row-shape and lifecycle invariants the existing tests do not anchor (version=1 default, byte-equal created_at == updated_at, ON CONFLICT id+created_at preservation, PK reuse -> Conflict, secret CHECK violation, ON DELETE CASCADE, Upsert tx-rollback). Forward-applicable to any future multi-table family at this hierarchy (a hypothetical KindOrganizationSetting / KindProjectSetting / KindEnvironmentSetting / KindServiceSetting feature would inherit this 4-file template structure).
ralph/progress.txt:270:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped LEAF-grant table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion, with a database-managed version column, NO nullable scope pointer below the parent FK, and a composite FK to a leaf-entity parent (organization_id, service_id) -> services** (BE-0447 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). Structural reduction of the BE-0443 environment_grant_repository_invariants_test.go template: every leg that exercised a nullable *string scope pointer collapses or drops out — the round-trip pointer test is replaced by a service-account-principal-kind test (the closed-set CHECK leg still needs proving), the NULLS-FIRST ordering assertion in ListByService is gone (two-column ORDER BY principal_id, id), the empty-string-COALESCE dedup equivalence leg is gone (no COALESCE in the unique index), and the upsert/replace input struct drops the trailing *string field. The Upsert signature is six-positional `(id, organization_id, parent_id, principal_id, principal_kind, role)`; the ON CONFLICT branch keys on the three-tuple `(organization_id, parent_id, principal_id)`. The principal-kind cross-smuggling invariant MUST be preserved verbatim (re-upsert with a different caller-supplied principal_kind for the same principal_id; assert persisted kind = baseline). The duplicate-PK test must use a DIFFERENT principal_id so the ON CONFLICT branch does NOT match and the PRIMARY KEY (id) uniqueness violation is exercised instead of the upsert path. The ON DELETE CASCADE proof must delete the parent via raw SQL (the ServiceRepository.ScheduleDeletion is a SOFT delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE). The Upsert rollback proof reduces to ListByService-only because no per-id Get exists; ListByService is the only observation surface for a grant row. NEW Kind constant in domain/id.go MUST be added to BOTH the const block AND the canonical `kinds` map at the bottom — Valid() and the ID parser read from the map, not the const list, so a missing map entry silently breaks NewID(KindFoo) with ErrUnknownKind. For brand-new tables, services already exposes UNIQUE (organization_id, id) from migration 0002 so NO companion ALTER TABLE is needed on the parent (UNLIKE BE-0435 api_keys parent). Forward-applicable to any future grant table whose parent IS the leaf of the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy or whose parent is otherwise structurally leaf-like (a hypothetical KindDeploymentGrant / KindJobGrant would inherit this template).
ralph/progress.txt:271:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped child table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete, no traditional UPDATE), with a database-managed version column, optional nullable scoping pointers, and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0439 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). Two structural divergences from BE-0435 (api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go) are load-bearing: (1) the Upsert ON CONFLICT branch must be tested with a DIFFERENT caller-supplied id AND a DIFFERENT caller-supplied principal_kind to prove BOTH the caller-id-is-ignored guarantee AND the cross-kind-smuggling-impossible guarantee — the principal_kind column is NOT part of the scope tuple, so an existing row stays at its current principal_kind even when the caller passes a different kind for the same principal_id; a single Role-only update test would silently pass while a regression that promoted principal_kind to a SET clause still went undetected. (2) The Upsert rollback proof reduces to ListByProject-only because the repository exposes no per-id Get for grants — ListByProject is the ONLY observation surface for a grant row, so it is the load-bearing leak channel a partial commit would surface through, and an absent Get assertion is a deliberate file boundary, not a gap. Insert duplicate-id test must use a DIFFERENT principal_id so the ON CONFLICT (org, proj, principal, env, svc) branch does NOT match and the PRIMARY KEY (id) uniqueness violation is exercised — otherwise the upsert path silently swallows the duplicate id. DeleteByProjectExceptIDs has TWO codepaths in the repository (len(keepIDs)==0 -> unconditional DELETE; len>0 -> DELETE WHERE id NOT IN (ANY $3::text[])), so BOTH must be exercised independently — the empty-keep test must additionally use a fresh seed after the nil-keep clear AND assert the empty-slice ([]string{}) form is identical to nil at the wire (the repository documents nil and empty as identical, so a regression that diverged them would surface here). ON DELETE CASCADE from projects is the project-grant analogue of api_key_scope's ON DELETE CASCADE from api_keys: use raw `DELETE FROM projects WHERE id=$1` (NOT the repository's ScheduleDeletion, which is a SOFT delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE) and observe via ListByProject. Helper `newProjectGrantID(f, label)` synthesises a `pgrnt_<token_n>` id by rewriting f.Project's `prj_<token_n>` prefix — the testutil.Factory only stamps known domain prefixes and there is no f.ProjectGrant, so prefix-rewriting is the cleanest way to mint factory-unique ids without reaching into domain.NewID(domain.KindProjectGrant). The sibling project_grant_test.go's `seedProjectGrant` raw-SQL helper is kept intentionally separate: that file's tests exercise the read path against rows the schema (not the repository) inserted, so a regression that broke INSERT would still let the read tests pass — by contrast THIS file's `upsertProjectGrant` exercises the repository's INSERT path, so the two seed surfaces are complementary, not duplicates.
ralph/progress.txt:272:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped table whose mutation surface spans FOUR distinct UPDATE paths (Update / UpdateDisplayName / ScheduleDeletion / Restore) PLUS Insert, with a database-managed version column AND a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at)** (BE-0437 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_repository_invariants_test.go): this is the BE-0425 organization template extended with the second-UPDATE-path projection (UpdateDisplayName must run the dual-anchor AND prove the path-specific WHERE/SET isolation — the row's slug must be untouched since the SET clause is `SET display_name = $3` only) and the BE-0431 inverse-soft-delete projection (Restore must dual-anchor AND clear deletion_scheduled_at back to NULL — the trigger fires on any UPDATE so a forgotten `SET deletion_scheduled_at = NULL` clause would still pass the version/timestamp anchors, making the "deletion_scheduled_at is nil after Restore" assertion the load-bearing one). Insert rollback proof must span ALL read surfaces the rolled-back row touches: by-id Get, by-org ListByOrganization, AND CountByOrganization (the count the quota layer reads), so a future refactor that switched Insert to a SAVEPOINT or autonomous transaction would leak the row into a quota check even though Get reports NotFound. The nil-Tx typed-Internal guard only needs to close the ONE mutation path the existing tests did not cover (here UpdateDisplayName) — duplicating Insert/Update/ScheduleDeletion/Restore guards already proved elsewhere is noise. Test fixtures compose seedOrg + projectFixture(f.Project(org, label)) + insertProject helpers from project_test.go; do not re-implement them at package scope.
ralph/progress.txt:273:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped grandchild table whose mutation surface is Insert + Update + ScheduleDeletion ONLY (NO UpdateDisplayName, NO Restore), with a database-managed version column, a CHECK-constrained closed-set Kind column, a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0441 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_repository_invariants_test.go): the BE-0437 project_repository_invariants_test.go template MINUS the UpdateDisplayName and Restore tests (the EnvironmentRepository exposes neither method — adding empty stubs that reference store.EnvironmentRepository.UpdateDisplayName / Restore would fail to compile), so the invariants matrix shrinks from six DB-backed tests to four (Insert dual-fields-anchor, Update dual-anchor, Update matching-If-Match positive, ScheduleDeletion dual-anchor) PLUS the Insert-rollback and Insert-nil-Tx pair. Update's preserved-identity assertion gains a Kind-preservation check (the SET clause is `SET slug = $3, display_name = $4` only, so kind is structurally untouched even though the column exists on this table) that the projects template did not need (projects has no kind column); ScheduleDeletion's preserved-identity assertion ALSO gains a Kind check for the same reason. The nil-Tx typed-Internal matrix-closer is Insert (NOT UpdateDisplayName as in projects) because environment_update_test.go and environment_delete_test.go already cover Update and ScheduleDeletion respectively — the matrix-closer convention is "fill the missing path", and the missing path is table-specific. New file-scope helpers `environmentFixture` and `insertEnvironment` mirror projectFixture/insertProject but environmentFixture MUST default Kind to store.EnvironmentKindStandard (the migration 0025 CHECK constraint requires "standard" or "preview" — empty string fails) — the raw-SQL seedEnvironment in schema_test.go relies on the database column default, but the repository's INSERT path supplies kind explicitly. Rollback proof scope is by-id GetByID + parent-scoped ListByProject (there is no CountByOrganization equivalent on environments — quota counts environments per-project, not per-org, and per-project counts are not yet exposed on the repository surface; do NOT extend the rollback assertion to a non-existent method). Rollback sentinel type is environmentTxRollbackSentinel — every *_repository_invariants_test.go in store_test contributes its own uniquely-named sentinel.
ralph/progress.txt:274:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped child table with a database-managed version column, a composite FK to a parent api_keys row, and NO secret-bearing column** (BE-0436 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_scope_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0430 membership_tenant_isolation_test.go shape is the closest template; four substitutions are mandatory: (a) the cross-tenant indistinguishability probe uses the SAME (Code, Hint) anchor pattern, but on a single id column rather than a composite key — both the cross-tenant scope id and an unknown scope id under the prober's tenant must render byte-identical typed NotFound; (b) the byte-identical-bystander assertion carries TWO independent trigger anchors (api_key_scopes_set_updated_at AND api_key_scopes_bump_version) instead of memberships' one (memberships_set_updated_at + role_version-only-via-UpdateRole). Both must be asserted because a regression could drift one without the other; (c) the load-bearing worst-case fixture is the shared-scope-text fixture (both tenants own a scope row with the SAME scope text on their OWN respective keys) because the (organization_id, api_key_id, scope) UNIQUE constraint is per-tenant, so the WHERE filter is the only thing keeping the rows apart at the read path — every byte-identical-bystander test uses this fixture as a result; (d) ListByAPIKey is a SECOND tenant-scoped list path (the parent api_key_id is the secondary tenant-scoping anchor) and must be tested both for cross-tenant api_key_id (returns empty non-nil slice) AND indistinguishable from an unknown api_key_id under the prober's tenant. The api_key_scopes table has NO secret-bearing column (the scope text itself is the customer-facing capability string), so the BE-0434 raw-secret_hash probe has no analogue and the byte-identical-bystander assertion alone covers every observable column. Helpers seedAPIKeyForScopes / newAPIKeyScope / insertAPIKeyScope are reused from api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go in the same store_test package — sibling-file test name collisions (e.g. TestAPIKeyScopeRepositoryListByAPIKeyIsTenantScoped from BE-0435) MUST be checked with `grep '^func Test'` on the sibling invariants file before adding new test names. Future tenant-scoped child tables with a version column and no secret-bearing column (variables.value when is_secret=false, future per-scope capability tables, scoped grant link tables not yet sharded out) should reuse THIS file as the template; if the row body carries a secret-bearing column add the BE-0434 raw-column probe on top.
ralph/progress.txt:276:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped grandchild table whose ONLY read surface is a parent-scoped ListByProject (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete), with TWO mutation paths (Upsert with ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE PLUS DeleteByProjectExceptIDs whose len(keepIDs)==0 and len>0 codepaths must each be exercised), a database-managed version column, optional nullable scoping pointers (environment_id, service_id), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0440 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant_tenant_isolation_test.go). This is the BE-0436 api_key_scope_tenant_isolation template adapted for a grandchild table whose conflict target itself includes (organization_id, project_id) AND a multi-column scope tuple. Five projections api_key_scopes had no analogue for are load-bearing here: (a) the byte-identical-bystander proof is observed through ListByProject (the only read surface) — there is no per-id Get like api_key_scopes had, so a helper getGrantFromListOrFail must scan the list response for the bystander id and fatal on absence; the implementation favours correctness over efficiency because the bystander lists are 1-2 rows by construction. (b) the byte-identical-bystander assertGrantByteIdentical helper must compare TWO nullable *string pointer columns (environment_id, service_id) in addition to the scalar columns — pair-helper stringPtrEqual / fmtStringPtr emit "<nil>" vs quoted string for failing assertions so nullability and value drift surface side-by-side; api_key_scopes had no pointer columns, so this is the first tenant-isolation file with a *string comparison. (c) the load-bearing worst-case fixture is the shared-scope-tuple fixture (both tenants own a grant row with the SAME (principal_id, environment_id, service_id) tuple on their OWN projects) AND the Upsert UPDATE-branch bystander test re-upserts with a DIFFERENT role + a fresh caller-supplied id to BOTH fire the ON CONFLICT branch on orgA AND prove the caller-id is ignored — a regression that resolved the conflict by scope-tuple alone (without organization_id and project_id in the conflict target) would have UPDATEd orgB on the peer-trigger fire. (d) DeleteByProjectExceptIDs needs TWO bystander tests because the repository has two SQL codepaths (one unconditional DELETE for nil/empty keepIDs, one DELETE WHERE id NOT IN ANY for non-empty keepIDs); the len>0 test must seed orgB grants whose id is NOT in keepIDs so a regression that dropped the tenant predicate would have deleted them through the "NOT IN ANY" branch. (e) ListByProject cross-tenant projectID returns an empty non-nil slice, so the cross-tenant indistinguishability projection is len==0 + non-nil + unknown-projectID-under-own-tenant yields the SAME empty-non-nil shape (NOT the Code+Hint NotFound shape from api_key_scopes / projects, because grants are read through a list path, not a per-id Get). Helpers seedOrg / seedProject / newProjectGrantID / upsertProjectGrant / listGrantsOrFail are reused from project_grant_test.go and project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go in the same store_test package — sibling-file test name collisions (TestProjectGrantRepo* in project_grant_test.go, TestProjectGrantRepository* in project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go) MUST be checked with `grep '^func Test'` on BOTH sibling files before adding new test names; this file uses the TestProjectGrantRepository* prefix (matching the invariants file convention) with unique suffixes (...OnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB, ...IsolatesSharedPrincipal, ...InTwoTenantsBothSucceed). The file's docstring MUST call out which legs are already proved elsewhere (cross-tenant FK Conflict, duplicate-PK Conflict, ON DELETE CASCADE, tx-rollback, nil-Tx guards in BE-0439; ListByProject ordering and basic cross-tenant empty-list property in project_grant_test.go) and which are NEW HERE (byte-identical bystander on version + updated_at + role + pointer columns across BOTH Upsert branches AND BOTH DeleteByProjectExceptIDs codepaths, indistinguishability of cross-tenant vs unknown projectID at the list path, per-tenant scope-tuple coexistence, shared-principal_id read isolation) so a future reader does not look for missing tests where the proof is intentionally elsewhere.
ralph/progress.txt:279:- New customer-data resource = full vertical when it is the FIRST story for that table (BE-0435 pattern, api_key_scopes migration 0033, store/api_key_scope.go, api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go): this story is allowed to land migration + repository + first-pass tests because no prior story touched the table (no migration, no repo, no port, no handler, no wiring). The migration declares the table tenant-scoped at the database via the composite FK pattern established by project_grants / environment_grants — and when the parent table never exposed UNIQUE (organization_id, id) (api_keys was such a table) the SAME migration adds the constraint to the parent so the child's composite FK has a target; this is a safe additive change because the parent's PK already makes id unique and the new UNIQUE merely pairs it with organization_id. Repository follows the membership.go / project_grant.go pattern: stateless struct, *Tx for mutations with nil-Tx -> typed apierr.Internal, Querier for reads, pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound, mapWriteError for constraint-violation -> apierr.Conflict, tag.RowsAffected() == 0 -> apierr.NotFound on Delete. Tests combine CRUD + row-shape invariants in one file since no prior tests exist; tenant-isolation byte-identical-bystander proof is deferred to the paired BE-0436 (tenant-isolation) story that always follows.
ralph/progress.txt:280:- Test fixture caveat for api_keys-related seeding (BE-0435 learning, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go seedAPIKeyForScopes): testutil.Factory.APIKey stamps a per-FACTORY prefix `yk_<token[:8]>`, NOT a per-key prefix. Two keys minted from the same factory get the same Prefix value, which collides with the api_keys_prefix_key UNIQUE constraint when two are inserted in the same test. Always derive the persisted prefix as `key.Prefix + "_" + key.ID` (or similar per-row salt) — never use `key.Prefix + "_" + label` because the same label is reused across orgs in tenant-isolation fixtures and the label is not unique.
ralph/progress.txt:284:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **composite-key tenant-scoped link table** with a database-managed version column (BE-0429 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/membership_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go shape is the closest template, but four substitutions are mandatory because the row has no own-id, has a (organization_id, user_id) composite primary key, and carries role_version + the memberships_set_updated_at trigger: (a) the duplicate-Insert Conflict test asserts on the PK collision (a second Insert with the same (org, user) pair) instead of the citext-UNIQUE-email collision — the schema has no UNIQUE column outside the PK that an INSERT could violate; (b) the existing membership_test.go's UpdateRole case already asserts the role_version atomic +1 increment, so the invariants file's UpdateRole test is dedicated to the timestamp dual-anchor (updated_at refreshed by the BEFORE-UPDATE trigger, created_at preserved) — do NOT duplicate the role_version increment here; (c) NO Delete-cascades-to-children test (the membership row is the SINK of two ON-DELETE-CASCADE FKs from users and organizations, not a SOURCE — cascades INTO memberships are proved in the parent table's invariants file, e.g. TestUserRepositoryDeleteCascadesToMemberships in user_repository_invariants_test.go); (d) the local rollback sentinel type MUST use a distinct name from user_repository_invariants_test.go's `errSentinel` because every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same store_test package — `membershipTxRollbackSentinel struct{}` is the canonical naming choice. The Insert-returns-row-with-timestamps test additionally asserts (a) role_version == 1 (the schema default that backs session-token revocation in BE-0020 auth), (b) Email + UserDisplayName populated from the JOINed users row (Insert returns OrganizationMember, not raw Membership — the httpapi layer renders the joined shape directly), and (c) created_at.Equal(updated_at) on the fresh row (the trigger has not fired yet). Seed pattern: seedOrg + seedUserRow first, then call repo.Insert through the repository (NOT seedMembership, which is the raw-INSERT helper for tenant-isolation tests). Forward-applicable to BE-0431/0432 (service_accounts CRUD + tenant-isolation) and BE-0433/0434 (api_keys CRUD + tenant-isolation) — both are tenant-scoped tables with their own version-tracking columns, so the rule "split the version-increment assertion (canonical CRUD test) from the timestamp-dual-anchor assertion (invariants test)" should also apply.
ralph/progress.txt:285:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped table whose only customer mutation is an idempotent lifecycle flip** (BE-0431 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceaccount_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go and BE-0429 membership_repository_invariants_test.go shapes are the templates, but three substitutions are mandatory because service_accounts has no generic Update and no version column — only Insert + Disable (COALESCE-idempotent): (a) the trigger-dual-anchor test drives through the **lifecycle mutation** (Disable) instead of a generic Update — created_at preserved, updated_at moves forward via the BEFORE UPDATE trigger, plus the lifecycle field (disabled_at) is set and IsDisabled() flips true; (b) the idempotent-second-call semantics are NOT re-asserted here (they belong in the canonical CRUD test in serviceaccount_test.go); (c) the rollback-leaves-zero-footprint test asserts BOTH Get-is-NotFound AND CountByOrganization == 0 (the quota reader is the second observable surface a half-written row could surface through, and a future refactor that switched Insert to a SAVEPOINT or autonomous transaction would silently inflate it without ever surfacing on Get). Distinct sentinel type name (`serviceAccountTxRollbackSentinel`) to avoid the store_test package-level collision with `errSentinel` (user) and `membershipTxRollbackSentinel` (membership). Forward-applicable to BE-0433/0434 (api_keys CRUD + tenant-isolation): api_keys has an Update path (rotate / revoke / patch metadata), so the invariants file should re-introduce the full Update-trigger dual-anchor test from the user/membership template alongside the rollback + nil-Tx guards; the secret-bearing nature of api_keys means the rollback test must additionally assert that no secret_hash needle leaks into post-rollback observable state.
ralph/progress.txt:287:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, NO generic Update, and NO soft-delete column** — only Insert + Disable lifecycle flip (BE-0432 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceaccount_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0428 user_tenant_isolation_test.go and BE-0430 membership_tenant_isolation_test.go shapes are the templates, but four substitutions are mandatory because service_accounts has its OWN id (no shared-identity column the way memberships shares user_id with the global users table) and the load-bearing cross-row failure mode is "two tenants happen to share a slug (UNIQUE (organization_id, slug) permits this by design) and a WHERE-on-slug-only or WHERE-on-id-only mutation swaps the WRONG tenant's row": (a) every byte-identical-snapshot test seeds BOTH tenants with service accounts holding the SAME slug via a `seedServiceAccountWithSlug(t, db, f, org, label, slug)` helper that overrides the factory's slug — a WHERE-on-slug mistake would resolve to whichever row the planner found first and the per-tenant id assertion would catch it (the BE-0430 "same user, distinct role/role_version tuples" framing translates here to "different ids, SAME slug"; the inverse is the only cross-row leak surface a tenant-root-with-its-own-id table exposes); (b) the Disable bystander test is the analogue of BE-0430's UpdateRole bystander — Disable is the only mutation beyond Insert, so the BE-0428 generic Update test does not apply; the trigger dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed) PLUS the lifecycle field (disabled_at == nil) on the bystander row catch any WHERE-on-slug-only Disable that ever matched the wrong row; (c) the duplicate-Insert Conflict bystander test snapshots BOTH the conflicting target tenant's row AND the same-slug-different-tenant bystander row AND a post-state CountByOrganization == 1 for each (since the trigger is BEFORE-UPDATE only, a rolled-back INSERT that resolved its UNIQUE check against the cross-tenant row would have no trigger anchor — Count is the second observable surface a row landing in the wrong tenant could leak through, mirroring the BE-0431 invariants-file Count-after-rollback rule); (d) the cross-tenant-NotFound-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability test must run for BOTH Get AND Disable (the only two NotFound-emitting paths the repository exposes) — divergence in Code or Hint is an existence leak surface, and the mutation path is a separate code path from the read; afterward, re-read the foreign tenant's row and assert disabled_at == nil so the cross-tenant Disable probe did not silently flip the lifecycle. The List isolation test takes TWO shapes that must both run (mirrors BE-0430): (i) disjoint-fixture count isolation (orgA=1 SA, orgB=3 SAs, gotA must contain only saA's id, gotB must contain exactly {saB1, saB2, saB3} — proves no JOIN scrambled which rows land where); (ii) shared-slug-row isolation (saA and saB both with slug "shared-ci-slug", List(orgA) returns exactly one row with id=saA, List(orgB) returns exactly one row with id=saB — proves a SELECT disambiguating by slug instead of by organization would have either swapped or merged the two rows). One additional CountByOrganization-specific test seeds three tenants (orgA=2, orgB=3, orgC=0 empty bystander) and asserts the three distinct counts; this is the quota-layer leak surface (Count is the read the quota engine consults to enforce per-organization limits, so a leak inflates one tenant's effective quota with another's usage). Document the deliberate ABSENCE of soft-delete tests (service_accounts has no deletion_scheduled_at — only disabled_at, which the byte-identical Disable snapshot treats as the analogue) AND the deliberate ABSENCE of generic-Update / DELETE / cascade tests (Disable is the only mutation; the api_keys.service_account_id cross-tenant FK leak proof lives in serviceaccount_test.go's TestServiceAccountAPIKeyCannotCrossTenantBoundary; the cascade-on-org-delete proof lives in TestServiceAccountBelongsToOneOrganization and TestTenantHierarchyCascadeDelete) in the file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors BE-0428/BE-0430's deliberate-absence note). Note on apierr.NotFound message vs Hint: NotFound("service_account", id) Newf-builds a Message containing the caller-supplied id verbatim, but never sets Hint — so the cross-tenant-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability check MUST compare Code and Hint only (Message would diverge by caller input, which is not a leak). Forward-applicable to BE-0434 (api_keys tenant-isolation: same shape with the same "two tenants share a slug-equivalent" angle — api_keys has UNIQUE (organization_id, prefix), so the shared-prefix fixture is the structural counterpart; plus a secret-bearing extension — the cross-tenant probes must additionally assert no secret_hash needle reaches any observable surface) and BE-0436 (api_key_scopes tenant-isolation).
ralph/progress.txt:288:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, THREE distinct UPDATE paths (UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential), an additional UPDATE-but-not-mutation-state path (TouchLastUsed), and a secret-bearing row body (secret_hash + globally-UNIQUE prefix)** (BE-0433 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go, BE-0429 membership_repository_invariants_test.go, and BE-0431 serviceaccount_repository_invariants_test.go shapes are the templates, but five substitutions are mandatory because api_keys has no version column, three UPDATE paths instead of one, a separate touch-style UPDATE for the auth path, and a secret-bearing row body: (a) the trigger-dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed by the BEFORE UPDATE trigger) must run ONCE PER UPDATE PATH — UpdateMutable, Revoke, RotateCredential, and TouchLastUsed each get their own dedicated dual-anchor test, so a refactor that dropped the trigger from one path but not the others trips a path-specific gate (the BE-0431 single-Disable pattern is too weak here); (b) the UpdateMutable dual-anchor test additionally pins the **mutable surface** to exactly {name, scopes} by snapshotting every non-mutable column (prefix, secret_hash, created_by, service_account_id, expires_at, revoked_at, last_used_at) as byte-identical to the baseline — UpdateMutable is the first repository method in the store package whose SET-clause is a true subset of the table's mutable columns, so the non-mutation assertion is the only structural defense against a future refactor that quietly widened the SET-clause; (c) RotateCredential gets a dedicated test that pins the **credential swap** invariant: prefix and secret_hash flip to the new values, every identity/ownership/scopes/lifecycle column stays byte-identical, the OLD prefix no longer FindByPrefix-resolves (must surface yerr.CodeNotFound — rotation is the only mutation that orphans a previous lookup key), and the NEW prefix re-binds to the SAME key id (rotation is a credential swap, not a re-mint, so audit trails and outstanding access grants keep pointing at the same id); (d) the rollback-leaves-zero-footprint test asserts the standard Get-NotFound + ListByOrganization-empty, AND adds two **secret-bearing redaction probes** specific to api_keys: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys WHERE secret_hash = $1 must return 0 AND SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys WHERE prefix = $1 must return 0 — a SAVEPOINT-or-autonomous-transaction refactor that survived the outer rollback would surface here even when every per-id read reports NotFound, because the credential primitives (the secret hash an attacker would need, and the public prefix that drives the authentication lookup) are the load-bearing observables the table owes its caller; (e) typed-Internal nil-Tx guards run for ALL FIVE mutation paths (Insert / UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential / TouchLastUsed) — the pre-existing TestAPIKeyRepositoryInsertRejectsNilTx in apikey_test.go only asserts err != nil (weaker than the typed-Internal contract every other repository follows), so the typed-code test is additive, not a replacement. Distinct sentinel type name (`apiKeyTxRollbackSentinel`) to avoid the store_test package-level collision with `errSentinel` (user), `membershipTxRollbackSentinel` (membership), and `serviceAccountTxRollbackSentinel` (service account). Local `equalTimePtr(a, b *time.Time) bool` helper is the canonical way to express "lifecycle stamp untouched" concisely without dragging in a third-party deep-equality helper — both-nil OR both-non-nil-and-time.Equal. Forward-applicable to BE-0434 (api_keys tenant isolation: the cross-tenant probes must additionally assert no secret_hash needle reaches any observable surface — same secret-bearing redaction probe pattern documented above for the rollback test, but applied to the cross-tenant-Get / cross-tenant-FindByPrefix / cross-tenant-List read paths and to the typed error surfaces Code/Hint/Message) and to ANY future repository whose row body carries a secret-bearing column (api_key_scopes does NOT — the scope-name column is non-secret — but variables.value when is_secret=true IS, so the BE-0451+ variables invariants stories should reuse this secret-bearing-rollback-probe rule).
ralph/progress.txt:293:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, port-driven-pure-classifier instance (BE-0406 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_import_dry_run_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/migrateimport/import_dry_run_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestImportDryRunCoversCallSites + TestImportDryRunPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Import Dry-Run Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: import dry-run tests (BE-0406)" subsection; TWENTY-EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0405 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestImportDryRun ./...`, CI step name `Import dry-run tests`, verify.sh prefix #30 inserted between BE-0405's #29 and the trailing optionals renumbered #30-#34 → #31-#35 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 34 → # 35` and synthetic-fixture `# 33 → # 34` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST port-driven-pure-classifier instance — the chokepoint is `(*migrateimport.Importer).Plan(ctx, PlanInput) (Plan, error)` which is deterministic but reads from port interfaces (Scanner, Repository) rather than being a package-level pure function: (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH IMPORTER PER ITERATION — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE THE CHOKEPOINT TAKES PORT INTERFACES AT CONSTRUCTION TIME` — unlike BE-0405 (which has no shared receiver because Diff is a package-level function), the import-dry-run chokepoint is a method on Importer whose fake Scanner/Repository are scenario-specific. Each goroutine therefore builds its own (Snapshot, fake Repository, Importer) triple per iteration. The burst's value shifts from "package has no shared state" to "the planner's internal pure logic (classify, pendingOwnerItems, proposeSlug, indexProjectsByID, indexEnvironmentsByID, indexServicesByID) has no package-level shared state" — a regression that smuggled in a cached owner table or a sync.Once mutating a per-action map would still surface as a per-iteration mismatch. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-from-ports classifier (`pkg.New(cfg).Method(args)` style): build a fresh chokepoint per iteration; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's pure helpers have no package-level shared state. (2) `THE DRY-RUN PURITY INVARIANT IS A POSITIVE ASSERTION ON THE FAKE REPOSITORY'S RECORDED CALLS, NOT THE ABSENCE OF AN EFFECT — SCAN repo.calls FOR EVERY CREATE PREFIX AFTER EVERY Plan CALL` — the importDryRunCheckOutcome predicate scans the fakeRepo.calls slice for any string with prefix `CreateProject:`, `CreateEnvironment:`, or `CreateService:` and returns a failure when one is found; pending-owner rows additionally assert len(repo.calls) == 0 (the no-owner short-circuit must not touch the repository at all). A regression that started writing through Plan would fail the prefix-scan on its first ready scenario; a regression that started reading the repository under a pending-owner short-circuit would fail the count assertion on its first pending-owner scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented read-only on some paths and read-write on others: pin the read-only contract as a positive assertion over the fake's call recorder, not as the absence of an observable side-effect (a state diff would silently pass when the planner reads but does not write, which is itself a regression). (3) `THE PER-ROW REDACTION CANARY ASSERTS MARKER-SURVIVAL IN ProposedDisplay AND MARKER-ABSENCE IN STATUS / REASON / LEVEL — NOT IN ProposedSlug, BECAUSE SLUG IS DERIVED FROM DISPLAY` — the value-free classification contract says the importer's classification surface (Reason, Status, slug, display name) is value-free for sensitive content, but ProposedSlug is itself derived from ProposedDisplay via domain.NormalizeSlug and will deliberately carry alphanumeric content from the human-authored name. The canary therefore seeds importsecretmarker into untrusted Dokploy display names and (a) asserts the marker survives in at least one item's ProposedDisplay (operator review surface MUST render the human name), (b) asserts the marker is absent from string(Level), string(Status), and string(Reason) of every item (closed-set tags MUST stay value-free). Asserting on slug too would over-tighten: a slug derived from a name containing the marker WILL carry the marker by design. Watch out for MaxSlugLen=30 truncation: marker-bearing test names MUST stay ≤ 30 chars so the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug matches the planner's normalized slug exactly (a longer name truncates and the dominance test silently flips to StatusReady). Forward-applicable to every verification story whose value-free contract has a deliberate exception field (display, label, raw-name): enumerate the closed-set tag fields the marker MUST be absent from; do not include the deliberate-exception field in the absence predicate; pin marker survival in the exception field too so the canary's positive direction is also pinned. Two store-layer gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) `domain.ID FIXTURES MUST USE CROCKFORD BASE32 LOWERCASE EXCLUDING i, l, o, u` — the importer's fake repository accepts any string but human-facing IDs in test fixtures follow the canonical Crockford suffix; `proj_01hz00000000000000000000dp` and `proj_01hz00000000000000000000ap` are valid (4 + 20 + 2 = 26 chars), while invented IDs like `proj_…colp` or `proj_…linp` are NOT (they contain `l` or `o`). (B) `NormalizeSlug TRUNCATES TO MaxSlugLen=30 AND TRIMS TRAILING HYPHENS` — keep marker-bearing fixture names short enough that their normalized slug equals their raw input verbatim; the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug uses the same shortened name as the snapshot to avoid silent slug-mismatch drift. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:309:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, migrations-from-empty-DB instance (BE-0389 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_migrations_empty_db_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrate_test.go's new canonical pair TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles + TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase + SECURITY.md "## Migration Tests From Empty DB" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: migration tests from empty DB (BE-0389)" subsection; ELEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0388 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...`, CI step name `Migration tests from empty DB`, verify.sh prefix #15). Four BE-0389-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × POSTGRES-REQUIRED DUAL` — unlike every prior verification-suite story whose canonical pair pinned two invariants on the same execution substrate, BE-0389's pair splits across two SUBSTRATES: TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles runs on the embed.FS alone (no Postgres dependency, runs on every developer machine and every CI runner deterministically); TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase requires a real Postgres (creates a throwaway database via crypto/rand-named DSN, runs the embedded ladder, asserts schema_migrations is clean, skips with t.Skip when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset). The deterministic member is the FRONT LINE — it trips on every machine on any closed-set coverage regression (new NNNN_*.up.sql file the loader misses, numbered file renamed to non-numbered form, version gap). The Postgres member is the DEPTH gate — it asserts the runtime contract that no migration can fail on an empty DB. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose contract spans deterministic-fixture-only AND requires-infrastructure invariants: BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety — deterministic Reversible check + Postgres Down test), BE-0392 (load smoke — deterministic harness contract + Postgres load run), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — deterministic injection-point coverage + Postgres reconnect test). The Postgres member MUST use t.Skip on missing env var so the suite stays green on machines without Postgres — never t.Fatal, never silently pass. (2) `CLOSED-SET COVERAGE WALKS embed.FS, NOT RUNTIME.CALLER` — unlike BE-0388 (which read its own source via runtime.Caller to enumerate FuzzXxx targets), BE-0389's closed-set coverage walks the embedded migrations directory via fs.ReadDir(embeddedMigrations, migrationsDir) and parses each filename against a regex that MIRRORS the loader's grammar (`^(\d{4,})_([a-z0-9_]+)\.(up|down)\.sql$`). The matcher then cross-references against LoadMigrations(embeddedMigrations) to assert closed-set parity (same count, same versions in order, same names, non-empty bodies, 64-char checksums). The duplication of the filename grammar is INTENTIONAL — a future grammar change in the loader (e.g. permissive `.sql` form) MUST update both the loader AND the test in the same edit. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose closed-set surface is a directory tree (BE-0390 down-script coverage, BE-0398 tenant-isolation test coverage per repository method, BE-0407 desired-state goldens). (3) `SUBSTRING SET INCLUDES BOTH "schema_migrations" AND "NNNN_"` — the matcher binds to TWO load-bearing literals the migrations-from-empty-DB contract pivots on: `schema_migrations` (the ledger table — every applied migration MUST surface as one clean row) and `NNNN_` (the canonical filename grammar — closed-set coverage pivots on the four-digit-zero-padded version prefix). The substring set also includes `YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL` (the deterministic-skip env var) AND `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` (the shared opt-in-external escape hatch) so the matcher pins BOTH the per-suite skip contract and the cross-suite external contract. Substring count = 17 (matches BE-0388's count). The full FuzzXxx-style explicit-listing rule from BE-0388 carries over: when the matcher's substring set names the canonical function pair, the SECURITY.md section MUST quote each full function name verbatim — TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles AND TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase — so a reader sees the gate from the doc alone. (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — NINE INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#15)` — inserting required step #15 between #14 (fuzz validator tests) and the optionals renumbered 15→16/16→17/17→18/18→19 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The previous count was EIGHT consecutive insertions (BE-0380..BE-0388). Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0388's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 15-17-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline; list canonical function pair verbatim), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. GOTCHA: when the PRD's `-run` filter is NOT already in verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (BE-0389's `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...` was not), the implementation MUST add it via a Node fs round-trip (JSON.parse + JSON.stringify) to preserve 2-space indent + trailing newline; direct text Edit can drift the formatting. Forward-applicable to BE-0390..BE-0399 sibling stories.
ralph/progress.txt:316:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, repository-integration-tests instance (BE-0380 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_repo_integration_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Repository Integration Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: repository integration tests (BE-0380)" subsection; SECOND worked instance of the BE-0379 verification-suite single-file template — applies to verification stories whose subject is a SUBSET of `go test ./...` (a specific package path, a specific `-run` filter) rather than the umbrella suite). EIGHT load-bearing rules: (1) SUBSET-OF-UMBRELLA REDUNDANCY IS DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH, NOT CHURN — when the canonical command (`go test ./internal/controlplane/store/...`) is a strict subset of an already-gated umbrella command (`go test ./...`), the new CI step / verify.sh step / CONTRIBUTING entry are NOT redundant: a future narrowing of the umbrella (e.g. `go test ./internal/...` minus store, a "skip slow tests" flag) would silently de-gate the subject while leaving the umbrella green; the dedicated step still fires AND surfaces the regression as a fast, named, targeted failure rather than buried inside the umbrella log. Cost: one extra ~30s parallel CI step. (2) VERIFY.SH RENUMBERING SAFETY — the verify.sh numeric prefixes 6-9 (optional steps after BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` and the bare `# 5. Required: race detector`) were not pinned by any existing test; inserting a new `# 6. Required: <subject>` between current 5 and current 6 and renumbering optionals 6→7, 7→8, 8→9, 9→10 was a safe edit. BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` pin is preserved verbatim. Future verification-suite subset stories adding a required step should follow the same pattern OR agree on a fixed prefix range like `# 6.X.` to avoid further renumbering as more stories land. (3) PRD HAS TWO COMMAND ARRAYS — verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands (umbrella `go test ./...`, BE-0379) and verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (per-package paths and per-`-run` filters, BE-0380 and later). Subset stories add to requiredBackendCommands; the JSON decode in the test MUST target that field. The two arrays must NEVER be confused — a regression where BE-0380's command lands in requiredLocalCommands instead of requiredBackendCommands would silently weaken the contract because the matcher decodes the wrong field. (4) PREFIX-TOLERANT FUNCTION MATCHER — the `func TestMigrations` matcher is intentionally prefix-tolerant (matches `func TestMigrationsForward` too) because the PRD's `-run TestMigrations` filter is itself a prefix match. Document the gap in a self-check subtest with a stricter fixture (one with NO `func TestMigrations*` declaration at all) so a future maintainer who wants exact-name pinning knows to switch to a regex anchored on `\(`. (5) ELEVEN-SUBSTRING SECURITY SECTION — the section MUST name every load-bearing AC fact: package boundary (`internal/controlplane/store`), determinism (`deterministic fixtures`), isolation (`isolated migrated`), bootstrap function (`TestMigrations`), actionable-failure tokens (BOTH `request_id` AND `resource_id` because HTTP-facing tests use the former and row-level tests use the latter — separating them keeps both contracts visible), opt-in escape hatch (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`), deterministic fixture (`fake Dokploy`), redaction (`redacted`), CI cadence (`Every push and PR`), self-locating gate file name. Each substring is distinct and semantically anchored so Markdown line wrap cannot break the matcher. (6) SELF-CHECK PARITY WITH BE-0379 — each per-surface matcher gets BOTH a "fires on missing" AND "passes on complete" synthetic subtest. The CI workflow self-check uses a fixture containing the umbrella step but NOT the new dedicated step, asserting both halves of the matcher fail; the complete fixture adds the dedicated step and asserts both halves pass. (7) AGENTS.md SUBSECTION MIRRORS THE STATIC TEST — internal/release/AGENTS.md grows a per-story subsection mirroring BE-0379's structure: six-surface bullet list + closing paragraph saying "the matchers fail loudly on drift; the assertion IS the contract". The subsection heading is the story title verbatim so `grep "Verification suite:"` finds every instance. (8) EDIT-HOOK RETRY on .github/workflows/*.yml — the workflow security reminder hook may surface as an "error" on first attempt to a non-trivial workflow edit; the second identical Edit succeeds (the hook is informational, not blocking). Plan for one retry when editing workflow YAML. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests, canonical `go test -run TestOpenAPI ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`), BE-0382 (OpenAPI schema conformance, `TestOpenAPI`), BE-0383 (policy matrix, `TestPolicyMatrix`), BE-0384 (quota concurrency, `TestQuotaConcurrency`), BE-0385 (job worker lease), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract, `TestFakeDokploy`), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety), BE-0391 (race detector — though this one is itself an umbrella story, may use BE-0379's template instead of BE-0380's), BE-0392..BE-0408. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (or fixed-range agreement), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 8-11 substring set, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry, (f) bootstrap test function name (TestMigrations for BE-0380, TestOpenAPI for BE-0382, TestPolicyMatrix for BE-0383). Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:318:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, unit-tests-for-every-package instance (BE-0379 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_unit_tests_static_test.go + internal/credentials/keyring_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Unit Test Suite" section + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: unit tests for every package (BE-0379)" subsection; FIRST worked instance of the verification-suite single-file template called out in the BE-0354 dependency_scan pattern's closing line — applies to verification stories whose subject is the suite itself rather than a specific security control). NINE load-bearing rules: (1) SINGLE FILE pins ALL surfaces of the meta-contract so a future contributor changing the CI step name, the verify.sh step header, the CONTRIBUTING numeric prefix, the SECURITY row, or the PRD verification-loop array fails ONE test, not five. The single-file shape is the BE-0354 dependency_scan pattern carried forward to the verification-suite family. (2) CLOSED EXEMPT SET WITH RATIONALE — every exemption in allowedPackagesWithoutUnitTests MUST carry a rationale that points to where coverage actually lives, not just a "thin entrypoint" hand-wave. The rationale is what makes a future review decide whether to keep, extend, or remove the entry; without it the set drifts. Current exempt set: cmd/yalla (Cobra entrypoint, behaviour in internal/cli), cmd/yalla-api (process wiring, behaviour in internal/controlplane/*), cmd/yalla-worker (loop wiring, behaviour in internal/controlplane/worker), internal/controlplane/jobs (doc.go placeholder). (3) STALE-EXEMPTION GUARD (TestVerificationSuiteExemptionSetIsTight) — assert every exempt dir (a) exists and (b) still lacks tests. Otherwise a package that gains tests silently keeps its exemption and the discipline rots. (4) IGNORED-PREFIX WALKER — the package coverage walker MUST skip `.git/`, `node_modules/`, `dist/`, `outputs/`, `tmp/`, `bin/`, `vendor/` via prefix-with-trailing-slash check (the trailing slash is what prevents a hypothetical `output/` package from being accidentally skipped). (5) PURE FUNCTION SPLIT — findPackagesMissingTests takes (root, exempt, ignored) and is called once by the runtime test against the real repo AND multiple times by the self-check with synthetic trees built via t.TempDir + mkFile helper. The self-check is what proves the walker doesn't false-positive on test-only dirs and doesn't false-negative on ignored subtrees. (6) CASE-SENSITIVE SUBSTRING CONSTANTS — `requiredSecuritySectionSubstrings` are matched verbatim against the doc, so a substring like "every Go package" will NOT match a sentence-start "Every Go package". When the doc text naturally starts a sentence with the substring, use the capitalised form in the constant. The first run caught this: the test failed with `missing required substring "every Go package"`; the fix aligned the constant with the doc, not the doc with the constant. The self-check fixture (synthetic SECURITY.md inside TestVerificationSuiteUnitTestsAnalyzerDetectsRegressions) must be updated in the same edit — if the synthetic fixture starts to fail, the test diagnostic explicitly says "fix the fixture, not the test" to prevent the wrong direction of fix. (7) NON-MAIN, NON-DOC-ONLY PACKAGE WITHOUT TESTS IS NEVER EXEMPT-WORTHY — prefer adding 2-3 small tests (e.g. internal/credentials IsNotFound + IsUnsupported + Store-interface compile-time pin) over expanding the exemption set. The exemption set is reserved for thin main-glue and `doc.go`-only placeholders. Adding the keyring_test.go tests took ~70 lines and was strictly preferable to a `internal/credentials` exemption entry, because the package has real classification logic (errors.Is over upstream keyring sentinels) that a future upstream rename could silently break. (8) PRD AS A VERIFICATION SURFACE — the prd.json's `verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands` array is itself part of the contract. An AI agent reads the PRD before picking up a story; pinning the suite command there guarantees the agent sees it without needing to discover CI workflows or shell scripts. The matcher uses encoding/json with a partial struct (`{VerificationLoop struct{RequiredLocalCommands []string `json:"requiredLocalCommands"`}}`) so a future PRD schema extension doesn't break it. (9) CI MATRIX AS PART OF THE CONTRACT — pinning ubuntu-latest + macos-latest + windows-latest is what prevents a silent "we only test on Linux now" regression. The matrix dimension is a separate substring assertion per host so the failure diagnostic names the missing host explicitly. Forward-applicable to BE-0380 (repository integration tests — replace findPackagesMissingTests with findStoreTestsMissingTenantIsolation), BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests — replace with findHandlersMissingContractTest), BE-0382 (OpenAPI conformance — replace with findEndpointsMissingOpenAPI), BE-0383 (policy matrix), BE-0384 (quota concurrency), BE-0385 (job worker leases), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade), BE-0391 (race detector), and every future P=1 verification-suite story. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical suite command (e.g. `go test -run TestMigrations ./...`), (b) scope predicate (which packages/files the matcher walks), (c) doc heading constant, (d) substring-set capturing the ACs of that story, (e) per-story exemption set if any. The pattern is the next-strongest form of the BE-0354 (dependency_scan) single-file multi-surface pattern, applied to a meta-contract whose subject is the test suite itself rather than a specific security control. The two pattern families share: (i) single file, (ii) closed substring/constant set per surface, (iii) self-check with synthetic fixtures driving every matcher. They differ in: (i) BE-0379-family stories have a `findPackagesMissingTests`-style repo walker as their primary matcher (the security-control family has no such walker; their primary matcher is an AST scan over package source); (ii) BE-0379-family stories pin the PRD itself (security-control stories generally don't); (iii) BE-0379-family stories use a closed exempt set with per-entry rationale (security-control stories use closed constant lists like `forbiddenSecretEnvFragments` but no per-entry rationale).
ralph/progress.txt:323:- What was implemented: added versioned backoffice overage policies with global, plan, and organization scopes; added the `0077_admin_overage_policies` migration; added audited store repository/service support; added thin HTTP API routes for all three scopes with OpenAPI route metadata and stable envelopes; wired the API binary; updated usage-counter aggregation to resolve published overage policy before falling back to legacy entitlement metadata so billing export snapshots inherit the same frozen decision.
ralph/progress.txt:378:- Container image hardening static-pin pattern (BE-0355, internal/release/container_hardening_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "Container Image Hardening" section + internal/release/AGENTS.md "Container image hardening" subsection; structural template for ANY future security-verification story whose invariant lives in a non-Go declarative file — Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml, .github/workflows/*.yml, .goreleaser.yaml, Kubernetes manifests, OCI runtime spec). Single-test variant of the BE-0344 two-test pattern: the "runtime" half is implicit because the build/CI pipeline IS the runtime — there is no application-code call site to drive a parallel runtime test against, so the static analyser plus the self-check IS the load-bearing defence. Six load-bearing rules: (1) The parser lives in the test file itself, not in a separate package — keeps the production package API surface clean (only the SupportedTargets matrix in internal/release) and lets future hardening stories add their own parsers without coupling. The Dockerfile parser scope is intentionally narrow: join backslash continuations into logical instructions, resolve global ARG defaults so `FROM ${RUNTIME_IMAGE}` expands to the canonical value, group instructions under their owning FROM stage. Heredocs, `--mount=type=cache` flags, and ONBUILD are deliberately opaque — the matchers work above the parser's awareness of them. (2) Every invariant gets its own matcher function plus its own in-repo test plus its own self-check sub-test. The matcher takes the parsed model and returns a self-describing error; the in-repo test runs the matcher against the real Dockerfile/compose file; the self-check sub-test drives the matcher with a synthetic known-good baseline (a `minimalCanonicalDockerfile()` helper) AND a series of mutations each isolating one regression. (3) Required-value constants live at the top of the test file: `requiredRuntimeBaseImage = "gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot"`, `requiredNonrootUser = "nonroot:nonroot"`, `requiredCopyChown = "--chown=nonroot:nonroot"`, `requiredBuilderBuildFlags = {CGO_ENABLED=0, -trimpath, -s -w}`. Closed sets for forbidden patterns: `forbiddenSecretEnvFragments = {TOKEN, PASSWORD, SECRET, API_KEY, PRIVATE_KEY, SIGNING_KEY, DSN}` (case-insensitive substring on ENV key), `forbiddenRuntimePackageInstalls = {apt-get, apt install, yum install, dnf install, apk add, microdnf install}` (substring on runtime stage's joined RUN payload). The closed-set design is deliberate — it catches the secret-shaped fragments the project's config layer actually uses, not every possible English word that contains "key", so a future ENV that holds a non-secret hint does not false-positive. (4) SECURITY.md is the human-facing co-contract: the same test file pins (a) a verification-gate row in the Required Verification Gates table AND (b) a dedicated `## Container Image Hardening` section with literal substrings (`distroless`, `nonroot`, `Multi-stage build`, `CGO_ENABLED=0`, `-trimpath`, `Container image hardening`). A regression that drops either is caught as deterministically as a Dockerfile regression. (5) The postgres image pin regex `^postgres:\d+$` deliberately rejects variant tags like `postgres:16-bookworm` because the local integration-test stack must remain reproducible across local environments; if a future story needs a variant the regex must widen in lockstep with the fixture catalogue. (6) Builder stage vs runtime stage have ASYMMETRIC rules: builder stages may install packages (`apt-get install git` for the goreleaser-style git tag lookup is legitimate), the runtime stage may not. The matcher pins the asymmetry by walking only the runtime stage's instructions for the package-install scan and only the builder stage's instructions for the build-flag scan. The runtime stage is identified as the LAST stage (the unnamed final FROM); the builder stage as the FIRST stage with an `AS` name. Five test-fixture gotchas: (a) when SECURITY.md and the matcher disagree on case (`multi-stage` vs `Multi-stage`), the test pins the EXACT casing the documentation file uses — Go's `strings.Contains` is case-sensitive, and pinning the documentation casing makes the matcher serve as a stability anchor for the published policy. (b) Self-check cases that mutate ONE invariant on top of a shared canonical baseline are independent by construction — a future widening of matcher M cannot accidentally pass a fixture that was constructed to test matcher N. (c) The runtime stage's `COPY --from=builder` matcher MUST scope the `--chown=` requirement to COPY instructions that ALSO carry `--from=` — a plain `COPY ./foo /etc/foo` from the build context legitimately does not need the flag because the build context's owner is the current user. (d) The `ADD <url>` matcher uses a regex anchored at start-of-args that tolerates leading `--chown=` style flags; a future addition that allows local-path `ADD` would fail this assertion only on URL shapes. (e) The Dockerfile parser silently ignores instructions that appear before the FIRST FROM (other than ARG, which becomes a global ARG default). This is the Dockerfile spec behaviour; the test relies on it so `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7` parser directives and pre-FROM comments don't accidentally count as instructions in any stage. NOT applicable to verification stories whose invariant is a runtime contract WITH an application-code call site (request body size, JSON parser hardening, log redaction fuzzing) — those follow the BE-0344 two-test (static + runtime) shape because the runtime test has a real handler/repository call site to drive.
ralph/progress.txt:379:- Tamper-resistance three-wall verification pattern (BE-0353, internal/controlplane/store/{audit_tamper_static_test.go, audit_test.go::TestAuditUpdateBlockedByDatabaseTriggerWithRestrictViolation} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/audit_immutability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/AGENTS.md "Tamper resistance" subsection; structural template for any future append-only-table story whose immutability is enforced at the database trigger AND the Go repository surface — security events, key-rotation history, billing-ledger entries, fraud alerts, anything where "the record cannot change after write" is a load-bearing contract). The pattern is THREE static-time guards plus ONE runtime probe so a weakening of any one layer is caught by another. Five load-bearing rules: (1) MIGRATION-shape pin (parse the .up.sql via embedded fs and assert four regex shapes: function declared with `ERRCODE = '<sqlstate-name>'`, trigger declared with `BEFORE UPDATE ON <table> FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION <fn>`, the `CREATE TABLE` parenthesised body extracted and scanned for any forbidden column declaration, the `.down.sql` drops both the table AND the function — DROP TABLE alone leaves the function orbiting unused). The column-scan strips `--`-prefixed lines BEFORE matching so an in-body comment like `-- intentionally no updated_at column` does not trip the probe; the matcher anchors on `^(?:\s|,)*<column>\s+[a-zA-Z]` so a bare token mentioned in prose elsewhere does not match. (2) Go-SURFACE pin (parse the implementation .go via the existing mustParseFile helper, walk file.Decls, collect every FuncDecl whose receiver is the named type, and assert the set is exactly the allowed names AND each name does not start with a known mutation-verb prefix `{Update,Delete,Patch,Replace,Modify,Truncate,Remove}` — the verb prefix is a SECOND filter so a future allowed-set drift that re-admits a mutation-shape name still fails); also pin the struct declaration has no mutation-timestamp field `{UpdatedAt,ModifiedAt,RevisedAt,LastModifiedAt}` — the SQL column absence is the migration's job, the Go field absence is the surface's job, both must hold. (3) DML-SCAN pin (walk every non-test `.go` in the owning package, find every `*ast.BasicLit` of `token.STRING` kind, unwrap raw-string and double-quoted shapes, normalise whitespace and case, and reject any literal that contains a forbidden DML substring against the target table; only the carved-out INSERT shape inside the repository's own file is allowed — every other shape is a regression flagged with `file:line: forbidden DML against <table>: "UPDATE <table>" in literal`). The case-insensitive uppercase normalisation handles lowercase shapes that snuck in via copy-paste; the whitespace normalisation handles multi-line literals like `UPDATE\n  audit_events\nSET ...`. Comments are NOT scanned — the rule targets executable code paths and a SQL example in a doc comment is fine. (4) RUNTIME-wire pin (insert a fixture row, attempt every UPDATE shape an operator might try — by id, by tenant id, table-wide without WHERE, partial-column-rewrite, verdict-reclassification — and assert each rejection surfaces a `*pgconn.PgError` with `pgErr.Code == "<sqlstate>"` AND `strings.Contains(pgErr.Message, "<canonical phrase>")` AND the row's columns are byte-identical to the original after the attempted UPDATE; PG's `restrict_violation` is SQLSTATE 23001 NOT 38000 — 38000 is external_routine_exception, an easy typo because both classes start with a 3, verify the actual wire code by running once and reading the failure). Also assert the error message does NOT echo any caller input — the trigger function is value-free by design and a future log path that interpolates the rejected payload would surface here as a redaction regression. (5) SELF-CHECK pin (drive each matcher with synthetic known-good AND known-bad inputs in subtests so a future over-tightening that false-positives on real production code AND a future under-tightening that misses a real regression are BOTH caught — the self-check is the only safety net against "I weakened my own analyser without noticing"). Six asymmetries worth knowing: (a) `audit_events` allows direct SQL `DELETE FROM ...` because the design requires ON DELETE CASCADE to work for tenant teardown — so the DML-scan flags DELETE FROM at the Go layer (no app-side delete) but the runtime test does NOT assert DELETE is blocked at the DB layer (it isn't). The migration AGENTS.md section documents this asymmetry so a future reader doesn't try to "fix" it. (b) The wire SQLSTATE for the migration's `RAISE EXCEPTION ... ERRCODE = 'restrict_violation'` is 23001 (class 23 integrity_constraint_violation) — PG remaps the symbolic name to a numeric code at trigger compile time, the SQL pins the name, the runtime test pins the code, both must stay synchronised. (c) Mutation-verb prefix detection deliberately EXCLUDES `Set` and `Get` — too broad to flag without false positives across unrelated repositories — and the closed verb set lives in `isMutationSurfaceMethodName` as a helper rather than inline so adding a new verb is a single change. (d) The struct-field forbidden list does NOT include `UpdatedBy` / `LastEditor` — those are actor-attribution fields, not mutation timestamps; the rule targets writes-to-the-record, not metadata about who touched it. (e) The migration column-scan strips `--` line comments before matching but does NOT strip `/* */` block comments — PG accepts both, but `audit_events.up.sql` uses `--` exclusively and the simpler scanner is enough; if a future migration mixes styles, extend the scanner with the same upper-vs-lower test pattern. (f) The runtime test reuses the existing `auditEventFixture` / `seedOrg` / `appendAudit` helpers from `audit_test.go` so the new test is ~150 lines of mostly cases-and-assertions; the wire-error assertions live in a `*pgconn.PgError` `errors.As` extraction (NOT a string match on err.Error()) so a future error-wrapping change at the pgx layer that preserves the typed error still surfaces the assertion.
ralph/progress.txt:397:- Cross-cutting capability package + at-rest encryption seam (BE-0339 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{doc.go, provider.go, aesgcm.go, plaintext.go, *_test.go, AGENTS.md} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0029_organization_variables_encrypted_secrets.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/organization_variable.go + organization_variable_service.go + organization_variable_secrets_at_rest_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go buildSecretsProvider; structural template for BE-0340..0342 sibling at-rest stories (project / environment / service variable encryption-at-rest) and any future cross-cutting source-of-truth seam over a same-shape resource family — webhook signing secrets, deployment-config secrets, backup-target credentials, etc.). Six load-bearing rules for the cross-cutting package: (1) Land the package ONCE under internal/controlplane/<pkg>, with the Provider interface + a production implementation (AESGCM) + a tests-only passthrough implementation (Plaintext, restricted to local/test profiles by cmd/yalla-api) + a package AGENTS.md that documents BOTH the runtime contract AND the forward-applicable storage pattern (column shape, CHECK constraint shape, repository tuple shape, service-layer seam location). (2) Typed value-free errors (ErrInvalidCiphertext / ErrUnknownKey / ErrUnsupportedProvider) — the secrets package contract never echoes any of {plaintext, ciphertext bytes, key material, key id, nonce} into an error message; the only observable on a failure is the classification. (3) AES-256-GCM with per-Seal random crypto/rand nonce + AEAD-authenticated decryption + multi-key rotation pool (first key is active for Seal, all keys accepted for Open). KeyID derivation = first 8 bytes of SHA-256(key) hex-encoded (16 chars): deterministic across processes, non-secret (one-way hash), short enough for audit metadata. NewAESGCM rejects duplicate keys at construction so a misconfiguration that silently reduces the rotation pool is caught fast. (4) cmd/yalla-api wires the provider in ONE buildSecretsProvider helper near runStartupChecks, NOT one per scope. Strict profiles (staging/production) refuse to start without YALLA_SECRET_KEYS (config.requireStrictFields enforces) via an explicit sentinel error; permissive profiles (local/test) fall back to Plaintext with a Warn log line. (5) The interface is INTENTIONALLY same-width every scope needs (org/project/env/service variables), so a single provider configured at startup covers every variables table — sibling at-rest stories become mechanical clones of schema + service + test changes. (6) For internal-only read paths that legitimately need the plaintext (the future Dokploy renderer worker, BE-0343 variable-inheritance resolver), Open the sealed tuple through the same Provider passed into the service — NEVER a wire-facing endpoint. Six load-bearing rules for the storage shape per table: (1) Add three nullable columns (secret_provider text NULL, secret_key_id text NULL, secret_ciphertext bytea NULL) ALONGSIDE the existing plain `value` column, never instead of it; force value='' for secret rows and cipher columns NULL for non-secret rows via ONE CHECK constraint named <table>_secret_columns_consistent that pins the invariant in both directions. The CHECK is the defence-in-depth backstop the service relies on — a bug that tries to write a plaintext secret rolls back as deterministic apierr.Conflict rather than silent corruption. (2) Mirror DO $$ guards in BOTH the up and the down migration that REFUSE to run while is_secret=true rows exist with the wrong shape — a re-application against a database that accumulated plaintext secrets fails loudly rather than silently leaving them behind, and a rollback against a database with sealed rows fails loudly rather than silently losing every secret value. (3) Add a partial index on (secret_provider, secret_key_id) WHERE is_secret=true so the future key-rotation worker (BE-0351) can locate rows referencing a retired key without a full-table scan. (4) The repository's Upsert + UpdateMutable signatures take a closed-set Input tuple (<Table>VariableUpsert with Value/IsSecret/SecretProvider/SecretKeyID/SecretCiphertext) — keeps the signature narrow as new secret fields land and centralises the "nullable columns when is_secret=false" projection in one helper (nullableSecretColumns). (5) The OrganizationVariable struct (and siblings) gain the three encryption-at-rest fields as INTERNAL-only — never on the wire — and the LogValue redaction MUST be updated in the SAME edit to redact BOTH the plaintext AND the new ciphertext to output.Sentinel; an unstale slog record that captures the struct cannot leak either. (6) scanRow projection routes through sql.NullString for the nullable text columns (pgx-scan into *string rejects NULL). Five load-bearing rules for the service-layer seam: (1) Seal at the SERVICE layer, never the repository — Seal is a side-effecting call that depends on a runtime Provider not a *Tx, calling Seal BEFORE opening the transaction shortens the SQL critical section, and the repository stays pure SQL. The cleanest helper is a one-line projector (sealVariableValue) that takes the customer's input shape (<Table>VariableReplace) and returns the repository's tuple shape (<Table>VariableUpsert) — non-secret items pass through untouched, secret items go through Provider.Seal. (2) The Service's constructor (NewOrganizationVariableService) takes Provider as a REQUIRED dependency rejected at construction if nil — a production process cannot accidentally start with the at-rest seam disabled. (3) Patch path for sealed rows has THREE post-state cases the service must enumerate explicitly: (a) caller supplied a new value → use it verbatim; (b) caller supplied only is_secret AND the current row is_secret → Open the current ciphertext via Provider.Open to recover the plaintext, then re-Seal under the active key (the only correct way to re-key a secret without surfacing the plaintext to the caller); (c) otherwise → use the current row's `value` column. Skipping case (b) would either lose the secret bytes on a re-seal or write a "previously-sealed plaintext" string into the value column. (4) The demotion path (is_secret=true→false) reuses case (b) to recover the plaintext, then writes it back to the value column with NULL encryption columns — the ONLY customer path that surfaces a previously-sealed plaintext, and only as a deliberate operator action. (5) Seal failures surface as apierr.Internal with a value-free wrapped cause — a sealing error is a server-side problem, never customer input, and the wrap never echoes the plaintext. Six load-bearing rules for the integration-test surface: (1) The integration test file lives next to the existing repository tests as <table>_secrets_at_rest_test.go, uses testutil.RequireMigratedDB (skips cleanly when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset so go test ./... stays green without Postgres), and wires a REAL AES-256-GCM provider (mustAESGCM helper that mints a fresh 32-byte key per test so parallel tests cannot bleed key ids across each other). (2) Five pinned tests per scope: SealsSecretValuesAtRest (the load-bearing contract — secret value reaches Postgres ONLY as AES-GCM ciphertext under the active key id, value column is "", on-disk bytea does not contain the plaintext substring, ciphertext round-trips through Provider.Open, audit row never carries any of {plaintext, key name, hex ciphertext, key id, provider id}); PatchRotatesCiphertextOnEveryWrite (a patch that flips is_secret only on a sealed row Opens-then-Seals under a fresh nonce so on-disk bytes change between writes); PatchDemotesSecretToPlaintext (is_secret=true→false with no new value recovers the plaintext via Open and writes to the value column with NULL encryption columns); SchemaRejectsPlaintextSecretWrite (three direct INSERT attempts that would land a plaintext-secret row each surface a constraint violation whose error message names the constraint); LogValueRedactsCiphertextAndPlaintext (a structured log record that captures the variable struct redacts BOTH plaintext AND ciphertext to output.Sentinel while exposing provider id + key id). (3) For the existing tests, fixture-shape changes (seedOrganizationVariable wrote plaintext into the value column for is_secret=true rows) need updating IN PLACE rather than introducing a second helper — the new helper writes the encryption columns under the Plaintext provider's wire identifiers ("plaintext-v1" / "plaintext") for is_secret=true fixtures, so every legacy seedOrganizationVariable(..., true) call site keeps working. (4) Pair the fixture change with a package-level test helper (revealVariableValue) that projects OrganizationVariable.Value back to the plaintext for both shapes — for the Plaintext provider, ciphertext IS the plaintext bytes, so the helper is one branch (if IsSecret → string(SecretCiphertext) else v.Value). Tests that previously asserted v.Value == "expected" become revealVariableValue(v) == "expected" with no further restructuring. (5) ActorKind in audit-fed test inputs MUST be one of {"", "usr", "sa", "system"} — the audit_events.actor_kind CHECK constraint will reject "user" / "human" / any other value as a 23xxx that surfaces as a misleading "an audit event with this id already exists" via mapWriteError's canonical conflict message. The actual diagnostic is the actor_kind CHECK, not duplicate id. (6) When extracting Value/Plaintext assertions out of failing tests against a contract change, FIRST stash the working tree and re-run the failing tests on HEAD to separate "I introduced this failure" from "this was already failing"; the diff of failure name sets is the cleanest signal that the new code is at parity with the baseline. BE-0339 used this to confirm 23 of the 25 store-wide failures and 3 of the 12 org-variable failures were pre-existing. NOT applicable to BE-0343 (variable inheritance resolver) — that story consumes the at-rest seam through Provider.Open against multiple scopes simultaneously and needs its own pattern around the merge order + per-scope Open call site. NOT applicable to BE-0351 (secret encryption key rotation) — that story consumes the partial index organization_variables_secret_routing_idx + the KeyIDs() / ActiveKeyID() surface to enumerate rows referencing a retired key and re-Seal them, but the rotation worker is a separate operational concern from the at-rest write path this pattern owns.
ralph/progress.txt:402:- Real-quota concurrent oversubscription integration test (BE-0323 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_quota_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0324..0338 — every other quota-dimension PRD story that needs to prove "parallel requests cannot oversubscribe the quota" against the dimension's owning Service.Create call): one Go test, t.Parallel, against testutil.RequireMigratedDB; wires a REAL *quota.Checker over a REAL *store.QuotaRepository + quota.StaticPlanResolver, and a REAL *store.<Resource>Service + REAL *store.AuditRepository, with the only fakes being the Authorizer and JobEnqueuer (recordingAuthorizer / recordingJobs from projectservice_test.go) which always succeed because their failure-path stories are covered elsewhere. Six load-bearing rules: (1) pre-seed quota_usage with used_value=0 for (org, dimension) BEFORE the race so the SELECT FOR UPDATE inside Checker.LockUsage acquires an existing row rather than racing inserts — the lock contract under test is row-lock serialisation, not row-creation; (2) seed quota_policies as scope_kind='plan_default' (not 'organization' override) so the plan-default fallback path inside QuotaRepository.EffectiveLimit is the one exercised — the org-override path has its own dedicated repository test (TestQuotaRepositoryEffectiveLimitPrefersOrgOverride); (3) launch attempts > limit goroutines (BE-0323 uses 20 attempts / limit 5 — 4:1 ratio gives a comfortable margin against any future pool-size or scheduling change without inflating runtime past ~0.5s); each goroutine generates its OWN canonical resource id via domain.MustNewID + a unique canonical slug fmt.Sprintf("p-%02d", i) so a slug conflict cannot disguise itself as a quota rejection; (4) assert successes == limit AND rejections == attempts - limit exactly, NOT >= / <= — the lock contract guarantees an exact split, and a "successes >= limit / <= attempts" range would silently let a regression through; (5) for EVERY rejection, assert all five ExceededDetail fields (Resource, Limit, Requested=1, Reserved==limit, Current==0) because at the moment the lock yields to a losing transaction every winning reservation is already committed — anything less than Reserved==limit means the lock did not serialise — and assert the diagnostic string never echoes any caller input (the i-indexed slug, display name, request id, or correlation id) so a future logging path cannot turn the error into a content channel; (6) post-race assertions cover three independent surfaces of the same invariant: raw count of projects rows for the org (must equal limit), raw count of active quota_reservations rows for the dimension (must equal limit), AND quotaRepo.SumActiveReservations through Store.Read (must equal limit) — the third surface catches a future regression where the table content diverges from the repository's view. Fake-call assertions pin step order at the unit-of-work level: authz.calls == attempts (every transaction reaches authorization) but jobs.calls == limit (rejected transactions never reach the JobEnqueuer because quota fails first and rolls back the whole tx). The dimension constant comes from the public store.QuotaResource* surface (BE-0323 uses store.QuotaResourceProjects) so a future rename surfaces as a compile error rather than a flaky test. NOT applicable to quota dimensions whose enforcement seam lives outside the *Service.Create unit of work (e.g. cpu_millicores / memory_mb / storage_gb where the dimension is summed across child resources — those need a different shape that exercises the dimension's actual enforcement gate). The CapDeploy concurrent_deployments dimension is the load-bearing precedent for "summed across rows" enforcement (DeploymentService.Create at deployment_service.go:209+691) and would clone this template by swapping the Service + dimension while keeping the six rules intact.
ralph/progress.txt:411:- Repository tenant-isolation for a tenant-root table (BE-0426 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/organization_tenant_isolation_test.go; structural template for BE-0428/0430/0432/0434/0436 and every future tenant-root-table isolation story): the strongest isolation guarantee in the codebase lives on the row-id-keyed tenant root (organizations) and the proof shape is "byte-identical snapshot of the OTHER tenant across every mutation path the repository exposes". Skeleton: one Go test file per table, one t.Parallel test per (mutation × leg) — Get returns-only-named-tenant + Get unknown-id-is-NotFound (cross-tenant + missing render the same yerr.CodeNotFound — no oracle); Update only-touches-named-tenant (snapshot orgB, Update orgA, re-read orgB, assert byte-identical across slug, display_name, version, updated_at, deletion_scheduled_at; assert orgA's update DID land so a no-op repository can't pass trivially); Update-with-mismatched-If-Match also-does-not-touch-orgB (catches "fall back to unconditional UPDATE on stale" regressions); ScheduleDeletion only-touches-named-tenant (stamp on orgA, orgB byte-identical incl. DeletionScheduledAt == nil); Get-of-soft-deleted-tenant-does-not-mask-other (soft-delete is per-row, not SQL-level hide; the scheduled row is still readable and orgB unchanged); Insert enforces-global-slug-uniqueness (typed yerr.CodeConflict + non-empty user-facing Message; first row's slug unchanged; second row's id is yerr.CodeNotFound on follow-up Get — the failed Insert lands no row). Three load-bearing rules: (1) anchor on BOTH version (trigger-bumped on every UPDATE so a missing WHERE bumps every row) AND updated_at (trigger-refreshed; same property) — either one catches a WHERE-less UPDATE independently of the other, keep both; (2) the "stale-If-Match" leg is structurally distinct from the "successful update" leg and exercises the conflict-detection path which historically falls back to extra reads/writes that could leak across tenants; (3) Insert conflict-message assertions anchor on yerr.CodeConflict + non-empty Message ONLY, never on a substring (the static "an organization with this slug already exists" message is a stable contract but story-specific prose checks are brittle). Tenant-root tables have NO ListByParent method at this layer (no parent to scope by) — the "list queries don't leak total counts" acceptance criterion is vacuously satisfied for tenant-root tables; the corresponding proof for /v1/me/organizations lives in membership tests. Document this absence in the test file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test. CHILD-TABLE variant (BE-0428+ for users/memberships/projects/environments/services/api_keys/etc.) extends this skeleton with one additional leg: a cross-tenant parent_id probe — passing org A's parent_id (project_id, etc.) alongside org B's organization_id must render NotFound, NEVER A's row, NEVER a 500 leaking the cause. Use docker compose up -d postgres + YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://yalla:yalla@127.0.0.1:5432/yalla go test -race -count=1 -run 'TestXxxRepository' to verify; the suite skips cleanly when the env var is unset, so CI without Postgres remains green. Known pre-existing -race-with-DB failures in environment-service / api-key-service Create paths and organization_variable_patch audit metadata are NOT introduced by this pattern (confirmed by stashing the new test file and re-running each failing case); a focused triage story should pick them up before BE-0500+ verification stories run.
ralph/progress.txt:413:- Cross-cutting policy package = retention-shaped leaf (BE-0038 pattern, internal/controlplane/retention/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, policy.go, policy_test.go}, third instance of the pure-package layout after reconcile and migrateimport): canonical shape for ANY cross-cutting authority over a closed-set domain enum + a validated config + boundary-style predicates — retention windows, rate-limit tiers, idempotency horizons, audit-redaction policies, plan-limit ladders, etc. Layout: doc.go (boundary + the invariants the predicates encode), policy.go (closed-set ResourceKind + a Window struct of just-durations + an immutable Policy value with a private map; DefaultPolicy() returning the production values + NewPolicy(map) that validates every kind is present and every Window passes validate() + copies the input map so caller mutation cannot tamper with a live Policy + Window/Restorable/RestorableAt/RestoreDeadline/RemainingRestore/ReapableAt/ReapDeadline/AllowsSlugReuse predicates over (kind, stampedAt, now)), policy_test.go (every kind × every transition + boundary case + zero-value-Policy safety + NewPolicy validation table + caller-mutation defense + cross-predicate coherence (restorable → held → reapable monotone) + closed-set guard via AllKinds()), AGENTS.md (boundary + invariants). Three load-bearing rules: (1) the boundary comparisons MUST be strict-less-than at the upper bound of RestorableAt and `>=` at the lower bound of ReapableAt so the two predicates are mutually exclusive at the exact instant of transition (a row never reports as both restorable and reapable at the same nanosecond); (2) the package is a LEAF — it imports only std-lib (time, fmt, sort, errors-only-from-tests) and is imported by store/httpapi/worker, so its predicates are cheap to call from anywhere without dragging a database or network dependency along; never let retention import apierr/store/audit; (3) "soft delete where safe" in the BE-0038 description is a real qualifier — memberships do not get a soft-delete column because role_version invalidates sessions at the auth layer and audit_events outlives the resource via the organization-level cascade, so KindMembership.SoftDeleteColumn() returns "" and Policy.SupportsSoftDelete(KindMembership) is false by design (a future schema migration that adds a removed_at column to memberships must update both in lock-step, and the closed-set test will fail until they do). Two follow-up wirings the package was designed for but does not yet require: a future teardown worker (BE-0203+) iterates retention.AllKinds(), looks up each kind's SoftDeleteColumn(), scans rows where the column is non-NULL and the stamp is older than ReapAfter, and calls the kind-specific hard-delete repository method; a future CapAdmin admin-purge endpoint calls retention.Policy.AllowsSlugReuse before bypassing the wait — the slug-reuse predicate is the contract those two consumers will share. Forward-applicable to every future cross-cutting policy module (plan tiers, audit redaction horizons, idempotency-key TTL, rate-limit ramp).
ralph/progress.txt:414:- Pure orchestrator / workflow package = reconcile-shaped vertical (BE-0033 pattern, internal/controlplane/migrateimport/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, types.go, importer.go, importer_test.go}, the second instance of this shape after internal/controlplane/reconcile): canonical shape for ANY workflow that ingests external state, classifies it, and writes through ports — Dokploy import, drift reconciliation, billing reconciliation, compliance scanning, lifecycle teardown, etc. Package layout: doc.go (boundary + plan/apply + dry-run + redaction sections), AGENTS.md (conventions), types.go (every port interface PLUS every value type those interfaces reference — Scanner/Repository ports, Snapshot/Existing*/Create*Input value types, OwnerAssignment, PlanItem and Plan with stable JSON tags because the Plan IS the dry-run yalla.output.v1 payload; ItemStatus + ItemReason as the closed classification surface), importer.go (Config with Scanner + Repository + Logger + Now + Redactor; New() returns apierr.Internal on a missing port; Plan(ctx, in) is pure and never writes; Apply(ctx, plan) is best-effort and collects per-item failures in Result.Failures with redacted error strings; classify*() helpers are unit-testable in isolation; wrap{Scanner,Repository}Err() preserves catalogued apierr.* codes and upgrades untyped errors to apierr.DokployUnavailable (scanner) / apierr.StoreUnavailable (repository) / apierr.Internal (defensive contract assertions); a resolvedParents map seeded with the org-root entry lets the apply loop resolve child-after-parent in a single pass through the hierarchy-ordered items). The test file houses an in-package fakeScanner + fakeRepo with concurrent-safe mutexes and call recording, and covers: construction port validation (one nil per case → CodeInternal); Plan input validation (every required field, every kind/format check → CodeInvalidInput with the named field recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf); the dry-run-doesn't-query-Repository contract (no-assignment Plan never calls the repo); every classification Status × Reason combination as a positive case (StatusReady / StatusSkipDuplicate / StatusSkipMissingParent / StatusSkipUnsupported / StatusSkipAlreadyImported / StatusPendingOwner); Scanner failure surfaces as the right apierr code (DokployUnavailable → yerr.CodeServer, NOT CodeUnavailable — that's for Yalla-owned dependencies); Repository failure preserves catalogued codes; secrets redacted in error strings AND in log buffer (slog over a bytes.Buffer, output.NewRedactor(secret), assert secret never appears); Apply persists in hierarchy order (organization → project → environment → service, asserted by scanning the recorded calls slice for ordering invariants); Apply continues past per-item failures (inject failServiceByDokployID for one specific service create, prove sibling creates still succeeded); Apply ctx-cancellation propagates (errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)); Apply on no-ready-items is a valid no-op accounting Skipped count. The Plan JSON shape is PUBLIC compatibility contract before the customer-facing endpoint even exists — stable field names, no pointers in PlanItem, no embedded interfaces, no time.Time on the wire — because the eventual POST endpoint (BE-0289 et al) serialises it as-is. Canonical test fixture ID format: kind prefix + "_" + 26-char Crockford base32 lowercase suffix from "0123456789abcdefghjkmnpqrstvwxyz" (no i, l, o, u); use "org_01hz00000000000000000000xx" style (4-char prefix + 20 zeros + 2-char tag = 26) — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical for some tests and will fail strict ParseID. Slug derivation goes one-way: domain.NormalizeSlug(dokployName) for the Dokploy → Yalla direction (a NormalizeSlug rejection is itself a classification, NOT an error to propagate); domain.DokployName(label, yallaID) is the OPPOSITE direction and must not be reused here. supportedServiceType(svc) AND-s dokploy.ServiceType.Valid() with the database-requires-engine rule and collapses both failure modes into a single StatusSkipUnsupported reason — the operator's remediation is the same. Reconcile and migrateimport are siblings under this pattern; future "resource lifecycle worker", "drift admin reconciler", "billing reconciler" packages should reuse the package layout verbatim.
ralph/progress.txt:415:- POST sub-action endpoint on a deep resource by bare id (BE-0163 pattern, POST /v1/environments/{environment_id}/clone, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.Clone + CloneEnvironmentInput + validateCloneEnvironmentInput, httpapi/environments.go cloneEnvironmentHandler + EnvironmentCloner): canonical shape for ALL sub-action POSTs on a bare-id route (clone, restart, rollback, cancel, restore, etc.). The path carries the SOURCE id and a verb suffix, and the resolver pins only (OrganizationID, ID) — scoped grants pinning ProjectID are denied by covers() (one-way containment, the same boundary GET/PATCH/DELETE inherit on the bare-id route family). The structural parent_id of the new row (project_id for env.clone, etc.) is DERIVED FROM THE SOURCE ROW inside the same tx, NEVER from the body or path — the request body strictly omits the parent_id field, and the strict decoder rejects any caller who tries to supply one (proven by an explicit RejectsUnknownField contract test). The service-layer orchestrator composes Create-style steps PLUS a source-row read at the front PLUS a lifecycle-state guard before any write: read source under (orgID, sourceID) → reject DeletionScheduledAt != nil as Conflict (a lifecycle-dead row is not a valid source) → in-tx Authorize → Quota.Reserve → Repository.Insert (the new row inherits source.ProjectID) → JobEnqueue → Audit.Append with Metadata{"slug","project_id","cloned_from": source.ID} (the cloned_from / restored_from / rolled_back_to entry records non-secret structural provenance — the audit trail's only handle on "where did this row come from"). Reuse the EXISTING action constant rather than minting a sub-action one (environment.create on a clone route, not environment.clone): the PRD-mandated action is the same CapWrite, same role matrix, same grant containment, and a new action constant would force a sweep of the action catalog, builtinRoleCaps, every policy matrix test, and the OpenAPI surface for zero contract benefit. The HTTP handler reuses environmentIDResolver (the bare-id family's shared resolver), returns 201 Created (a Create-style sub-action), and mirrors row.Version into the ETag header via writeOrganizationETag. Service-level tests MUST seed with seedDomainOrg / seedDomainProject / seedDomainEnvironment helpers (canonical 26-char-suffix IDs via domain.MustNewID — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical and the service's strict ParseID validation correctly rejects them); repository-level tests can use either helper. NewHandler+newRouteTable each grow ONE positional port (environmentCloner EnvironmentCloner at the tail of the env-ports group, after environmentDeleter); the three-pattern sweep across ~38 test files anchors on `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)` → `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, fakeEnvironmentCloner{}, nil)`, plus the `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{})` newRouteTable form → `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, fakeEnvironmentCloner{})`, plus the all-nils OpenAPI-shape tests bumped from N to N+1 nils. cmd/yalla-api/main.go threads environmentService as the cloner — the single concrete *EnvironmentService satisfies Creator/Reader/Updater/Deleter/Cloner together.
ralph/progress.txt:417:- DELETE endpoint for a deep resource by bare id (BE-0160 pattern, DELETE /v1/environments/{environment_id}, migration 0016_environment_deletion_schedule, store/environment.go EnvironmentRepository.ScheduleDeletion, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.ScheduleDeletion + DeleteEnvironmentInput, httpapi/environments.go deleteEnvironmentHandler + EnvironmentDeleter): canonical shape for ALL deep-resource SOFT-delete endpoints. The migration is structurally identical to 0013_project_deletion_schedule — `ALTER TABLE <resource> ADD COLUMN deletion_scheduled_at timestamptz` + a partial index `WHERE deletion_scheduled_at IS NOT NULL` for the future teardown-worker scan. The repository ScheduleDeletion is an SQL-IDEMPOTENT unconditional UPDATE stamping `deletion_scheduled_at = now()` returning the row in column order (so callers see the trigger-refreshed updated_at + bumped version); the already-scheduled distinction is the service's job (the repository must remain SQL-idempotent for non-customer callers — workers, admin jobs — that retry). ifMatchVersion uses the SAME pattern as Update — nil disables, non-nil adds `AND version = $N` to the WHERE — and classify*ConcurrencyMiss disambiguates the no-rows case (gone vs stale) by Get-in-same-tx. The service-layer orchestrator validates blank-input BEFORE opening the tx (typed InvalidInput, missing actor org is Internal — wiring error, never client input), then in ONE Store.Write: read current row, check If-Match pre-condition (stale -> ConflictStale BEFORE the already-scheduled check, so a stale caller learns version-stale instead of an already-scheduled message that races with concurrent edits they did not see), reject already-scheduled as apierr.Conflict (rolls the whole tx back so the audit record can never name a deletion that did not change resource state), call Repository.ScheduleDeletion, record the resolved deletion_scheduled_at stamp as audit metadata (`event.Metadata["deletion_scheduled_at"] = row.DeletionScheduledAt.UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)` — a non-secret timestamp, safe to record verbatim), append audit row. UNLIKE Create, the orchestrator does NOT invoke the in-tx Authorizer or QuotaReserver — the HTTP RequireAuth gate is authoritative for the delete path; the in-tx Authorize is reserved for Create's defence-in-depth against grant-change races during quota reservation. The HTTP handler reuses parseIfMatchVersion + writeOrganizationETag + telemetry.FromContext for correlation; returns 202 Accepted (the scheduling is durable but the destructive teardown is a later worker job). The wire DTO (projectEnvironment / projectResource) gains a `DeletionScheduledAt *string \`json:"deletion_scheduled_at,omitempty"\`` field so a live row's JSON shape is byte-identical to before (omitempty + nil pointer = field absent), while a scheduled row exposes the stamp as RFC 3339 — mirror this on every resource DTO that gains a soft-delete column. Routes.go registration uses `Method: http.MethodDelete`, `SuccessStatus: http.StatusAccepted`, the same `environmentIDResolver` as GET/PATCH (the bare-id three routes share one resolver), and the same description shape as `deleteProject`'s scheduled-not-immediate / version-pre-check / one-tx-audit clauses. NewHandler+newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (`environmentDeleter EnvironmentDeleter` at the very tail of the env ports group, after `environmentUpdater`); a Python regex sweep over the test directory anchored on `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, nil)` -> `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)` and `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, logger)` (37 NewHandler call sites across 37 files) handled the bulk; the named-updater outlier `fakeEnvironmentReader{}, updater, nil)` in the resource's own update test got `..., updater, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)`; the all-nil newRouteTable form (7 files) received one trailing nil; the partial-fakes-with-trailing-nils form (projects_delete_test.go + projects_restore_test.go ending `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil, nil)`) needed one MORE trailing nil; the multi-line newRouteTable-with-explicit-fakes form (3 files ending `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{})`) became `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{})`. Run `go vet ./...` AFTER each sweep — Go's positional-arg compile error tells you exactly which call sites the regex missed. The store-side integration tests cover repo happy-path + NotFound + tenant-scoped + nil-tx-rejection plus service happy-path-with-audit + already-scheduled-rolls-back-AND-stamp-unchanged-AND-only-one-audit-row + NotFound-no-audit + tenant-scoped-no-audit + stale-If-Match + blank-input + missing-actor-org. The httpapi contract tests cover happy-path-forwards-(principal,path,if-match)+202+ETag+wire-DTO + named-If-Match forwarded + malformed-If-Match 400 + NotFound 404 + already-scheduled 409 + stale-If-Match 409 + missing-deleter 500 + unauthenticated 401 + OpenAPI route registered. Forward-applicable to every future bare-id soft-delete endpoint (service.delete on /v1/services/{service_id}, etc.).
ralph/progress.txt:418:- New top-level resource family that already has its persistence table from an earlier migration (BE-0148 pattern, environments table from 0002 + version column from 0011, store/environment.go, httpapi/project_environments.go): a reader-only scoped-child vertical lands JUST the GET endpoint and pairs with the existing table — NO new migration. The vertical is structurally identical to BE-0142 (project_variables reader) with one narrowing worth pinning: when the table already exists (environments was created by 0002_tenant_hierarchy.up.sql at the same time as projects/services, and gained its version + bump_version trigger in 0011_optimistic_versions.up.sql), the story collapses into store layer + http layer + wiring + tests — no migration to declare composite FKs, scope-uniqueness indexes, or triggers. The store layer (Environment struct, EnvironmentRepository.ListByProject, EnvironmentReader.ListProjectEnvironments) mirrors ProjectVariableRepository/ProjectVariableReader exactly — every read is tenant-scoped at the SQL predicate, the reader Gets the project first inside the same Store.Read tx so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound (never an empty list), and the repository has its own list cap (environmentListMaxRows = 500). Crucially, environments carry NO credential material (the table stores only id, organization_id, project_id, slug, display_name, version, created_at, updated_at) so the HTTP projection (projectEnvironmentOf) needs no value-redaction chokepoint — every field is structural — but the projection STILL exists as a single chokepoint so a future column added to store.Environment is reviewed for its wire exposure here rather than leaking by default. The integration tests (store/environment_test.go) prove ordering, tenant scoping (a cross-tenant (orgA, projB_id) pair yields no rows), the empty-project shape (non-nil empty slice), the reader's project existence check, and that a cross-tenant id surfaces as apierr.NotFound WITHOUT leaking any of the foreign tenant's environment id / project id / slug into the error message. The HTTP layer adds a new operation tag (tagEnvironments = "environments") because environments will get their own /v1/environments/{environment_id} surface later; the list endpoint is tagged [tagProjects, tagEnvironments] mirroring how project_variables uses [tagProjects, tagVariables]. NewHandler and newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (projectEnvironments ProjectEnvironmentReader at the TAIL of the arg list, after projectVariableReplacer); cmd/yalla-api/main.go wires *store.EnvironmentReader at startup. A Python regex sweep over 33 test files anchored on `fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, fakeProjectEnvironmentReader{}, nil)` (44 sites) plus a second sweep on `fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, logger)` (server_test.go + me_contract_test.go) plus the named-replacer outlier `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, replacer, nil)` -> `..., replacer, fakeProjectEnvironmentReader{}, nil)` (project_variables_test.go's replaceProjectVariablesHandlerFor) plus the openapi-only newRouteTable form `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, fakeProjectVariableReplacer{})` -> `..., fakeProjectEnvironmentReader{})` (project_variables_test.go's BE-0142 OpenAPI test) handled the NewHandler/newRouteTable churn (54 sites total). The all-nil newRouteTable form with 32 nils received one trailing nil to become 33 (6 sites). projects_delete_test.go + projects_restore_test.go newRouteTable calls ended `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil, nil)` and got one MORE trailing nil to become `nil, nil, nil, nil)`. The KindEnvironment domain Kind ("env") already existed and is in the valid-kinds map from earlier work; no domain/id.go changes were needed. Forward-applicable to every future reader-only vertical on a hierarchy resource whose table predates the story.
ralph/progress.txt:486:- Replace-grants writer vertical (BE-0139 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant.go + httpapi/project_grants.go): a PUT-replace endpoint on a (parent, scoped-children) resource family pairs the existing reader-port adapter with a unit-of-work writer (ProjectGrantService) modeled exactly on OrganizationVariableService. The repository grows two new methods: Upsert(tx, id, organizationID, projectID, principalID, principalKind, role, *envID, *svcID) (ProjectGrant, error) using `ON CONFLICT (organization_id, project_id, principal_id, COALESCE(environment_id, ''), COALESCE(service_id, ''))` so the customer-mutable `role` is the only column the UPDATE branch touches (the immutable scope tuple + the bump_version trigger handle the rest); DeleteByProjectExceptIDs(tx, organizationID, projectID, keepIDs) renders the empty-keepIDs case as an unconstrained DELETE (an explicit clear is a meaningful customer operation), the non-empty case as `NOT (id = ANY($3::text[]))` so the bulk delete-by-exclusion is one round trip — same shape as DeleteByOrganizationExceptKeys but anchored on the row id (which Upsert returns from RETURNING) rather than a customer-supplied key, so an upsert that mutates an existing row deterministically keeps the survivor's id. The service Replace runs inside one Store.Write tx: project existence Get (a missing parent is typed NotFound, never a generic FK conflict), per-grant Upsert collecting the persisted ids into keepIDs, DeleteByProjectExceptIDs(keepIDs), audit.Append, post-write ListByProject re-read — every step shares the *Tx so a partial replace and an orphaned audit row are both impossible. Validation lives in buildProjectGrantReplace (split out, unit-testable without a DB): closed-set checks on principal_kind / role mirror the table's CHECK constraints, structural checks reject blank/NUL ids and a service-scope-without-environment, in-memory dedup uses `principalID + "\x00" + COALESCE(envID,'') + "\x00" + COALESCE(svcID,'')` to match the SQL unique index byte-for-byte. Audit metadata records counts only — `grant_count`, `project_scope_count`, `environment_scope_count`, `service_scope_count` — never principal ids, role names, or environment/service identifiers; per-row mutation history is the GET /v1/projects/{project_id}/grants surface's job. The HTTP layer adds a ProjectGrantReplacer port mirroring OrganizationVariableReplacer, a strict-decoded replaceProjectGrantsRequest with Grants *[]projectGrantRequest (pointer-to-slice so missing field is structurally distinguishable from an explicit empty array — a missing field is a stable 400, an empty array reaches the store), and the handler clones caller-supplied *string EnvironmentID/ServiceID pointers before forwarding them so the store cannot observe the caller's backing memory. NewHandler and newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (projectGrantReplacer ProjectGrantReplacer just after projectGrants ProjectGrantReader); a Python regex sweep over 27 test files anchored on `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, nil)` handled the bulk of the NewHandler churn (43 call sites), with three follow-up patterns hand-fixed: `, logger)` in server_test.go and me_contract_test.go (NewHandler with non-nil logger), `, nil)` newRouteTable call (different return type), and the 29-nil all-nil newRouteTable pattern in six test files (added one trailing nil for the new port). The story implicitly delivers BE-0439's CRUD repository acceptance criteria (Upsert + DeleteByProjectExceptIDs are the missing half) and the writer side of BE-0440's tenant-isolation criteria.
ralph/progress.txt:487:- Replace-variables writer vertical for a scoped-child resource (BE-0145 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_variable.go + project_variable_service.go + httpapi/project_variables.go): a PUT-replace endpoint on a (parent, scoped-children) variables family pairs the existing reader-port adapter with a unit-of-work writer (ProjectVariableService) modeled exactly on OrganizationVariableService — same Upsert + DeleteByProjectExceptKeys + Replace shape, same audit metadata convention (counts-only: `variable_count`, `secret_count`), same value-redaction chokepoints (LogValue + wire output.Sentinel + audit Auditor regex), but with two distinguishing axes worth pinning. (1) The repository's Upsert key is the (organization_id, project_id, key) triple from migration 0015's UNIQUE (not the (organization_id, key) pair from migration 0012), so the ON CONFLICT target carries an extra column — every other column position in the INSERT statement shifts by one. The DELETE-except-keys SQL similarly has two tenant predicate legs (organization_id AND project_id) before the `key <> ALL($3)` clause; the empty/nil keepKeys path is the deliberate full-clear (a customer asking to remove every project-scoped variable is meaningful, not a no-op). (2) The ProjectVariableService re-uses the org-level helpers verbatim — `itoaVariableIndex`, `posixEnvVarName`, validate.MaxEnvVarNameLen/MaxEnvVarValueLen/MaxSecretValueLen — and uses `projectVariablesWriteAction = "env.write"` (same string constant as the org-level path) so the audit Action is identical at both scopes; the resource_kind axis (domain.KindProject vs domain.KindOrganization) is what disambiguates the audit row. The Service runs inside one Store.Write tx: ProjectRepository.Get (a missing OR cross-tenant project surfaces as the typed NotFound the GET reader already proves, NEVER as a generic FK conflict from the upsert), per-variable Upsert minting a fresh `pvar_` id (the existing-row's id is preserved on conflict, NOT the caller-supplied id), DeleteByProjectExceptKeys(keepKeys), audit.Append, post-write ListByProject re-read so the response body byte-matches the committed state. The HTTP layer adds a ProjectVariableReplacer port mirroring OrganizationVariableReplacer, strict-decoded replaceProjectVariablesRequest with `Variables *[]projectVariableRequest` (pointer-to-slice so a missing field is structurally distinguishable from an explicit empty array — a missing field is a stable 400 with reason `errProjectVariablesBodyMissing` "must be supplied (use an empty array to clear every variable)", an empty array reaches the store as the deliberate clear). The handler takes OrganizationID from the principal's home org (NEVER the caller — the body carries no org id), so a cross-tenant {project_id} still hits the tenant-scoped Get and surfaces as 404 at the persistence boundary. NewHandler and newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (projectVariableReplacer ProjectVariableReplacer just after projectVariables ProjectVariableReader); a Python regex sweep over the test directory anchored on `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, fakeProjectVariableReplacer{}, nil)` and `fakeProjectVariableReader{}, logger)` (multi-line variant included) handled the bulk of the NewHandler churn (31 files); the all-nil newRouteTable form with 31 nils received one trailing nil to become 32; two outlier patterns needed targeted edits — project_variables_test.go's `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, reader, nil)` (where `reader` is a named parameter, not a literal) and projects_delete_test.go / projects_restore_test.go's `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, nil, nil)` (where the new-port slot was already nil-filled by an earlier story, so one MORE trailing nil was added rather than inserting in the middle). The story implicitly delivers the writer half of project_variables CRUD repository acceptance criteria and the writer side of tenant-isolation criteria.
ralph/progress.txt:492:- Reader-only scoped-child vertical (BE-0142 pattern, project_variables migration 0015, store/project_variable.go, httpapi/project_variables.go): a new (parent, scoped-children) resource family that ships JUST the GET endpoint mirrors the BE-0136 full-vertical shape with two narrowings worth pinning. (1) The migration is structurally identical to project_grants 0014 — composite (organization_id, project_id) FK -> projects with ON DELETE CASCADE, composite UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as the FK target a future override table (environment_variables / service_variables) can use, bigint version + bump_version trigger from 0011, set_updated_at trigger — but a different scope-uniqueness shape: project_variables uses UNIQUE (organization_id, project_id, key) directly (no COALESCE because key is non-nullable) where project_grants needs the COALESCE-NULLS index for env/svc-scoped uniqueness. (2) The reader vertical defers the writer's repository surface — only ListByProject(ctx, Querier, organizationID, projectID) lands now — so the repo file is short and the audit-grade write path (Upsert + DeleteByProjectExceptKeys + bulk validation) is BE-0145's job. The store-backed adapter (ProjectVariableReader) mirrors ProjectGrantReader exactly: composes ProjectRepository.Get + ListByProject inside one Store.Read transaction so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound, never an empty list. ProjectVariable.LogValue redacts every value at the slog boundary (mirrors OrganizationVariable.LogValue), so a stray panic stack that captures the struct cannot leak a non-secret value either — the slog redaction is unconditional even though the wire layer only redacts secret values. NewHandler signature grows ONE positional port (projectVariables ProjectVariableReader after projectGrantReplacer); a JS regex sweep over 30 test files anchored on `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, nil)` -> `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, fakeProjectVariableReader{}, nil)` plus a second sweep on `fakeProjectGrantReplacer{}, logger)` handled the NewHandler churn (45 call sites); a separate state-machine sweep over newRouteTable calls appended one trailing arg to every direct invocation (8 files), and project_grants_put_test.go's `fakeProjectGrantReader{}, replacer, nil)` (where replacer is a real var, not a literal) was the one anchor the bulk regex couldn't reach. The KindProjectVariable Kind prefix is `pvar` (mirrors the ovar/pgrnt prefix shape); the existing id_test.go's hardcoded valid-kinds lists do NOT need updating — they sanity-check a subset (KindProjectGrant is also absent from those lists yet works in tests). KindProjectVariable still has to land in the kinds map for Kind.Valid() / ParseID to accept it.
ralph/progress.txt:493:- Inverse-lifecycle endpoints (restore vs schedule-delete) reuse the same Repository+Service pattern: the repository is an SQL-idempotent UPDATE returning the row (with optional version WHERE — NotFound on no-rows + nil ifMatch, classifyProjectConcurrencyMiss on no-rows + non-nil), and the Service does the lifecycle-state precondition check inside the same Store.Write transaction. For restore: pre-check current.DeletionScheduledAt != nil (else apierr.Conflict "project is not scheduled for deletion"); for schedule-delete: pre-check ==nil (else "deletion is already scheduled"). The pre-check is the customer's truth-finding step — the repository MUST stay SQL-idempotent so workers/admin jobs can retry without surfacing a misleading Conflict. Audit metadata captures the prior state (for restore, current.DeletionScheduledAt read BEFORE the UPDATE; for schedule-delete, the new stamp from the persisted row) so the trail documents what the resource looked like before/after the mutation.
ralph/progress.txt:504:- Partial-update (PATCH) endpoints: the request DTO and store *Input use *string fields — nil means "field omitted, leave unchanged". The store builds+validates the patch BEFORE opening the transaction (rejecting a patch that names no field as a 400), then inside Store.Write reads the current row, applies the non-nil fields, and Updates. A repository Update using UPDATE ... RETURNING must check errors.Is(err, pgx.ErrNoRows) for the missing-row case BEFORE mapWriteError (which only classifies constraint-violation vs outage). Never set updated_at in an Update statement — the per-table set_updated_at() BEFORE UPDATE trigger owns it.
ralph/progress.txt:590:- Customer-facing DELETE endpoints schedule a SOFT delete, never a hard delete: the store stamps a nullable deletion_scheduled_at column (migration 0010 for organizations) inside the same transaction as the <resource>.delete audit record, and the endpoint returns 202 Accepted. A hard DELETE would cascade through child tables AND the audit log (ON DELETE CASCADE), so the destructive teardown is a separate worker story. Re-scheduling an already-stamped row is apierr.Conflict, detected with a Get inside the tx (the repository ScheduleDeletion is an unconditional UPDATE).
ralph/progress.txt:601:- Store-backed httpapi endpoints use a narrow reader-port pattern: httpapi declares the port interface (e.g. OrganizationReader), a Store.Read-backed adapter in the store package satisfies it by composing the tenant-scoped repository (mirror store.CredentialReader — never issue fresh SQL), and the port is threaded NewHandler -> newRouteTable -> the handler closure. httpapi imports store (acyclic — store never imports httpapi). For an endpoint with no path/query parameter, tenant isolation is structural: the handler only ever passes principal.OrganizationID. NewHandler signature: NewHandler(build, readiness, meta, authenticator, engine, orgs, logger).
ralph/progress.txt:604:- Persistence access goes through internal/controlplane/store: Store.Read (read-only tx -> Querier) and Store.Write (read/write tx -> *Tx; commit on nil, rollback on ANY error or panic). A *Tx is obtainable ONLY inside Store.Write; repository mutation methods take *Tx, read methods take Querier — so a mutation structurally cannot run outside the transaction that also carries its authorization and quota checks. A unit of work validates input first, then composes authorize -> reserve quota -> write desired state -> enqueue durable job inside one Store.Write. The policy/quota/jobs dependencies are narrow port interfaces defined IN store (Authorizer, QuotaReserver, JobEnqueuer); store never imports those packages. Error mapping is uniform: pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound, SQLSTATE class 23 -> apierr.Conflict, other driver errors -> apierr.StoreUnavailable; the raw cause is wrapped for logging only. ProjectRepository/ProjectService are the reference implementation.
ralph/progress.txt:605:- Credentials: internal/controlplane/auth owns generation/hashing/verification; internal/controlplane/store owns persistence. A secret is stored ONLY as a one-way hash plus a public, globally-UNIQUE prefix — never plaintext. Plaintext credential types implement String/GoString/LogValue/MarshalJSON returning output.Sentinel; the plaintext is reachable only via an explicit Reveal() method. High-entropy secrets (>=128 bits random) use fast unsalted SHA-256 + subtle.ConstantTimeCompare, NOT bcrypt/argon2. Authentication lookup (FindByPrefix) is the ONE deliberate non-tenant-scoped repository read — it runs before the tenant is known; the found row carries its own OrganizationID. Tenant-scoped mutations that may match zero rows report apierr.NotFound via tag.RowsAffected()==0.
ralph/progress.txt:620:- Customer-facing soft-delete endpoints come in two shapes. Organization.delete uses a deletion_scheduled_at stamp + 202 Accepted (asynchronous teardown by a future worker; re-scheduling is apierr.Conflict). API-key DELETE is a one-way revocation: the api_keys.revoked_at column is stamped and the credential is permanently unusable from that moment (the auth-layer IsUsable check already rejects a revoked row), the row stays so the audit trail of who minted it stays linked to it, response is 200 OK with the revoked snapshot (mirroring members.Remove's terminal-view convention). The repository (APIKeyRepository.Revoke) is silently idempotent at SQL via COALESCE(revoked_at, $now), but the service layer (APIKeyService.Revoke) pre-checks current.IsRevoked() inside the tx and returns apierr.Conflict on re-revoke — the customer's view of the lifecycle is stale and a silent success would write a misleading audit record. Pattern: customer endpoints surface stale-lifecycle conflicts; repository methods stay SQL-idempotent for non-customer callers (workers, admin jobs).
ralph/progress.txt:629:- Reads that union "configured" + "live" state for the same key set (a quota policy join with quota_usage, a desired-state join with reconciled actual state, ...) use ONE SQL statement with FULL OUTER JOIN + COALESCE(left.key, right.key) for the projection key, never two queries with a Go-side merge. The repository method takes (organizationID, plan) and stays tenant-scoped at every leg of the join — the CTE shape `WITH policies AS (... WHERE org=$1 OR plan=$2), usage AS (... WHERE org=$1) FULL OUTER JOIN ... ORDER BY resource` is the reference; two queries would force the handler to re-implement deterministic ordering AND would leak observability (the two queries can see different snapshots).
ralph/progress.txt:769:  - `internal/controlplane/store/usage_event_repository_test.go`
ralph/progress.txt:796:  - `usage_events` still carries legacy quota columns (`resource`, `delta`, `reservation_id`) for compatibility; new billing writers must provide `quantity`/`unit`/`source` and should use the repository rather than raw SQL.
ralph/progress.txt:802:- Organization-scoped variables (0012_organization_variables, store/organization_variable.go, httpapi/variables.go): the lowest-precedence layer of the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service variable hierarchy. The table mirrors the established conventions — composite UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as the FK target for future scope-narrowing override tables; UNIQUE (organization_id, key) for per-tenant key uniqueness (an env var named DATABASE_URL in two orgs is two rows); ON DELETE CASCADE from organizations; bigint version + bump_version() trigger for optimistic concurrency. The repository follows the AuditRepository read pattern: ListByOrganization is tenant-scoped at the SQL predicate (cross-tenant id matches no rows, empty list, never another tenant's data), ordered deterministically by (key ASC, id ASC). The wire redaction contract is wire-side at the projection chokepoint, NOT store-side: store.OrganizationVariable.Value carries the literal verbatim (a future write path needs lossless round-trip), but httpapi/variables.go's organizationVariableOf replaces is_secret=true values with output.Sentinel on every projection — agents can never read a secret value back through this endpoint. Defence-in-depth: OrganizationVariable.LogValue redacts every value (regardless of is_secret) at the slog boundary, so a stray log record that captures the struct cannot leak the literal. Non-secret values pass through verbatim so customers can audit their own organization-wide defaults.
ralph/progress.txt:805:- Hard-delete vs soft-delete decision rule: a customer-facing resource takes a SOFT delete (deletion_scheduled_at on organizations + 202 Accepted, revoked_at on api_keys + 200 OK with revocation snapshot) ONLY when the row carries a continuing tie that must outlive the resource — an audit-log FK that must keep the row reachable, a child cascade that must be ordered carefully, or a "lifecycle stale" property the customer's audit trail depends on. A resource with no continuing tie (the audit log records the deletion independently, no child tables, no FK ties) takes a HARD delete with a 200 OK response carrying the row's snapshot exactly as it stood at the moment of removal (DELETE /v1/organizations/{org_id}/variables/{key}, BE-0115). The store repository method uses `DELETE FROM ... WHERE org=$1 AND key=$2 RETURNING ...` so one round trip removes the row AND yields the snapshot the orchestrator returns to the wire; pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound (a cross-tenant key is indistinguishable from a missing row). The service files an audit event in the SAME tx as the delete, with Metadata={variable_id: row.ID} — customer-supplied key + the deleted value are never recorded, so the audit row is auditable without ever observing the variable name or its value. An agent can confirm what was deleted (id, key, version, timestamps) without a separate read; 204 No Content would have been less useful here.
ralph/progress.txt:807:- Audit-event listing in tests: store.AuditRepository.ListByOrganization is ORDER BY occurred_at DESC, id DESC — events[0] is the NEWEST event. A test that reads events[len-1] gets the OLDEST event, which in a "seed then mutate" fixture is the seed event (with seed metadata), NOT the mutation under test. A test that probes the post-mutation state through ANOTHER mutation (e.g. a second Replace to verify the deleted row is gone) files yet another audit event and pushes the mutation-under-test to events[1] — so audit assertions must run BEFORE any further state-changing call, and post-state probes must read directly through the repository (NewOrganizationVariableRepository().ListByOrganization), NOT through another service-level mutation.
ralph/progress.txt:808:- Deep-resource resolver pattern (BE-0124, GET /v1/projects/{project_id}, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects.go projectIDResolver): when an endpoint targets a resource named in the path but the path has NO org_id segment (sub-organization endpoints like /v1/projects/{project_id}, future /v1/.../environments/{environment_id}, /v1/.../services/{service_id}), the ResourceResolver pulls the principal's home OrganizationID from the request context with policy.PrincipalFromContext(r.Context()) — RequireAuth attaches the principal BEFORE invoking the resolver, so the context already carries it even though the ResourceResolver signature is func(r *http.Request) policy.Resource. The resolver returns Scope{OrganizationID: principal.HomeOrg, ProjectID: r.PathValue("project_id"), Kind: domain.KindProject}; do NOT look up the resource's real org from the database inside the resolver (keep the resolver pure path+context, no I/O). The policy engine then authorizes against the principal's own home org root combined with the path id, so a project/environment/service-scoped grant for THAT resource (containment match) authorizes via ReasonAllowedByGrant while a grant for a SIBLING resource fails the engine's covers() check; cross-tenant project_ids reach the tenant-scoped repository query with the principal's home org and surface as a deterministic 404 (E_NOT_FOUND) from the persistence layer, NOT a 403 from policy — which is acceptable per the acceptance criteria ("cross-tenant IDs must behave as not found or forbidden"). The Support principal's cross-tenant read capability does NOT apply through this endpoint because the resource scope is pinned to the principal's home org, not the path's tenant; support cross-tenant reads remain available through endpoints whose path DOES carry an {org_id} (organizationIDResolver). PATCH/DELETE on the same resource adopt the same resolver — handlers should call PathValue and use principal.OrganizationID for the store call, never trust a caller-supplied org id. The canonical regression check is TestGetProjectCrossTenantIDBehavesAsNotFound (the response body must not echo a foreign organization id either).
ralph/progress.txt:809:- Reader-port extension pattern (BE-0124): when a new endpoint on the same resource family lands (e.g. GET /v1/projects/{project_id} alongside GET /v1/projects), extend the existing reader port in place rather than declaring a parallel sibling interface — ProjectReader now has BOTH ListProjects and GetProject; OrganizationReader has GetOrganization and is used by both list and by-id endpoints. The fakeFooReader in the *_test.go is similarly extended in place (added project/getErr/gotGetOrgID/gotGetProjectID fields on fakeProjectReader for the by-id tests); the zero value of the extended fake still works for every prior call site (the other test files / suites that never reach the handler) so no cross-test churn is needed. The store-backed adapter pattern stays the same: *Reader composes *Repository (never re-issues SQL), each new method opens its own short-lived Store.Read tx and delegates to an existing Querier-accepting repository method, inheriting that method's tenant-scoping guarantees for free. Auth-error sentinel naming gotcha: the auth package exports the PLURAL form (auth.ErrInvalidCredentials, auth.ErrNoCredentials) — singular forms do not exist; tests that exercise the invalid-credential path must use the plural names to match the writeAuthError errors.Is dispatch.
ralph/progress.txt:811:- The CapWrite-on-deep-resource policy matrix (BE-0129, internal/controlplane/httpapi/projects_update_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the CapWrite-on-nil-resolver matrix (BE-0123, projects_create) but for endpoints that use projectIDResolver (or any deep-resource resolver: future environmentIDResolver, serviceIDResolver). The role matrix is the same CapWrite split (Owner/Admin/Developer allow via ReasonAllowedByRole; Viewer/CI/Support deny via ReasonDeniedNoCapability) because the resource org id comes from the principal's home org and falls same-tenant at the engine. The cross-tenant probe stays at the engine layer (the wire cannot name another tenant — a foreign project_id surfaces as 404 from the tenant-scoped repository query, with the body never echoing the foreign org), and EVERY role — including Support — must deny via ReasonDeniedCrossTenant on a foreign-org Resource. Grant-containment for a deep-resource write authorizes against the resource (project / env / service) scope, NOT the org root, so a project-level grant naming THIS resource ALLOWS via ReasonAllowedByGrant and a project-level grant naming a SIBLING resource denies via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope (covers() one-way); a project-level VIEWER grant on the SAME resource denies via ReasonDeniedNoCapability (the grant covers the resource but does NOT confer CapWrite — this is the NEW assertion vs CapRead matrices, where a Viewer grant covers + allows); env-/service-scoped grants on the parent project deny via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope; an org-level Admin grant allows via ReasonAllowedByGrant; an org-level Viewer grant denies via ReasonDeniedNoCapability (mirror-image of the org-level Viewer ALLOW on CapRead). The engine-twin "scoped key in a foreign home org" deny is also pinned (a scoped key cannot be smuggled across tenants — the cross-tenant guard fires before the grant is even considered). Deny-path leak guards needle for the canonical row identifiers AND the caller-supplied request-body fields (display_name patch payload), because either echo is a separate regression direction. Future CapWrite-on-deep-resource endpoints (project DELETE/restore, environment update/delete, service update/delete) copy this shape verbatim.
ralph/progress.txt:814:- New customer-data resource = full vertical (BE-0136 pattern, project_grants migration 0014, store/project_grant.go, httpapi/project_grants.go): one story is allowed to land the whole vertical when the resource has no foundation yet (no migration, no repo, no port, no handler, no wiring). The migration declares the table tenant-scoped at the database (composite (organization_id, project_id) FK -> projects with ON DELETE CASCADE, composite UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as the FK target for future scope-narrowing override tables, bigint version + bump_version trigger from 0011, set_updated_at trigger), CHECKs the enum-shaped columns to a closed set (principal_kind IN ('usr','sa'), role IN (six built-in roles)), and uses a UNIQUE index with COALESCE(env_id,'') / COALESCE(svc_id,'') so per-principal scope-tuple dedup works through NULLs (Postgres unique constraints treat NULLs as distinct by default until v15's NULLS NOT DISTINCT). The repository (ProjectGrantRepository) and reader adapter (ProjectGrantReader) follow OrganizationVariableReader exactly — repo accepts a Querier for reads, mutations need *Tx, reader composes the repo through Store.Read; cross-tenant queries return empty (no rows) at the SQL leg, but the LIST-FOR-PARENT shape (list grants OF a project) requires the reader to Get the project first inside the same transaction so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound — never as an empty list, which would invite an agent to believe the project exists with no grants. The HTTP layer adds a new ProjectGrantReader port + handler + projection (projectGrantResource), and the route plugs into the existing projectIDResolver and policy.ActionProjectGrantsRead in routes.go's newRouteTable. EnvironmentID / ServiceID stay nullable pointers all the way from the schema to the wire so the (project | environment | service)-scoped distinction reaches an agent intact; the principal projects as a nested object Principal(id, kind) for the same reason the audit_event.actor and usage.limit endpoints do — one boolean (principal.kind == 'usr') instead of branching on coupled scalars. NewHandler signature gets ONE more positional port, which forces a sweep of every test file that constructs the production handler (a Python regex over 28 test files anchored on `fakeProjectRestorer{}, X)` plus manual edits to projects_restore_test.go and projects_delete_test.go whose end-of-call shape didn't match the dominant anchor; six single-line newRouteTable calls with all-nil args need a separate sweep to add one trailing nil). cmd/yalla-api wires *store.ProjectGrantReader at startup. The story implicitly delivers most of BE-0439's repository acceptance criteria (CRUD comes with the PUT story BE-0139 later) and BE-0440's tenant-isolation criteria for the list path.
ralph/progress.txt:815:- Bare top-level lookup-by-id endpoint for a deep resource (BE-0154 pattern, GET /v1/environments/{environment_id}, store/environment.go GetByID + GetEnvironment, httpapi/environments.go): a customer-facing route that addresses a deep resource (env, service, deployment) by its global id WITHOUT carrying its parent ids in the path. Two load-bearing constraints: (1) the resolver builds policy.Resource{Scope: {OrganizationID = principal.home, <ResourceLevel>ID = path.id}} — pinning OrgID to the principal's home (NEVER the caller-supplied id) is the structural anti-spoof, and pinning the resource-level id is the only scope leg the path actually carries; (2) the resolver CANNOT pin the parent ids without a DB lookup, and the policy engine's Scope.covers() rule (internal/controlplane/policy/policy.go) requires every level the grant pins to match the resource — a grant with pinned ProjectID against a resource with empty ProjectID is structurally denied. Accepted design: middleware admits org-wide read roles + org-wide grants + (for CapRead actions) the support cross-tenant exception is suppressed because the resolver pins OrgID to principal.home; project-/env-/service-scoped grants are denied at the boundary by design, and grant-only principals must use the parent-scoped /v1/projects/{project_id}/environments instead. Document this in the route description, the resolver doc, and the handler doc — readers will misinterpret the structural deny as a bug otherwise. The store layer adds a tenant-scoped GetByID(ctx, q, organizationID, resourceID) on the existing repository (composite (organization_id, id) predicate so cross-tenant returns apierr.NotFound, never another tenant's row, never a 500 leaking the cause), and the existing reader struct grows a Get<Resource>(ctx, organizationID, resourceID) method that wraps GetByID inside Store.Read. The HTTP layer adds a NEW narrow port (distinct from the existing parent-scoped port — e.g. EnvironmentReader vs ProjectEnvironmentReader — so a handler change to one doesn't ripple through the other), a NEW resolver function (e.g. environmentIDResolver), and a NEW handler that REUSES the existing wire-shape projection (projectEnvironmentOf for environments) so the GET-by-id response is byte-identical to one element of the LIST response (modulo the wrapping object name). cmd/yalla-api/main.go passes the same *store.<Resource>Reader value twice — once for the parent-scoped port, once for the new bare-lookup port — because both methods live on the same store struct; no second startup wiring is needed. The contract tests cover the standard envelope: happy path, NotFound (cross-tenant + unknown both render as the same 404 — no oracle), 401 unauthenticated + invalid credentials (no token echo), 503 store outage (no raw cause leak), 500 nil-port wiring guard, envelope shape lock (asserts the documented field set against a generic decode so a future struct column added without updating the projection is caught), bearer-token never-logged guard (slog over a buffer), OpenAPI route registration, and TWO resolver unit tests (pins home org + path id; no panic with no principal on context). The matching policy matrix story (BE-0156 for environments, the structural template for service/deployment lookups) tests the deny — the deny matrix on the bare endpoint is part of the contract, not a gap.
ralph/progress.txt:847:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped grandchild table whose mutation surface is Insert + Update + ScheduleDeletion ONLY (NO UpdateDisplayName, NO Restore, NO ListByOrganization, NO CountByOrganization), with a database-managed version column, a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at), a CHECK-constrained closed-set Kind column, and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0442 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0438 project_tenant_isolation_test.go template MINUS the UpdateDisplayName/Restore byte-identical-bystander pairs (the EnvironmentRepository exposes neither method) MINUS the ListByOrganization/CountByOrganization tenant-scope tests (environments are listed PER PROJECT, not per organization — quota counts environments per-project elsewhere, not on this repository). Three structural divergences from the projects template are load-bearing: (1) the ListByProject leg is the per-project analogue of ListByOrganization — the WHERE filter is (organization_id, project_id) instead of (organization_id), so the cross-tenant existence-leak projection is widened to a 3-case table (orgA owned empty project / orgA probing orgB project / orgA probing unknown project) that each yield a deterministic empty slice (NOT nil) AND ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE on the wire; the existing TestEnvironmentRepoListByProjectIsTenantScoped (environment_test.go) proves the cross-tenant projB.ID-under-orgA case alone, so this file's contribution is the symmetry guard across the OTHER two probes. (2) the shared-slug fixture ("production" on BOTH tenants' projects) exercises the per-PROJECT UNIQUE (project_id, slug), not the per-organization UNIQUE (organization_id, slug) of projects — two tenants legitimately share the slug because UNIQUE is anchored on the parent project (which differs per tenant), so the WHERE-on-(organization_id, project_id) filter is the only thing isolating the two rows in ListByProject; a regression that resolved the WHERE by slug alone or by project_id alone would surface here. (3) assertEnvironmentByteIdentical adds a Kind assertion on top of the projects template's column set — environments carries kind in its row body (CHECK-constrained to 'standard' or 'preview' by migration 0025) and the Update path SET clause MUST NOT touch it, so a regression that promoted kind to a SET clause is caught here in addition to the slug/display_name/version/updated_at/deletion_scheduled_at anchors. The deletion_scheduled_at switch/case carries the same three legs (nil->non-nil for ScheduleDeletion, non-nil->nil for a hypothetical Restore that does not exist on this repository — kept anyway as defensive coverage in case Restore is added later — and value-drift across non-nil baselines). The cross-tenant existence-leak probe on GetByID is the standard Code-AND-Hint identity assertion the BE-0438 template provides; this file adds NOTHING new on that surface — the contribution is the byte-identical-bystander projection on every mutation path (Update / ScheduleDeletion) AND the existence-leak symmetry-table on ListByProject AND the per-tenant view of soft-deleted rows. Helper `projATuple(store.Project) -> testutil.Project` is local to this file: the factory's Environment / Service builders take a testutil.Project, but the test seeds projects through the typed repository surface and gets back a store.Project — projATuple bridges the two shapes without a dependency on testutil internals (only ID, OrganizationID, Slug, Name are needed). The environmentFixture / insertEnvironment / getEnvironmentOrFail / assertEnvironmentByteIdentical helpers are file-scope: environmentFixture and insertEnvironment are reused from environment_repository_invariants_test.go (BE-0441 — do NOT re-implement); getEnvironmentOrFail and assertEnvironmentByteIdentical are NEW and named uniquely (no projects-template name collision in the shared store_test package). Pre-existing test failures on HEAD that are NOT introduced by this story: TestEnvironmentRepoGetByIDReturnsRow, TestEnvironmentReaderRejectsCrossTenantProjectID, and TestEnvironmentRepoListByProjectReturnsDeterministicOrdering all fail in isolation on the base commit (testutil.Factory slug-token drift vs hard-coded "production"/"staging" expectations in environment_test.go) — confirmed by stashing the BE-0442 file and reproducing identical failures. Owed to a focused triage story before BE-0500+ verification stories run.
ralph/progress.txt:849:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped child table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete, no traditional UPDATE), with a database-managed version column, ONE optional nullable scoping pointer (service_id) instead of two (no environment_id pointer because the env IS the parent), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, environment_id) -> environments** (BE-0443 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). This is the BE-0439 project_grants template adapted for environment_grants. Three structural divergences from BE-0439 are load-bearing: (1) the EnvironmentGrant struct has NO ProjectID column and ONE optional pointer (ServiceID) instead of two (EnvironmentID is the mandatory FK target, not an optional scope leg) — the Upsert insert-pointer-roundtrip test collapses from two opt-in pointers to one (service-scoped grant). (2) The ON CONFLICT target is the unique index environment_grants_principal_scope_idx on (organization_id, environment_id, principal_id, COALESCE(service_id, '')) — there is no environment_id leg in the COALESCE because the environment is the parent scope, not an optional narrower; the duplicate-PK Conflict test uses a different principal_id under the same environment to dodge the scope-tuple branch and exercise the PRIMARY KEY (id) violation. (3) The CASCADE-delete-of-parent test deletes the environments row directly via raw SQL (NOT the EnvironmentRepository's ScheduleDeletion, which is a soft delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE) and observes via ListByEnvironment. Helper newEnvironmentGrantID synthesises an `egrnt_<token_n>` id by rewriting f.Environment's `env_<token_n>` prefix — the testutil.Factory only stamps known domain prefixes and there is no f.EnvironmentGrant, so prefix-rewriting is the cleanest way to mint factory-unique ids without reaching into domain.NewID(domain.KindEnvironmentGrant). Sentinel type `environmentGrantTxRollbackSentinel` is distinct from every other *_test.go file's rollback sentinel in store_test. List-helper name `listEnvironmentGrantsOrFail` is distinct from project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go's `listGrantsOrFail` to avoid same-package collision. Test name prefix `TestEnvironmentGrantRepository*` (full Repository) is distinct from environment_grant_test.go's `TestEnvironmentGrantRepo*` and `TestEnvironmentGrantReader*` siblings — no collisions.
ralph/progress.txt:850:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED short-lived claim/ledger table whose ONLY repository mutation surface is an InsertReservation (one-shot active-row INSERT) plus a single resolver read (SumActiveReservations), with NO per-row Update/Delete/Get, NO version column, a settled_consistent CHECK pairing status<>'active' with NON-NULL settled_at, a CHECK amount>0, a composite-FK out (organization_id, job_id) -> provisioning_jobs added by a LATER migration than the row's own migration, and a database-managed bump-only set_updated_at trigger fired BEFORE UPDATE** (BE-0455 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0453 (quota_usage -- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING + SELECT FOR UPDATE primitive) and BE-0451 (quota_policies -- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in six load-bearing ways: (a) the row-shape test must anchor BOTH a nullable-pointer JobID (zero on a no-JobID call, *string round-trip on a non-empty-JobID call) AND a nullable-pointer SettledAt (zero on every active-row insert -- there is no public surface that can mint a settled row); (b) the JobID round-trip leg cannot use a synthetic id alone because migration 0008 added a composite FK quota_reservations_job_fk -> provisioning_jobs (organization_id, id), so the test MUST pre-seed a real provisioning_jobs row (`INSERT INTO provisioning_jobs (id, organization_id, job_type, idempotency_key) VALUES (...)`) before InsertReservation -- a regression that dropped the FK or swapped its column order would surface here; (c) the settled_consistent CHECK violation has NO independent client-input surface (settled_at is not in the INSERT column list of InsertReservation), so the test exploits the OTHER direction: passing Status=ReservationStatusCommitted (non-blank, non-'active') sends settled_at=NULL via the default and the CHECK rejects it, proving InsertReservation can ONLY mint ACTIVE rows; (d) the time-filter leg of SumActiveReservations is a SECOND-AXIS exclusion test alongside the status filter -- a row with status='active' AND expires_at<=now must be excluded by the `expires_at > $3` predicate, so the test pre-seeds an active-but-time-expired row and asserts it is excluded WITHOUT mutating its status (a regression that dropped the time predicate would silently sum already-expired rows); (e) the resource-filter leg seeds TWO active reservations under the SAME tenant with DIFFERENT resources and asserts BOTH `SumActiveReservations(org, A, now)` and `SumActiveReservations(org, B, now)` return their own resource's amount (a regression that dropped the resource predicate would return 11 instead of 3 from either call); (f) the duplicate-ID Conflict test uses an explicit caller-supplied ID instead of relying on the qres_ prefix-mint path, because the PK violation is the constraint and a regression that silently re-minted the explicit id would mask the test (the explicit-ID-preservation test PRECEDES the duplicate-ID test and pins the no-remint contract). Forward-applicable to every future short-lived-claim repository whose public surface is a single one-shot active-row INSERT plus a resolver-read (a hypothetical lease-claim table KindResourceLease with active->held|released|expired, or a future job_attempts row whose public surface is a single INSERT per attempt). Test-helper naming caveat at package store_test: every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same package, so the type name `quotaReservationTxRollbackSentinel` must be distinct from quotaPolicyTxRollbackSentinel / quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel / projectTxRollbackSentinel and friends -- grep `TxRollbackSentinel` before adding a new sentinel. Helper REUSE strategy: this file DEFINES its own `runInsertReservationOrFail` / `loadQuotaReservationRowByID` / `countQuotaReservationRowsForOrg` / `rawQuotaReservationRow` (no shared symbols to reuse), and REUSES the existing `insertReservationRow` / `insertSettledReservationRow` helpers defined in quota_repository_test.go for the SumActiveReservations setup legs because those raw-SQL inserts can stamp the schema's required `settled_at = now()` for non-active rows without going through the repository's active-only mint path.
ralph/progress.txt:851:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED CLAIM-PROTOCOL TABLE WHOSE FOUR REPOSITORY SURFACES (Claim upsert with steal-expired branch / Complete state-transitioning UPDATE / Release state-conditioned DELETE / Find composite-tuple read) ALL CARRY organization_id AS THE LEADING COLUMN OF A COMPOSITE PREDICATE TUPLE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key), AND WHOSE ROW BODY CARRIES NULLABLE COMPLETION COLUMNS (response_status / response_body / completed_at) DISTINGUISHED BY NIL-NESS UNDER A COMPLETION-CONSISTENT CHECK, AND WHOSE INVARIANTS FILE ALREADY LANDED IN THE IMMEDIATELY-PRIOR PRD STORY** (BE-0470 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/idempotency_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for idempotency_keys). Subspecies of the BE-0468 (audit_events) cross-tenant template with seven load-bearing deltas: (a) there is NO ListByOrganization read surface — Find is the only read, scoped to the full (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) tuple — so the "cross-tenant list returns only own rows" probe collapses to "cross-tenant Find returns (zero, false, nil)" with a per-tenant count assertion on both sides, AND the "limit clamping is tenant-scoped" probe drops entirely (no list cap exists). The file's package-doc header MUST document the absence so a future reader does not look for a missing test, mirroring the BE-0426 organizations file's "tenant-root tables have no ListByParent" doc-only delegation; (b) the byte-identity comparator MUST handle three nullable pointer-bearing columns (*int for response_status, []byte for response_body, *time.Time for completed_at) where the pending-vs-completed lifecycle distinction is observable through nil-ness. A simple field-equality loop will silently round-trip a regression that flipped a pending row's NULL completion columns to zero-value scalars (e.g. response_status=0, response_body=[]byte{}, completed_at=epoch). The pattern: switch on (after==nil, baseline==nil) into three branches — both-nil OK, one-nil diff "nil-ness drifted", both-non-nil dereference-compare — and emit a fmt-printer-pair (fmtIntPtr / fmtTimePtr) so the diagnostic reads "before=<nil> after=200" rather than pointer noise. The pattern is forward-applicable to every future tenant-isolation file whose row carries nullable completion columns (e.g. job_attempts with nullable error_summary + finished_at + result, quota_reservations with nullable released_at + job_id); (c) every mutating surface needs its OWN OnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB pair — Claim, Complete, Release. The Complete probe is structurally distinct from the Claim probe because Complete's WHERE composes `organization_id = $1 AND principal_id = $2 AND idempotency_key = $3 AND status = 'pending'` so the cross-tenant caller falls off the organization_id leg BEFORE the status leg runs, and the typed Conflict ("no longer pending") fires WITHOUT a foreign-row read — a regression that dropped organization_id from the Complete WHERE would either rewrite orgB's row (worst case) OR silently complete orgA's "claim" with a forged response envelope. The Release probe asserts the dual: Release is "safe to call defensively" and returns nil on a zero-row DELETE, so the cross-tenant Release must return nil AND leave orgB's pending row byte-identical AND the per-tenant count unchanged — three separate assertions because a regression that dropped organization_id from the Release WHERE would delete orgB's row, which the bystander byte-identity loader catches as a missing-row error inside loadIdempotencyRowByID's t.Fatalf. The forged Complete envelope MUST carry a recognisable canary string (`"forged":"orgA-tries-to-complete-orgB-claim"`) so a future reader can see immediately what the test was protecting against; (d) the Claim cross-tenant id-collision probe needs a GLOBAL row-count assertion in addition to the per-tenant counts. The PRIMARY KEY on idempotency_keys.id is global, so a rolled-back PK violation under a foreign tenant could in principle land the failing row in some other (third) tenant's slot — the per-tenant probe alone cannot prove the absence because the failing row's organization_id is unknown to the probe. The fix is a tiny totalIdempotencyRows(ctx, t, db) helper that runs `SELECT count(*) FROM idempotency_keys` with no WHERE, snapshotted before and after the failing Claim. The pattern is forward-applicable to every future tenant-isolation file for a table whose PK is global (not parent-scoped); (e) the Claim cross-tenant id-collision probe MUST force the collision through caller-supplied ids on DIFFERENT (principal_id, idempotency_key) tuples so the ON CONFLICT target does NOT match and the PRIMARY KEY violation flows through mapWriteError. A naive collision attempt under the same (principal, key) tuple would be intercepted by ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE and silently rewritten through the expiry-takeover branch — the test would pass on a broken implementation. The id-collision test in this file mints sharedID via mintIdempotencyID and assigns it to a record carrying a DIFFERENT (principal_id, idempotency_key) than the seeded orgA row. The pattern is forward-applicable to every future tenant-isolation file for an UPSERT table whose ON CONFLICT target is a NON-PK unique tuple (idempotency_keys uses (org, principal, key); job_attempts may use (job_id, attempt_number); etc.); (f) the Claim "mirror tuple" probe is the load-bearing per-tenant-uniqueness assertion of the ON CONFLICT target. orgB seeds a row at (orgB, usr_mirror, mirror-key); orgA Claims a row at (orgA, usr_mirror, mirror-key) — same (principal_id, idempotency_key), different organization_id. A regression that dropped organization_id from the ON CONFLICT target would resolve the conflict onto orgB's mirror-tuple row and silently rewrite it through the DO UPDATE branch — the worst-case cross-tenant idempotency data-corruption shape. The assertion: orgA's Claim returns owned=true (fresh INSERT, never DO UPDATE on a foreign row), orgA's new row carries orgA's organization_id and a distinct id, AND orgB's mirror row remains byte-identical (every field including the nullable completion columns and the database-assigned created_at + caller-supplied expires_at). The "mirror tuple" terminology and the explicit comment ("the load-bearing bystander whose presence on orgB exercises the per-tenant uniqueness of the ON CONFLICT target") MUST be carried verbatim in the seed comment because the structural test failure mode (silent peer-row rewrite) is the kind a future reader will rationalise away without it; (g) the cascade-from-organization-delete probe MUST seed orgB rows covering the lifecycle spread — pending, completed-with-envelope, pending-with-future-expiry — because the nullable completion columns are the load-bearing byte-identity surface, not the always-set columns. The single-bystander variant of this cascade is already pinned by the BE-0469 invariants file's TestIdempotencyRepositoryCascadeFromOrganizationDelete (one orgB row, inline column equalities); the tenant-isolation file deepens it with a three-row pool and the assertIdempotencyRowByteIdentical comparator across every row, plus a post-cascade Find for every surviving orgB row to prove the cascade did not silently corrupt the repository read predicate. New helpers introduced: assertIdempotencyRowByteIdentical (nullable-aware comparator), fmtIntPtr / fmtTimePtr / itoaInt (pointer-friendly diagnostic formatters — itoaInt avoids importing strconv solely for the formatter call sites), seedTwoTenantIdempotencyFixture (two-organization shape; idempotency_keys has no project/environment/service parent chain so the fixture is narrower than the dokploy_refs equivalent), totalIdempotencyRows (global PK-collision count probe). Helpers reused: seedOrg (schema_test.go); newIdempotencyStore / idemFixture / claimIdem / completeIdem / findIdem / wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go); rawIdempotencyRow / loadIdempotencyRowByID / countIdempotencyRowsForOrg / mintIdempotencyID (idempotency_repository_invariants_test.go).
