ralph/progress.txt:19851:- Repository for an **APPEND-ONLY EVENT LOG table whose row body has NO updated_at column, NO version column, and is guarded by a BEFORE UPDATE trigger that rejects every UPDATE at the SQL level, AND whose tenant scope is enforced through a composite FK back to a DEEPER parent (deployments, not directly organizations)** (BE-0459 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_event.go + 0035_deployment_events migration + deployment_event_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0455 (quota_reservations — append-only-from-the-customer-perspective but still has updated_at + settled_at lifecycle UPDATE surface from the worker; tenant-scoped directly to organizations) and from AuditRepository (also append-only with BEFORE UPDATE trigger but tenant-scoped DIRECTLY to organizations with NO composite FK to a deeper parent) in three load-bearing ways: (a) the table has only TWO timestamp columns (occurred_at + created_at), both stamped by now() at INSERT; occurred_at is caller-overridable to support a worker reconciling after a restart that persists the original wallclock the event was observed at, while created_at is always database-stamped. The repository's Append uses `COALESCE($N, now())` so a zero time.Time defaults to now() but a non-zero caller-supplied time round-trips verbatim. (b) The composite FK leg (organization_id, deployment_id) references deployments(organization_id, id), not organizations(id) directly — this means a cross-tenant Append surfaces as apierr.Conflict via mapWriteError (FK violation) BEFORE the row gets near the organizations table, and ON DELETE CASCADE chains through deployments rather than directly from organizations. The cascade-delete test must therefore DELETE the parent deployment (not the parent organization) to exercise the chain, and the FK rejection test seeds two orgs but only ONE deployment so the cross-tenant probe references alpha's deployment id with bravo's organization_id — the (bravo.ID, alphaDep.ID) tuple matches no row in deployments and the FK trips. (c) The repository surface is intentionally narrower than QuotaRepository's: Append + GetByID + ListByDeployment, no Update, no Cancel, no aggregation method. GetByID exists despite the table being internal-accounting (audit_events has no GetByID for the same reason) because the timeline endpoint's future read paths benefit from a tenant-scoped point lookup and the cost is a single short method. ListByDeployment orders occurred_at ASC + id ASC (chronological — the timeline endpoint renders the deployment unfolding forward in time, opposite to deployments' reverse-chronological list path).
ralph/progress.txt:19853:The 20-test shape pins: Append-mints-id-and-row-shape (depev_ prefix), Append-blank-fields-default (Message='' RequestID='' CorrelationID='' Metadata=nil), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-respects-caller-supplied-occurred-at (worker-reconciliation contract), Append-unknown-event-type-rejected (CHECK via mapWriteError -> Conflict), Append-blank-event-type-rejected (apierr.Internal at application boundary, NOT a database-CHECK pass-through — the empty string is a programming error caught before SQL, since a blank value would also pass the CHECK if the CHECK accepted '' which it does not but the application boundary catches it first), Append-unknown-deployment-rejected (composite FK -> Conflict), Append-cross-tenant-deployment-rejected (composite FK -> Conflict + countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment proves no row persisted), Append-duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY collision via mapWriteError), Append-nil-Tx-Internal, Append-rolls-back-on-tx-rollback, Append-peer-row-byte-identity (NO updated_at column to drift, so the test asserts byte-identity on id+EventType+Message+OccurredAt+CreatedAt — the absence of bump_version + set_updated_at triggers MEANS the peer can never drift on a successful sibling Append), Update-rejected-by-trigger (raw `db.Exec UPDATE` bypasses the repository surface entirely so the trigger is the only thing that can stop the write), cascade-delete-on-deployment (raw `DELETE FROM deployments WHERE id = $1` cascades through the composite FK ON DELETE CASCADE), GetByID-returns-persisted-row / unknown-NotFound / cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak-on-foreign-deployment-id, ListByDeployment-chronological-order (seed three rows OUT OF chronological order so the ASC ordering must reorder them on read), ListByDeployment-unknown-deployment-empty (returns empty slice not error — distinguishes "no events" from "no parent" is the caller's responsibility, mirrors deployment ListByService), ListByDeployment-cross-tenant-empty. The peer-row test gets simpler than quota_reservations or deployments because there is NO updated_at column to drift and NO version column to bump — append-only is structural in the column list, not just the trigger. Forward-applicable to every future append-only event-log surface (e.g. provisioning_job_events when that lands).
ralph/progress.txt:19855:GOTCHA on apierr.Internal for blank-event-type at the application boundary (NOT mapWriteError -> Conflict): a blank EventType `""` would pass the `event_type IN (...)` CHECK only if `''` were in the closed set, which it is not — but the test must still encode that the application boundary catches it FIRST and surfaces yerr.CodeInternal, not the database CHECK surfacing yerr.CodeConflict. The repository explicitly does `if e.EventType == "" { return apierr.Internal(...) }` before any SQL — same reason AuditRepository.Append explicitly checks `if !e.Decision.Valid()` rather than letting the database CHECK reject it. The constraint surface and the application-boundary surface have DIFFERENT typed errors, and the test must pin both.
ralph/progress.txt:20000:  - BE-0271 (endpoint) and BE-0272 (contract tests) for GET /v1/jobs are still passes=false. Both depend on a jobs HTTP surface landing: a JobLister port over the existing internal/controlplane/store/job.go.JobRepository, a homeOrgResolver-built (or a new optional query-param-aware resolver) for Kind=KindJob, the canonical thin-handler -> service -> repository chain, and contract tests structurally like organizations_test.go for the org-level list path. When BE-0271 lands, the wire-level twin of this file should live at internal/controlplane/httpapi/jobs_list_policy_test.go and re-test the same engine verdicts through a real handler stack (resolver scope construction + 404-vs-403 ordering, NOT capability-matrix outcomes which the engine pin in this file owns). The engine pin will catch any catalog or builtinRoleCaps drift before the wire-level file does.
