ralph/progress.txt:32165:  - The environments table is the FIRST table encountered in the priority order whose mutation surface is Insert + Update + ScheduleDeletion ONLY — no UpdateDisplayName, no Restore. The invariants test file must therefore drop those two test functions from the project_repository_invariants_test.go template (do NOT add empty stubs that would refer to non-existent EnvironmentRepository.UpdateDisplayName / Restore — they would fail to compile). The Update test gains a Kind-preservation assertion because Kind is part of the persisted environments shape (the SET clause is `SET slug = $3, display_name = $4`, so kind is structurally NOT touched), unlike projects where Update's SET surface is the same two columns but there is no kind column on the projects table to assert preserved.
ralph/progress.txt:32166:  - Insert-nil-Tx guard goes in the invariants file (not the *_test.go siblings) when the existing test files cover only Update and ScheduleDeletion nil-Tx — the matrix-closer convention matches project_repository_invariants_test.go's TestProjectRepositoryUpdateDisplayNameWithoutTxIsTypedInternal (different mutation method, same role: file closes the missing-path in the nil-Tx matrix).
ralph/progress.txt:32168:  - environmentFixture / insertEnvironment helpers are NEW file-scope helpers in this file (sibling files only have seedEnvironment which goes via raw SQL; no projectFixture-equivalent for environments existed). These follow projectFixture / insertProject byte-for-byte except: (a) environmentFixture defaults Kind to store.EnvironmentKindStandard so the migration 0025 CHECK constraint is satisfied at the EnvironmentRepository.Insert path (the raw-SQL seedEnvironment in schema_test.go relies on the database column default — but the repository layer always supplies kind explicitly); (b) insertEnvironment signature uses *store.EnvironmentRepository instead of *store.ProjectRepository.
ralph/progress.txt:32169:  - environmentTxRollbackSentinel: yet another rollback-sentinel type. The cross-file name collision list (BE-0436 pattern) now includes: apiKeyTxRollbackSentinel, apiKeyScopeTxRollbackSentinel, membershipTxRollbackSentinel, serviceAccountTxRollbackSentinel, projectTxRollbackSentinel, environmentTxRollbackSentinel, and errSentinel — every *_repository_invariants_test.go in store_test contributes its own table-named sentinel. Future siblings (service_repository_invariants_test.go, variable_repository_invariants_test.go, ...) must add a new uniquely-named sentinel type — re-using a sibling's name is a compile-time collision in the shared store_test package.
ralph/progress.txt:32173:- What was implemented: byte-identical-bystander tenant-isolation tests for the environments table at the repository layer (BE-0442). Ten new database-backed tests in a new file prove that every EnvironmentRepository method (GetByID / ListByProject / Update / ScheduleDeletion) carries organization_id as the FIRST SQL predicate ahead of any other identifier, that a cross-tenant probe is indistinguishable from an unknown id on Code AND Hint (existence-leak projection), and that a soft-deleted environment in one tenant never leaks into ANY read surface of another tenant. The bystander row remains byte-identical across every observable column — id, organization_id, project_id, slug, display_name, kind, version, created_at, updated_at, deletion_scheduled_at — with the BEFORE-UPDATE environments_set_updated_at trigger and the environments_bump_version trigger as the independent anchors that catch a momentary cross-tenant UPDATE match even when the column writes themselves happen to look correct.
ralph/progress.txt:32176:- Results: BE-0442 acceptance criteria satisfied — (a) two organizations with overlapping-looking environment fixtures (shared "production" slug per tenant project), (b) principal-from-org-A cannot read/update/schedule-delete/list/infer org-B environments, (c) list queries return stable per-(org, project) counts without leaking other-tenant totals, (d) direct ID access AND parent-scoped access AND soft-deleted rows are all covered, (e) errors flow through the typed apierr surface — no JSON envelopes are constructed here (repository layer; envelope rendering lives at httpapi).
ralph/progress.txt:32181:  - When seeding environments through the repository's typed Insert, the parent project must also be inserted through the typed ProjectRepository.Insert (NOT the raw-SQL seedProject helper) so the database writes the (organization_id, id) composite UNIQUE index entry the environments FK needs. The existing insertProject helper from project_test.go composes correctly with insertEnvironment from environment_repository_invariants_test.go (BE-0441).
