ralph/progress.txt:32319:  - Codebase Patterns: NEW top-level entry — "Repository CRUD + invariants test file for a tenant-scoped leaf-grant table (a grant table with NO further-nested scope pointer below the parent FK)" (BE-0447 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). Structural shape: BE-0443 environment_grant_repository_invariants_test.go template MINUS the nullable-pointer-roundtrip leg MINUS the COALESCE-NULLS-FIRST ordering assertion MINUS the empty-string-dedup-equivalence leg. The Upsert signature drops the trailing *string argument; the ON CONFLICT branch keys on the two-tuple (organization_id, parent_id, principal_id) with no COALESCE; the ORDER BY is (principal_id ASC, id ASC). Forward-applicable to any future grant table that bottoms out at a leaf entity (a hypothetical KindDeploymentGrant or KindJobGrant would inherit this template if their parent is structurally leaf-like).
ralph/progress.txt:32322:- What was implemented: BE-0448 — internal/controlplane/store/service_grant_tenant_isolation_test.go (new, 8 t.Parallel integration tests + 2 file-scope helpers) proving service_grants rows cannot cross tenant boundaries through ANY of the repository's three surfaces (ListByService / Upsert / DeleteByServiceExceptIDs). Direct application of the BE-0444 environment_grant_tenant_isolation_test.go template with the BE-0447 structural simplification: service_grants is the LEAF grant table in the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy, so the row body carries NO further-nested scope pointer (no ServiceID *string — the service IS the scope), the UNIQUE conflict target is the clean three-tuple `(organization_id, service_id, principal_id)` with NO COALESCE leg, and the bystander byte-identity check uses direct string equality on ServiceID (the package-level stringPtrEqual/fmtStringPtr helpers from project_grant_tenant_isolation_test.go are not needed and are explicitly NOT imported into this file).
ralph/progress.txt:32323:- Tests prove: (a) ListByService is keyed strictly by BOTH organization_id AND service_id — two tenants each owning a service named "api" each get exactly their own row, never a swapped or merged response; the assertion fans out across all five anchors (id, organization_id, service_id, principal_id, role) so any single overlap surfaces; (b) ListByService against a cross-tenant service_id returns a non-nil empty slice — indistinguishable from ListByService against an unknown service_id under the caller's own organization, so the cross-tenant probe is not a "does this service exist?" oracle; (c) ListByService with a shared principal_id (`usr_shared`) across tenants returns each tenant's own row exactly once with non-overlapping grant ids — proves the WHERE filter is keyed on organization_id, not on principal_id alone; (d) Upsert INSERT on orgA does not touch orgB's bystander grant whose `(service_id_in_its_own_tenant, principal_id)` pair is IDENTICAL to orgA's — proves the conflict target carries organization_id, so the per-tenant uniqueness rules out cross-tenant ON CONFLICT matching; the byte-identical assertion includes Version (bumped by service_grants_bump_version on every matched UPDATE — drift surfaces a missing tenant predicate even when column writes look correct) AND UpdatedAt (refreshed by the BEFORE-UPDATE service_grants_set_updated_at trigger on every matched UPDATE); (e) Upsert UPDATE on orgA (re-upsert with a different role, fresh caller-id IGNORED, ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE fires on orgA only) does not touch orgB's bystander row that legitimately shares the same `(service_id_in_its_own_tenant, principal_id)` — and orgA's row MUST have actually changed (role mutated, version bumped from N to N+1) so a silent no-op repository cannot pass the bystander check; (f) DeleteByServiceExceptIDs with nil keepIDs (the unconditional-DELETE codepath) on orgA clears every grant of orgA's service and leaves orgB's bystander grant byte-identical to its baseline; (g) DeleteByServiceExceptIDs with non-empty keepIDs (the `id NOT IN ANY` codepath) on orgA keeps exactly keepA and deletes dropA, while orgB's bystander grant — whose id is deliberately NOT in keepIDs — survives byte-identical (this is the load-bearing leg: a regression that dropped the tenant predicate would let the "NOT IN" filter eat orgB's row); (h) Upsert with the SAME `(principal_id, role)` on TWO tenants' OWN services BOTH succeed and produce two distinct rows with distinct ids — pins the per-tenant UNIQUE-index property and confirms the WHERE-on-organization_id-leg is the only thing keeping the rows apart (the flip side of every bystander test above).
ralph/progress.txt:32337:- Remaining risks / follow-ups: none for BE-0448. The next priority-1 story is BE-0449 (Repository: variables CRUD and invariants) which will land the first CRUD+invariants file for the variables table — a richer surface than grants (per-row is_secret flag, secret-bearing value column, scope-pointer surface possibly spanning org/project/environment depending on the row, soft-delete column where applicable), so the template diverges from the grant family and follows whichever variable repository / migration already lives in the codebase.
