ralph/progress.txt:32626:  - **Repository-level invariants for an EXISTING table whose CRUD surface is already partially covered (BE-0451 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_repository_invariants_test.go)**: quota_policies is NOT a "first story for this table" — migration 0006 already exists, UpsertOrganizationPolicy / EffectiveLimit / ListEffectiveLimits / ListOrganizationUsage already exist in store/quota.go, and internal/controlplane/store/quota_repository_test.go already owns EffectiveLimit's read semantics + reservation flows. The story's "Repository: <table> CRUD and invariants" line therefore reduces to "fill the invariants gap": pin what the existing test file does NOT cover (row-shape of an Upsert INSERT vs UPDATE, byte-equal preservation of id/created_at across UPDATE, sibling-row non-interference for plan defaults, FK/CHECK/DOMAIN -> Conflict mapping, every enum constant round-trips, ListEffectiveLimits tenant + plan isolation, Store.Write closure rollback, nil-tx -> Internal). Before writing anything, grep for `func Test<RepositoryName><MethodName>` to enumerate what is already pinned, and write tests with NEW names that only address the gap. Reuse the test names verbatim only when intentionally re-shaping coverage; never write a same-named test in another file (vet's "redeclared in this block" fires at package store_test).
ralph/progress.txt:32629:  - **Direct DB SELECT for row-shape probes** is the right tool when the repository's public reader projections (EffectiveLimit, ListEffectiveLimits) deliberately omit id/created_at/updated_at/scope_kind/plan: an UPDATE-branch identity-preservation test needs to observe those columns, so the test issues `SELECT id, scope_kind, plan, organization_id, resource, limit_value, enforcement_mode, created_at, updated_at FROM quota_policies WHERE scope_kind='organization' AND organization_id=$1 AND resource=$2`. Wrap the load in a typed rawQuotaPolicyRow struct + a `load<Scope>PolicyRow` helper so the call sites read like the persisted-row assertions the variables invariants tests use, without the helper leaking out of the test file.
ralph/progress.txt:32634:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go — repository-layer tenant-isolation tests for the quota_policies table. quota_policies is a dual-scope table (plan_default OR organization) whose ONLY mutation surface is UpsertOrganizationPolicy and whose row body is only observable through the typed resolvers (EffectiveLimit / ListEffectiveLimits / ListOrganizationUsage). No per-id Get / Update / Delete surface exists, so the cross-tenant guarantee at this layer is exhaustively the projection invariant on every resolver leg AND the byte-identical-bystander invariant on the upsert. Coverage (9 tests): (1) EffectiveLimit per-resource is tenant-scoped — orgB's override does not appear when orgA queries the same resource; (2) ListEffectiveLimits rendered count is local to the queried tenant (orgA=1, orgB=3) — no count cross; (3) shared-resource isolation (both tenants override `projects` with distinct limit/mode — each sees its OWN); (4) plan isolation (orgA on 'starter' must not see 'pro' plan defaults); (5) ListOrganizationUsage is tenant scoped at BOTH the policies CTE and the usage CTE; (6) shared-counter isolation (both tenants have a quota_usage counter for the same resource with distinct used_value); (7) Upsert INSERT branch on orgA leaves orgB's bystander row byte-identical (the SAME resource on both tenants — the ON CONFLICT predicate is exercised); (8) Upsert UPDATE branch on orgA leaves orgB's bystander row byte-identical (both tenants pre-seeded with an override on the SAME resource — the maximally adversarial fixture for the partial-unique-index ON CONFLICT target); (9) same-resource upserts in two tenants both succeed (positive-direction pair for the bystander proofs — proves the partial unique index is per-tenant, not global).
ralph/progress.txt:32652:  - **Reuse the invariants file's helpers across files in the same package**. BE-0451 already defined `upsertOrganizationPolicy`, `loadOrganizationPolicyRow`, `effectiveLimitOrFail`, `listEffectiveLimitsOrFail`, and the `rawQuotaPolicyRow` struct in quota_policy_repository_invariants_test.go. All *_test.go files under internal/controlplane/store/ share the same store_test package, so BE-0452's tenant-isolation file inherits these symbols by package membership. The new file defines ONLY the symbols it introduces: `listOrganizationUsageOrFail`, `assertQuotaPolicyByteIdentical`, and per-file `stringPtrEqualQP` / `fmtStringPtrQP` nullable-pointer helpers with the `QP` suffix to avoid collisions with same-named helpers in sibling tenant-isolation files (BE-0440's project-grant file already defines a `stringPtrEqual` variant — naming conflict would block compilation).
ralph/progress.txt:32653:  - **Shared-conflict-tuple fixture for the Upsert UPDATE bystander test**. The maximally adversarial fixture for proving the ON CONFLICT branch of an `INSERT...ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind='organization' DO UPDATE` is BOTH tenants pre-seeded with an override on the SAME resource — so the ON CONFLICT target tuple matches for both rows when the WHERE predicate is stripped, and the planner's row choice (which row the UPDATE lands on without the partial index's organization_id leg) becomes observable through updated_at drift on the bystander. The fixture must be CONSTRUCTED through the same `upsertOrganizationPolicy` helper the test exercises, not through `seedOrgPolicy` raw SQL — using the repository to build the fixture proves the repository can land the second (peer) row at all without colliding with the first, AND aligns the baseline.updated_at timestamps so a wrong-row UPDATE shows up as a sub-second drift. The corollary "same-resource-in-two-tenants-both-succeed" test is the positive-direction pair: without it, a regression that resolved the conflict by (resource,) alone would have prevented the bystander INSERT from landing in the first place — and every byte-identical assertion in the UPDATE-branch test would silently pass against a non-existent row.
ralph/progress.txt:32714:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go — repository-layer CRUD-and-lifecycle invariants for the quota_usage table. quota_usage is the per-tenant counter table; its ONLY public write surface in QuotaRepository is LockUsage, which INSERTs the counter row ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING and then SELECTs FOR UPDATE. There is no per-row Update/Delete method, no version column, and no service_id pointer — the increment path is owned by quota.Checker and the delete-cascade is owned by schema CASCADE. The 9-test file pins what neither quota_repository_test.go (the original LockUsage happy-path test) nor the schema/tenant-isolation siblings already cover: (1) row-shape on the INSERT leg — qusg_ prefix, used_value defaults to 0, created_at/updated_at within 1s; (2) second-call byte-identity — id, created_at, updated_at, AND used_value all anchored across ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING (the set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only; a non-UPDATE must not stamp updated_at — proved with a measurable wallclock gap between calls); (3) peer-row byte-identity — LockUsage(org, services) does not touch the (org, projects) counter row's id/created_at/updated_at/used_value, and the two rows have distinct ids; (4) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING discards the minted qusg_ id when the row exists — proved by pre-seeding a row at used_value=17 with id `qu_seed_projects` and observing both the live used_value and the original id survive a LockUsage call; (5) FK violation (unknown organization) -> typed Conflict via mapWriteError; (6) DOMAIN violation by casting `"not-a-resource"` to store.QuotaResource -> typed Conflict; (7) transaction rollback — closure returns sentinel after a successful LockUsage, Write rolls back, no quota_usage row persists (counter probe = 0); (8) nil-tx -> yerr.CodeInternal. The 9th test is the pre-existing TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageCreatesAndReturnsCounter that the file does NOT duplicate — it lives in quota_repository_test.go and remains the load-bearing two-call-idempotency probe.
ralph/progress.txt:32716:  - internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go (NEW, ~370 lines)
ralph/progress.txt:32732:  - **Repository-layer CRUD-and-lifecycle invariants test file for a TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an external code path (quota.Checker), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — single-table INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO, because ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire — quota_policies's UPDATE leg actively REFRESHES updated_at via the trigger, so its byte-identity proof EXCLUDES updated_at; quota_usage's byte-identity proof INCLUDES updated_at as a load-bearing anchor (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof for quota_usage is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (the UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) constraint isolates rows by resource within a tenant), distinct from quota_policies's cross-scope peer (plan_default vs organization within the SAME quota_policies table); (c) the "row already exists with non-zero value" leg has no quota_policies analog — for quota_usage we pre-seed a row at used_value=17 via direct SQL (seedQuotaUsage from usage_test.go) and prove BOTH the seeded id `qu_seed_projects` AND the seeded used_value survive the LockUsage call, which is the only way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted qusg_ id does not replace the row's id (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT (...) DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would replace the row's id and reset used_value to 0 — a catastrophic data-loss path the typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to store.QuotaResource — `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")` — to bypass Go's compile-time check on the typed string and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer; this is the same casting pattern quota_policies uses for unknown enforcement_mode (`store.EnforcementMode("not-a-mode")`).
ralph/progress.txt:32733:  - Test-helper symbols added in this file (all *_test.go files under internal/controlplane/store/ share the same store_test package, so duplicate top-level symbols collide at compile time): `lockUsageOrFail`, `loadQuotaUsageRow`, `countQuotaUsageRowsForOrg`, `rawQuotaUsageRow`, `quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel`. Reused symbols from sibling files: `seedOrg` (schema_test.go), `seedQuotaUsage` (usage_test.go), `newStore` (store_test.go). The seedQuotaUsage helper is the ONLY way to set used_value to a non-zero value through a test fixture — the repository surface has no equivalent because LockUsage cannot increment.
ralph/progress.txt:32762:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_tenant_isolation_test.go — repository-layer tenant-isolation tests for the quota_usage table. quota_usage's only public mutation surface is LockUsage (INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT ... FOR UPDATE) and there is no per-row Update/Delete/Get; the typed read resolver ListOrganizationUsage's cross-tenant projection is already covered by quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go (BE-0452), so this file is additive and focused on LockUsage cross-tenant invariants + the cascade-delete tenant-isolation that TestQuotaCascadeDeleteOnOrganization (quota_schema_test.go) only proves on the deleted side. Coverage (6 tests):
ralph/progress.txt:32767:  (5) TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageSameResourceInTwoTenantsBothSucceed — load-bearing positive-direction pair: both LockUsage(orgA, projects) and LockUsage(orgB, projects) succeed via the repository surface (NOT a raw INSERT), two rows persist with distinct ids and distinct organization_id, each tenant has exactly one row on projects. Without this anchor, a regression that resolved the ON CONFLICT by (resource,) alone would have prevented orgB's seed in every other test and the byte-identical assertions would silently pass against rows that never existed.
ralph/progress.txt:32770:- Reused helpers (sibling files, store_test package): seedOrg (schema_test.go), seedQuotaUsage (usage_test.go), newStore (store_test.go), lockUsageOrFail / loadQuotaUsageRow / countQuotaUsageRowsForOrg / rawQuotaUsageRow (quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go, BE-0453).
ralph/progress.txt:32787:  - **Repository tenant-isolation tests for a TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY public mutation is INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT ... FOR UPDATE (LockUsage), where the row body's used_value is increment-managed BY AN EXTERNAL CODE PATH (quota.Checker, not the repository), with NO per-id Get/Update/Delete surface, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and the cascade-delete tenant isolation NOT covered by the existing single-tenant cascade test** (BE-0454 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0452 (quota_policies, dual-scope UPSERT) in four load-bearing ways: (a) NO typed-resolver coverage is added here — ListOrganizationUsage's two-CTE-leg tenant scoping is already proved in quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go (BE-0452) AND in usage_test.go (TestQuotaRepositoryListOrganizationUsageTenantScoped), and re-asserting would cause same-named tests to collide at compile time across the shared store_test package; the cross-tenant id-leak surface to defend is LockUsage's INSERT/SELECT primitive, which the typed projection cannot expose (the projection omits id by construction) — so the bystander proofs use raw-SQL row loads instead; (b) the bystander invariant has TWO peer classes — same-resource and cross-resource — both anchored on the same baseline tenant in ONE test (test 4), because both classes are governed by the SAME ON CONFLICT predicate (organization_id, resource) and a regression that dropped either column would surface in both; this is structurally simpler than BE-0452's split insert/update bystander tests (quota_policies has TWO upsert branches — DO NOTHING never happens, only DO UPDATE — so insert-on-orgA-doesn't-touch-orgB and update-on-orgA-doesn't-touch-orgB are distinct probes; quota_usage's ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING means orgA's INSERT against orgB's existing row is the ONLY upsert branch worth proving and the test surface collapses to one); (c) a NEW probe is added on top of BE-0452's template: a "per-tenant row-count invariance" test (test 3) that does NOT load any row — only counts rows owned by orgA and orgB before and after — to catch a cross-tenant FK swap that the byte-identity probes would miss (the byte-identity probes only observe rows that still exist; a regression that DELETED orgB's row and INSERTED an orgA-owned replacement at the same id would pass byte-identity if id and used_value coincidentally matched, but would fail this count probe); (d) the cascade-delete tenant-isolation test (test 6) is a NEW class of probe quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go did NOT carry because TestQuotaCascadeDeleteOnOrganization (quota_schema_test.go) proves single-tenant cascade only — it never checks that a different tenant's rows survive. quota_usage is the right place to add this probe because the cascade fires at the schema level (FK CASCADE on quota_usage.organization_id) and is per-table; quota_policies's cascade is asymmetric (plan_default rows have NULL organization_id and survive deletes by construction, so a "different tenant's rows survive" probe there would conflate two distinct invariants — the plan-default-survives invariant from quota_schema_test.go AND the organization-bystander-survives invariant — and the asymmetry would obscure the test's purpose). For quota_usage there is no plan_default scope, so the cascade-isolation probe is unambiguous: orgB's rows survive byte-identically (id, used_value, timestamps) when orgA is deleted.
ralph/progress.txt:32789:  - **The "positive-direction pair" anchor (test 5)** is the load-bearing complement of every bystander byte-identity proof in tenant-isolation tests, but for quota_usage it has a different mechanical purpose than for quota_policies: in BE-0452 the positive-direction pair (`SameResourceInTwoTenantsBothSucceed`) anchors the bystander proofs because the partial unique index `quota_policies_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind='organization'` is partial AND keyed by both columns — so a regression to a global UNIQUE would have made BOTH the insert-bystander and the update-bystander silently pass against missing rows; in BE-0454 the equivalent positive-direction pair anchors the bystander proofs because `quota_usage_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource)` is full (not partial — quota_usage has no scope_kind column) but is per-tenant, so the same anchor mechanic applies even though the partial-index angle drops out. Going through the repository surface on BOTH tenants — not via raw INSERTs — is the load-bearing detail: a regression that hard-coded a specific organization_id inside the LockUsage SQL itself would surface here too.
ralph/progress.txt:32793:- Implemented repository-layer CRUD-and-lifecycle invariants test file `internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_repository_invariants_test.go` covering 17 tests for the QuotaRepository reservation surface (InsertReservation + SumActiveReservations).
