ralph/progress.txt:34003:- Implemented persistence access patterns for the drift_findings table. Because drift_findings had no prior migration, repository, or fixtures on disk, this story landed the full vertical: migration 0037_drift_findings.{up,down}.sql, internal/controlplane/store/drift_finding.go (the typed repository + DriftKind/DriftReason/DriftLevel taxonomies mirroring internal/controlplane/reconcile/types.go), and internal/controlplane/store/drift_finding_repository_invariants_test.go (the integration test suite). Files added:
ralph/progress.txt:34007:  - internal/controlplane/store/drift_finding_repository_invariants_test.go
ralph/progress.txt:34008:- The repository surface is intentionally narrow: Append (worker writes a finding inside a tx), GetByID (operator reads one finding tenant-scoped), ListByOrganization (operator triage queue, newest first, capped at 200), and MarkResolved (the one mutation — a finding's only legal state-transition is to be resolved). There is no per-row Delete; row removal is reachable only through ON DELETE CASCADE.
ralph/progress.txt:34010:- Optional resource legs (project_id / environment_id / service_id / service_domain_id) use MATCH SIMPLE composite FKs referencing the parents' UNIQUE (organization_id, id). An organization-level finding with all legs NULL is legal; a scoped finding is pinned to its tenant. The repository serialises blank Go strings as SQL NULL via nullIfEmpty(s string) any so a cross-tenant or empty-string leg never engages the composite FK with an unmatched row.
ralph/progress.txt:34015:  - The reconcile worker that produces drift_findings rows does not yet exist (it lands in BE-0507+ "Periodic reconcile sweeper"). The repository was sized for that future writer; the Append signature takes a fully-populated DriftFinding rather than narrower constructors per planner action, matching the JobAttempt / DeploymentEvent pattern.
ralph/progress.txt:34018:  - The picker rule "highest-priority pending story that CAN be implemented end-to-end" (BE-0175 vs BE-0177 precedent) reads as PERMISSIVE for a P=1 repository story whose backing table is not yet on disk — the migration is itself "part of THIS story" because the acceptance criterion is "implement persistence access patterns for X", which presupposes the table. Compare to a P=1 policy-matrix story whose endpoint surface is missing — there the picker defers to the P=2 endpoint sibling first. The distinction: a repository story OWNS its migration; a policy-matrix story does NOT own its endpoint.
ralph/progress.txt:34019:  - When the optional resource leg of a polymorphic finding table is NULL, the Go struct projects to "" via the *string scan + zero-value coalesce in scanDriftFinding. Callers that need to disambiguate "absent" from "empty string" must observe the database row directly (rawDriftFindingRow uses *string). The repository's public DriftFinding struct deliberately collapses NULL onto "" because a finding with an empty-string project_id is structurally identical to a finding with NULL project_id — both are "no project leg". This mirrors the JobAttempt and DeploymentEvent shape where optional id columns are not modelled as pointers either.
ralph/progress.txt:34020:  - Test name sanitisation (sanitizeTestNameForDriftID) is required for any test-local mint helper that derives an id from t.Name() when the test has subtests: t.Name() returns "TestX/subtest" and the unescaped '/' produces ids like "drft_TestX/sub_..." which confuse grep/awk-style id parsing. Pattern is forward-applicable to every future *_repository_invariants_test.go that mints caller-supplied ids inside table-driven subtests.
ralph/progress.txt:34021:  - The "pre-existing flaky failures" surface (apikeyservice / environmentservice / project_variable_service) reproduces on a clean tree before any new code is added. The right cadence is a focused `go test -race -run TestDriftFinding ./internal/controlplane/store/...` for the story's own coverage + a smoke `go test -count=1 -run TestMigrations|TestSchemaMigration|TestSQLInjection` to prove the new migration applies cleanly + sql_injection_static_test.go still passes against the new repository SQL. Do NOT attempt to "fix in passing" the pre-existing failures — they are unrelated and out of scope.
ralph/progress.txt:34025:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants for a TABLE WHOSE MIGRATION + REPOSITORY DO NOT EXIST YET, WITH A NARROW APPEND + READ + ONE-MUTATION (RESOLVE) LIFECYCLE AND OPTIONAL POLYMORPHIC NULLABLE RESOURCE LEGS PINNED BY MATCH-SIMPLE COMPOSITE FKs (BE-0471 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/drift_finding.go + drift_finding_repository_invariants_test.go + migrate/migrations/0037_drift_findings.{up,down}.sql). Subspecies of the BE-0463 (job_attempts) new-table template with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the lifecycle has TWO writes (Append + MarkResolved) rather than one (Append) and NO append-only trigger — the resolved_at + resolved_by_actor_id transition is a legal UPDATE, paired by a drift_findings_resolved_consistent CHECK that ties the two columns nil-state-together. The migration uses set_updated_at trigger (not bump_version + version column) because there is no measurable optimistic-concurrency contention on operator-driven resolution; the file header documents the version-column-omission as load-bearing so a future bulk-resolve story knows it must add a new migration + version column rather than retrofit one. (b) The optional resource legs are NULLABLE composite FKs on (organization_id, project_id|environment_id|service_id|service_domain_id) referencing the parents' UNIQUE (organization_id, id) — MATCH SIMPLE so the FK fires only when the column is set. The repository serialises a blank Go string to SQL NULL via a private nullIfEmpty(s string) any helper (so a `f.ProjectID = ""` Append lands as project_id IS NULL on disk, and the composite FK does not engage with a non-existent empty-string id). scanDriftFinding reads *string into scratch and projects nil onto "" so the public DriftFinding struct collapses NULL onto the zero value (matching JobAttempt / DeploymentEvent — the customer never observes the distinction). Forward-applicable to any future polymorphic-resource-leg table (a future cost_attributions_findings or capacity_breaches table). (c) MarkResolved's UPDATE filters on `resolved_at IS NULL` so an already-resolved row updates zero rows; the method then distinguishes "missing" (typed apierr.NotFound, caller-supplied id only) from "already resolved" (typed apierr.Conflict) via a tenant-scoped follow-up Get inside the SAME *Tx. The Get-on-zero-rows shape is the right pattern for ANY future state-transition repository method whose pre-state is a column predicate (vs a row-existence predicate). A regression that removed the `IS NULL` predicate would silently allow a second resolver to overwrite the first's stamp; TestDriftFindingMarkResolvedAlreadyResolvedIsConflict pins this by asserting BOTH the typed Conflict AND the raw-SQL re-read confirms the original resolved_by_actor_id did not move. (d) Closed taxonomies (DriftKind / DriftReason / DriftLevel) mirror reconcile.DriftKind / DriftReason / ResourceLevel verbatim; the repository's Valid()/String() shape is identical to JobAttemptStatus / DeploymentSource. The CHECK constraints are unreachable from the typed Append API (Valid() pre-rejects), but they remain authoritative for any future raw-SQL writer; the test file header documents this delegation. The application-layer validation matrix (TestDriftFindingAppendValidationRejectsBadInput) exercises every taxonomy column's empty-and-unknown legs as typed apierr.InvalidInput, plus zero-DetectedAt, plus the not-yet-resolved guards (ResolvedAt-set-on-new-finding + ResolvedByActor-set-on-new-finding) which are unique to a table with a state-transition. (e) Test name sanitisation: sanitizeTestNameForDriftID strips '/' from t.Name() so an id minted inside a subtest stays a single underscore-delimited token. Forward-applicable to any future mint helper that derives a caller-supplied id from t.Name() in a table-driven test with subtests. The 19-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (drft_ prefix + server-stamped created_at within [before, after] + DetectedAt round-trip + ResolvedAt/ActorID stay zero), Append-organization-level-legs-are-NULL (the polymorphic-FK shape probe), Append-caller-supplied-id-preserved, Append-duplicate-id-Conflict (PK), Append-validation-matrix (10 sub-cases covering missing + unknown taxonomy + zero timestamps + premature resolution), Append-nil-Tx-Internal, Append-unknown-organization-Conflict (FK), Append-cross-tenant-parent-Conflict (3 sub-cases: foreign project / environment / service via composite-FK), Append-rollback-removes-row, Append-peer-row-byte-identical, GetByID-round-trip, GetByID-unknown-NotFound, GetByID-cross-tenant-NotFound, List-orders-newest-first (detected_at DESC + id DESC tiebreaker), List-clamps-limit (4 sub-cases), List-unknown-org-empty-slice-not-nil, MarkResolved-happy-path (resolved_at + actor + UpdatedAt-after-CreatedAt + raw-SQL byte-equal), MarkResolved-already-resolved-Conflict + no-overwrite (the load-bearing state-transition pin), MarkResolved-unknown-NotFound, MarkResolved-cross-tenant-NotFound + bystander-byte-identity, MarkResolved-validation-matrix (4 sub-cases), MarkResolved-nil-Tx-Internal, MarkResolved-peer-row-byte-identical, Cascade-delete-from-organization. New helpers introduced (per-file DriftFinding-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawDriftFindingRow (with *string + *time.Time fields for the nullable legs + resolved_at), loadDriftFindingRowByID, countDriftFindingRowsForOrg, mintDriftFindingID + sanitizeTestNameForDriftID, driftFindingTxRollbackSentinel, driftFindingFixture, seedDriftFinding, assertDriftFindingByteIdentical, assertStringPtrEqual, assertTimePtrEqual. Reused verbatim from sibling files: seedOrg, seedProject, seedEnvironment, seedService (schema_test.go); newStore (store_test.go); wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go). NEW migration added: 0037_drift_findings.up.sql + .down.sql, both with the standard DROP TRIGGER / DROP INDEX / DROP TABLE shape; the up.sql includes a service_domains composite FK so service_domains must remain a composite-FK target — a future migration that dropped service_domains' UNIQUE (organization_id, id) would break this FK and surface as a TestMigrations failure. The byte-identical-bystander tenant-isolation gap-fill is the paired BE-0472 story.
